      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The New Vicereine of Inclia.—The Countess of Mint° is sure to be very popular in India, where her sporting proclivities, to say nothing of her kindness and geniality will be much appreciated by Anglo Indian Society. Lord Curzon has always imported into his high °trice a little more of the legal hearing than have several of his predecessors, but in spite of that, Lady Curzon has been very popular at Simla and Calcutta,</p>
        <p>. . and it will be no easy role to keep up the traditions of her reign as Vicereine. Lady Minto however, won golden opinions in Canada, and her love of travel and adventure, and of outdoor sports generally, will find plenty of scope in India, Lady Minto is a daughter of the Hon. Char/es Grey, who was private secretary to Prince Consort, and Queen Victoria. She has two grown-up daughters,the Ladies Eileen and Ruby Elliott, who came out last year, and a younger girt at school. She has also two sons, the elder Lord Melgund, being only thirteen. The Queen's Statue.—There are many painted por- traits of Queen Alexandra, but no statue, and the one upon which Mr. George Wade is engaged is the first for which her Majesty has sat. Its site when finished, has not yet been decided upon, but it is certain that the figure will be lifelike in its representation of our Queen, for Mr. Wade is a past master in the art of sculpture. Be has executed, more than one statue of Queen Victotia, as we 1 as otheis, of King Edward, and the Prince of Wales. Of statues of Queen Victoria there are, of course, many, not only in Britain but in all parts of the Empire. Some years ago Princess Louise, Duchess of Argyll, who is an artist of no small merit in sculpture, wanted to execute a portrait bust of Queen Alexandra, but it was not carried out, and it is singular that up to the present time there should be no finished statue of her Majesty. Statues of Women.—Though statues abound of great men of the world, there are very few, excepting Royal i personages, of women who have played leading parts n history. It is said that with the exception referred to, there are only four statnes of women in England, but that is probably under the mark. Still it would be difficult to name bolt.a-dozen instances, in all Britain,in which women have had statues erected to their honour. There is only one in London—that of Mrs. Siddons, at Paddington 'Green. Another is that of Sister Dora. at Walsall. There are however' the better known monu- mental]] Scotland—those of Flora Macdonald,on Castle Hill, Inverness, and Burns beloved "Highland Mary," at Dunoon. Women, except as models, do not figure very largely in the statuary of the world, though there are scores if not _ hundreds of statues erected to.men</p>
        <p>who have never done half as much for mankind, either by act of heroism, or example to others. a:. did Grace Darling. Again, if one of the main objects of the erection of statues, is the encouragement of emulation, there should surely be prominent in our midst, images of such women as Florence Nightingaleand the Baroness Burdett-Coutts. Princess Louise' Duchess of Argyll.—The Duke and Duchess of Argyll are now settled at Roseneath, their place on tha,shores of Garelocb, a fact which affords summer visitors and residents alike, the greatest satis- faction. Her Royal Highness goes about quite unattended, and though exquisitely dressed always, she invariably affects the most simple and quiet styles. The poor adore the Princess who is ever anxious to help them to improve their condition. Of the late Queen's daughters, Princess Henry of Battenberg is the only one who prefers that her ;fink as a Princess of Great Britain should always be observed.—Princess Christian is much less strict with regard to such matters, while Princess Louise Duchess of Argyll, is ever ready to dispense with Royal ceremony, wheneve: it is possible to do so. A Typical Scottish Chieftain.—Unlike many of the Scotch nobles, the Duke of Atholliis seldom seen in Ldndon, and lives nearly all the year at one or other of his seats in Perthshire. Be is h typical Highland chief- tain, intensely Scotch, arid more proud of being the head of his clan than he is of his Dukedom. His prin- cipal place is Bloir Castle, where the Duke lives like a feudal lord, vad still retains all the old Highland manners and customs. He and his son, Lord Tulli- bardine, last week took an active interest in the bazaar at Blois Atholl in aid of erecting a drill hall for the Scottish Horse, a regiment which Lord Tullibardine raised himself. Lord Lovat—another Scottish chieftain —who also gave his assistance to the bazaar, is ODO of -the very few Scotch peers who are Roman Catholics. About Bazaars.—Once upon a time the contributions to a bazaar were generally of the useless persuasion, and when laying asiie a sum of money to spend at such a function, we were fully aware that we should bring away little in return for it. But we have changed all that, and nowadays we have the double satisfaction of helping in a good cause, and at the same time becoming possessed of really ornamental or useful articles. On the " dress " stall, at many of the fashionable bazaars this season, such things as dress, and blouse lengths, dainty handkerchiefs, petticoat frills, scarves, boas, pretty stocks, sprays of artificial flowers for hats or corsage, "vanity bags" fitted with powder-puff, mirror, and bag of rice powder, have been quite usnal, while numberless other useful items have found R place among the stock, and been quickly bought up. Good china, and antique glass have also been much to the fore and sold well at all the recent bazaars, while the inevit- able stall, stocked with heads and artificial gems, has generally been the first to conclude business operations. Noted Needlewomen.—lt is often said that the women who seriously take up beautiful and artistic needlework, have reached vanishing point, and that the few who pretend to aspire to the art, purchase a piece of work already half done, play with it in the drawing room occasionally, and then send it back to the shop to be finished. Doubtless there is much truth in all this, but nevertheless there are still many women left who , handle their needle with great skill and cleverness. 1 Among Royal ladies Princess Christian and Princess Louise Duchess of Argyll. are accomplished needle- women, so also is Lady Westmorlanu, • whose embroideries are the wonder and admiration of all</p>
        <p>privileged to see them. Lady Carew: too excels in embroidery, and is at present working busily at the panels for the drawing-room at Castle Boro. her country seat in Ireland. Lady Viola Talbot, Lady Scarborough, Lady C3nthia Graham, and Mrs. Clifford Gory, are all accomplished needlewoman, each striking out in her own particular way. Patchwork, carried out in wondrous patterns and variegated colourings, is in great favour ,gain for sofa cushions, the centres and corners of bedspreads, footstools, and divers other pur- poses. Fashions in Coats.—According to leading tailars in Paris and Vienna. the long directoire coat will be worn this autumn and coming winter, by the women who aspire to be in Fashion's van. Only those 'among us, however, who can afford to pay for the "master hand" should indulge in such a garment, as, unless cut, and fitted to perfection, it spells utter failure. Moreover, one needs to be tall and slender to carry the directoire coat well. Growing girls should never be given a tightly fitting coat, the sac variety being far preferable in every way.</p>
        <p>MA DGE.</p>
      </div>
