      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <p>•</p>
        <p>GtZ YOUR ER FOR. McDOUGALL'S Pewit SELF-RAISING FLOUR The Examiner, Saturday, September 2, 1905. WOMAN'S REALM.. HOW FLOWERS HARRY.</p>
        <p>mtuate al tong, spiral stalks, which can be com- pressed or lengthened according as the level of 'water may reuuire. As the levels of the Jivers change by sudden umows of water, these flan - ers can adjust themselves to . be always floating by means of the spiral apparatus. The liaaAe flowers are very small, and have no stalks at a: to enable them to reach the surface. When, however, these are ripe, the pollen grains de- tach themselves from the stamens and rise to the surface. On the- surface of the water are the female flowers, lying with open petals for fertilisation, and the pollen grains ripe for their part of the wondrous process. The pollen grains float about freely like tiny boats among the flowers, until a ripple on Lhe water, raised by a passing breeze, ushers them into a flower haven. Then is the pistil fer- tilised. Soon after this is effected, the long, spiral stalks coil up, and carry the seed capsules down to the bottom, where the seeds can ripen in perfect safety. While we watch the water- lilies, which gracefully bedeck the edges of the smooth lake, and which tire fertilised by insects, let us remember that there are other aouatic plants which have their pedigree even more curiously sustained.</p>
        <p>Artificial Cross fertilisation is carried on by the experienced gardener with some of his most glorious varieties ot flowers. With a fine camel hair brush, he can 'take, the pollen when ripe, from one pansy, say, and paint the pistil of an- other coloured pansy, when "that pistil is ripe for impregnation, (thougli its pollen is not at maturity). He marks that plant, and keeps the seed of the capsule, and propagates the seed in his flower house. He then watches the flower of the new plant.. Hundreds be has to cross in this way before he can fall' upon a really nice variety ; but then the trouble is often most curiously rewarded. This is a grand study— only in its infancy. But these hints will give enthusiastic renders the means of understand- ing varieties better, and propagating new cues.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</head>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME.</p>
        <p>QUEEN MARGHERITA'S jCKZ. DR. J-. G. ATC.PIIERS;)N, A story comes from the North of Italy, writes it Rome correspondent, which chews that Queen F. I t.S.E. Margherita is revaluing her spirits, so sadly (Les allalhematical Extuniiny in the lersi(., et' dashed by the death of her husband. She is now St. Andrews.) at Gressoney, which has always been a favourite summer rirlcrygintura With her, and is in the ' habit of taking long walks very often by herself, " If," says Schiller. " thou wonidst nttain t on which occasions she is followed by two big thy highest, "go look upon a flower ; What tie., carabineers, who, being nearly always the same, does willessly, that do thou wilfally." In Mu are more friends than servants to her. The other looking on a flower we must never think thu! day she left the villa alone and on foot, dressed ite chief end is to Minister to our sense of tile as usual, and on her return, when near the vit. -i. • . • oeauttlnt in form and colour. The poet Gra;,. lage, stopped at a peasant's hut, hesitated, was wrote.. when he wrote the saddened pantile smiled. and entered. the rarabineers, of course, between Flamm life :Ind flowers : " Full mon,. remaining outside. A half-htur passed, an hour, then two hours, and she still did not appear, a flower is born to blush unseen:" For no flowe. when, seriously alarmed, ths watchers took their blushes without being observed by some iuseer, courage in both hands, looked inside, and were Their beauty is not for man's p:easitre ; tin dazed to fine .no one—not the shadow of a person, purpose of the varied tints and exquisite form, and no door but the one which they were watch- as well as of the flue odours. is not for man', ing. What had become of her? Making record gratification. It is for the ' protection of tle time to the Villa, they found themselves the plant's health. 'lids will be explained in tb. joke of the household, and to anxious inquiries theory of cross-fertilieation. were told that the Queen Mother had arrived Look at a bunch a primroses ; you canine two hours before. It seems she had gone into • I help adadring the rich. creamy flowers. Cariosi y the hut dressed in black, and come out in full peasant's costume—short red skirt, black corsets, tempts you to examine one or two more care- and white chernisette—and had thus quietly fully. First, you take off the five outside green passed the carabineers, who did not even glance long-pointed leaves, called sepals, which first ie at her, having forgotten the time when she went all protected the young flower. . You next re- mountaineering day after day in a like costume. I move the five petals or flowers, and you observe. USEFUL SERGE FROCK.. .. insidOlte tube to which- they nre attached, thi ... . . . .. _ .. .. . .</p>
        <p>A smart and useful little frock of blue serge stamens with short stalks. Lastly, on the top 41! forms the subject of our sketch. The colour • the stalk of the flower you fina the pistil, r chosen is not the real indigo, but a brighter tittle swollen at the bottom, as it contains the and more becoming shade. Very simple in style, seed ready for fertilisation. The petals are di- the •bodice almost entirely consists of small box. sided in the centre, as if there; had been origin. pleats, well stitched and pressed. The turn- ally ten leaves. And, at the inner parts as they down collar, the shoulder straps, the pieces round the armhole, and the elbow cuffs are oi lent. to the tube, the colour mach darker, and of dark blue velvet strapped with white cloth. Box. an orange tint. Looking over a few you pistol you find pleated undersleeyes, closely fitting to the arm, that some are " pin-eyed," having come from beneath the elbow sleeves, and an long, up to the top of the tube, and the stamens grouped round the throat. Others =tin, are " thrum-eyed " when the pistil i- short, and the stamens are further an. What is the reason of the difference?</p>
        <p>DARWIN AND THE PRIMROSE.</p>
        <p>That question strikes at the btisis of the phi- losophy of flowers. The difference observed in the prinvoses was for many years thought a mere freak of nature, and nothing more ; al- though close observers find that the two types are nearly equally distributed. It remained nn- explained until Darwin's genius solved it. Th,- old; philosophy abont flowers, as well as plant-. was that all wern created solely for the deligll; a man. But the new thcory, °rig:tinted by Darwin, and cultivated by Lubbcck and others, Ls that all qualities possessed by plants of ever., :lescription, are just those which are of the greatest importance to the plants themselves. Most nowers require cloning. The floral machinery Is arranged to prevent self-fertilisation, at least in the first instance. An insect visits the prim- roses, being attracted by the petals ; it alights on a short pistilecl form and dusts its pro- boscis with the polleugrains which fall from the stamens at the flower's mouth. It then passes to a long pistiled form and shakes off this pollen upon the button-top of the pistil. This soon impregnates the seeds below through the tube of the pistil, for future germi- nation. Thus insects play a more important part than " design," in the flowering and pro- pagation of plants. A remarkable change has thus been wrought on the theory of flowers within the last few years. A century ago none would have doubted that vegetation of all kinds was created purposely to suit human needs. But notv enlightened theologians have modified Paley's reasoning by Darwin's conclusions.</p>
        <p>INTERMARUYEKG NOT APPROVED.</p>
        <p>piped at the wrist with white cloth and blue velvet. Blue velvet also composes the shaped waist-belt. The skirt corresponds closely with the bodice, and is arranged with a yoke of box- pleats stitched down to below the hips, and then released to fall in full folds to the feet. A touch of daintiness is given to the costume by: the lace neckband and suspicion of vest shewing above the velvet collar. The hat is, of course, burnt straw, simply trimmed with dark blue velvet.</p>
        <p>In some plants, moreover, the stamens are Found in one flower and the pistil in another. To effect fertilisation in this case some of the pollen must be conveyed from the pollen-case to the pistil. In the primrose, which has the organs entire, though modified in size and ar- rangement, the pollen and pistil are not both ready for fertilisation at the same time. As a general rule, there are remarkable contrivances to prevent the pollen being shed on the pistil of the same plant. And the great lesson of this is. that it is not to the plant's benefit, but other- wise, for its own pollen to fertilise its seeds. The sexes are, as it were, too " sib." Careful obser- vers have found that all plants fertilised with pollen from another individual produce far more robust offspring than the plant which is fer- tilised by its own pollen. Why this is so, no one can tell ; but it is beyond doubt a fact, ap- plicable to the animal as well as to the veget- able kingdom, that the offspring of individuals closely related are more or less sickly and weak. Seeing that Nature objects, as a rule, to self- fertilisation, we should naturally expect to find that she has taken means to ensure cross-fer- tilisation.</p>
        <p>Insects and the wink are the principal agents for carrying out this process. And the flowers fertilised by each of these agents are easily re- cognisable. To attract insects for the crossing work, the flowers are gaudily coloured or finely perfumed ; the former for day insects, the latter for night insects. It is not often that nature lavishly gives beautiful petals and sweet odour to the same flower. Anyone who has watched in- sects as they come near a beautiful flower wil remember how they hesitate in their flight to admire the brilliant colours which have allured them from afar. You have seen the flies in autumn with no idea of flowers or their colours attracted by the gaudy-coloured " ily-coges " which hang from the ceiling of the cottager's room.</p>
