      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>CLOTHES FOR OUTINGS.</head>
        <p>CLOTHES FOR OUTINGS.</p>
        <p>There are all sorts of women who demand all sorts of clothes as they wend their way annu- ally away from home, says the Queen. The knitted bolero which end just above the waist line, has no fastening in front, and boasts of bell-shaped elbow sleeves, after a game of lawn tennis, or golf, or any active exercise, is quite invaluable, and slips on easily for a drive. Some of these sweaters are of blouse form, ending at the waist, with a square neck, and some fit closely to the hips, fastening with gold or pearl buttons, with a broad coat collar, turning back to shew the blouse underneath. All these are invaluable to the sporting woman. The Inver- ness coat has come back to us, and come b., ck to stay, and when it is made of tweed which has been rendered waterproof it is most useful, as it can be slipped on and off in a minute. For the purpose Scotch tweeds are simply invaluable. sometimes trimmed with pipings, bindings, and buttons of leather. The capes, by means of straps, are sometimes made to form sleeves when desirable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>PRETTY WRAP.</head>
        <p>A PRETTY WRAP.</p>
        <p>With the approach of autumn a comfortable cloak or wrap becomes a very acceptable addi• tion to our wardrobe. Our illustration shews a very pretty, sensible, and new wrap of this kind. The material in which the original is made is a rough cloth of a deep prune shade. The novel feature of the cloak is the shaped yoke, bound on each side with prune velvet. When the cape is worn open the long ends of this yoke fasten behind the back, and keep the cloak comfortably in place. When, he ,ver, the wrap is closed,</p>
        <p>t ' „ i they hang down the front in the form of two long, ornamental stole ends. From beneath this 8 yoke hang two shaped, frilly coachman's capes, and beneath these again the cloak proper is I gathered in full folds. This wrap is very grace- ful and becoming, as well as extremely practical. llt should, however, be Cut long enough to COM) well below the knee; otherwise it loses.much of ! its style. ,</p>
