      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>FASHION OF A FLOWER'S DRESS.</head>
        <p>THE FASHION OF A FLOWER'S DRESS.</p>
        <p>Inside the flower, too, is generally honey of some kind to decoy the insects with its sweet- ness. Its true fanction is by attracting iniects to secure cross-fertilisation. Indeed, it may be laid down as a general rule that those flowers which are not fertilised by insects, are small in size, and do not possess either colour, scent or honey. We find, then, that nearly all insect- fertilised flowers are brightly and conspicuous- ly coloured. The bright hues are to attract in- sects to the flower. The streaks towards the central opening guide the insects to the honey reservoir with in and floating over the flower is n peculiar odour which entices them by its aroma. Thus the sweetness of the honey, the scent of the flower, and the colour of the petals attract bisects to carry on the work of fertilisation.</p>
        <p>Grasses, 7 sedges, rushes, &amp;c., which have no flowers at all, have no attraction for insects. The wind has to be the agent of the fertilisation there. Wind help is also employed in many of our trees, as the hazel, elm, oak, birch, alder, pine, &amp;c. ; and • our shrubs as the nettle, the Imp, dock, &amp;c. hi these there is a considerable quantity of pollen. Though half a dozen pollen grains are sufficient to fertilise a seed, yet thou- sands are sacrificed to ensure this germation. Everyone must have observed the clouds of pol- len produced by tile Scotch fir. The sulphur rain is familiar to people on the Continent, when the wind shakes off the pollen, and carries it in its arms for miles, to fall in quietness in another district. It has been estimated that each flower of the Pony produces between three and four million grains of pollen : and each flower of the dandelion a quartet of a mil- lion. Such a sacrifice is required by Nature for the absolute security or one life•—</p>
        <p>" So careful of the type she seems, So careless of the single life."</p>
        <p>But the microscopist can go lartlier. He can detect, by the shape and character of the grains of pollen, whether they are to be carried by in- sects or by the wind. lie finds that the pollen grains of insect-loving plants have their surfaces roughened over with minute points, or the means of easily attaching themselves to the hairy bodies ot insects. The bine-bell and the yellow water lily are noted en,implos. On the other hand. ti e pollen grains tlie wind fer- tilised plants or• exeeedingly ugh t,and smooth. with much exp .sed surface, as in tie case of maize. There are tome !duals, however. which could neither be ' •-• ' ;,• our by the</p>
        <p>wind. Some aquatic plants nave a. curioli method of crossing. No insect can touch tile pollen : the wind has no power over it. What. then, is the fertilising agent in the case of such plants ? How can the pollen of one plant he conveyed to the pistil of another, especially when the plants are of two sexes—the paw' entirely in the one (the male), and the pistil and seed capsule in the other (the female).</p>
        <p>TnE WOOING OF WATER PLANT&amp;</p>
        <p>Nature is fertile hi exnedieuts. Tt seems that 1 in many ways she fulfils herself, " lest one good custom should corrupt " the vegetable world. She employs water to effect the purpose. Oils of the most curious plants of this kind is the . - _</p>
        <p>......*....</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>Farewell Service. Wesleyan Ministerial Changes</p>
        <p>On Sunday evening, in Bewsey-road Wesleyan Church, the Rev. R. Wilkins Rees who shortly leaves Warring- ton for Fleetwood, said farewell to the congregation and the circuit, taking as his text the words :—"The grace of our Lord Jesus Christ be with you." At the close of his sermon the rev, gentleman expressed the hope that a spirit of brotherliness would prevail in the church, and that the congregation would receive the new ministers in a spirit of kindliness, It was in the best interests of the church that they should do so. There was a great sphere of usefulness in the church at Bewsey-road. During his connection with the circuit much kindness had been shown to him, and, as he was a very appreciative man, he should never forget it. He felt deeply grateful to all the friends. Mr. Wadsworth presided at the organ, and at the preacher's request the hymn commencing" Great God of wondersi "was sung to the tune " Sovereignity." The last hymn sung was "The day Thou gayest Lord is ended "—Mr.ReeA will, leave this week for the Blackpool circuit.—The new ministers will be the Rev. Sidney Pitt (superintendent) the Rev. W. Mellor (who will succeed Mr. Rees) the Rev. Joseph Manning and the Rev. Cecil Parker.</p>
        <p>St. Clement's Mission. The Rev. T. W. Barber, preaching at St. Clement's Mission to a good congregation, on Sunday evening, took as his text the words And when He was come near He beheld the city and wept over it." The rev. gentleman said that Jerusalem could be taken as typical of Wa.rrington. Jerusalem had its amusements, as they had in Warrington, some of which were of, a doubtful character, and it was their duty as members of a Christian church to ask themselves, before partaking of any doubtful pleasure, were they right and proper in the sight of God. The same Christ which beheld Jerusalem beheld Warrington. Would Jesus weep over the sin of their souls ? Was the business life of War- rington a Christian business life ? Did they act in business as Christ would act were He in their place? Jesus was their great example, and it was their duty to try with God's help to copy that ideal life,remernbering that it was the life and character of each one of them that went to forth the life and character of Warrington. In the afternoon the Rev. T. W. Barber conducted a children's end young people's service, taking for his subject" Baptism, Confirmation and Holy Communion." At the evening service the choir rendered a special anthem. Collections were taken at each service on behalf of mission expenses. Bev. J. F. Matthews and Dishonesty In Business. Preaching at the Latchford Baptist Church on Sun- day the Rev. J. F. Mltthews took for his text Psalm xxiv., 4, "He that hath clean hands and a pure heart : who bath not lifted up his soul unto vanity nor sworn deceitfully." He pointed out that there was as great a need to-day as in the times of the prophets for men with clean hands, clean lips, and clean souls, especially in business. He quoted instances from a recent press correspondence, on business frauds and dis- honesty. While admitting that inducements to fraud and lying,might be offered by the cupidity of customers for driving a hard bargain' he did not thin:: that any real justification could be found for the perpetual string of lies and dishonest practices which so fre- quently obtained in many business houses. He rather found the cause and ground of it all in the passion for getting money in the easiest way, and in the basic business axiom, that at all.costs sales of goods must be effected, whether customers required them or not. If business could not be conducted on purely ethical principles, then so much the worse for business, and the men. It would ultimately be found on the low ground of policy that honesty was.certainly the best policy. Turning to another phase of the same question, that in which young men and women were often compelled to cheat and lie,or lose their situations Mr. Matthews made the following suggestions :—That employers who conducted their business honestly and the various shop assistants' associations should combine in one federation 11) for mutual protection against dishonest firms; (2) for the promotion of a higher ethical standard in business ; (3) and for the purpose of safe- guarding the young people, who at the risk of personal loss and suffering, refused to sully, their con- sciences by lying, and their bands by cheating ; (1) that the general public should be asked not to do busi- ness with houses that were known to practice fraudulent devices, and that made the struggle to do right on the part of their employ4s exceedingly difficult ; (5) he would invite the co-operation of the Press, in the exposure of mean practices on the part of buyer and seller, and in the formation of a healthy public opinion. Turning to the young men and women present, Mr. Matthews urged them to tell the truth, to act the truth on every occasion, and to take the risks. The truth- loving man usually triumphed in the end. The God of truth, while apparently allowing the tyrant-taskmasters a temporary triumph, would bring the wickedness of the wicked to an end, and justify truthfulness and integrity before men.</p>
      </div>
