        <p>5? .. V, , a., .. .7, .• 10,1, $ I'l 1 @ tt.,,, 1, lirt C T" 111 F-4- ,; '.„; .i, ~, , 5U:417;0'4. . 11: cl, : „ A , } :',,- L 6 4,,4A'A ter.B 41 .kfOV'' CV` 4 ev--- 1v e d .1..., 4 ~7' v 0 ' .xi-Jv IS</p>
        <p>BY [WM MENEM DIAMONDS.</p>
        <p>The wife of Guatemala's assassinated President was detained at San Francisco, U.S.A., by Customs Officials, who seized her famous collection of jewels, and demanded X 12,000 duty. She wore them on State occasions and everybody thought they were worth :C200,000.</p>
        <p>Toiegra,m from "SAN FRANCISCO EXAMINER,"</p>
        <p>March 15th, 1904:</p>
        <p>"Madame Algeria Reyna Barrios' famous diamond necklace, which she as worn on State occasions in Guatemala, and at social functions in New York, is not composed of high-priced diamonds after all, but are Lucios Scientific Diamonds. Many other gems in which was thought to be one of the most magnificent collection of jewels in the world are but clever imitations."</p>
        <p>This astounding discovery was made by Custom House experts, who examined them for the purpose of fixing their value.</p>
        <p>Now what do you say to that? If Lucioa Scientific Diamonds are good enough for a President's wife to wear on State occasions, don't you think they are good enough for you? If expert jewellers selected by the United States Government to appraise such articles think Lucios Scientific Diainondp are the high-priced kind, do you think any ordinary citizen would detect the difference?</p>
        <p>MADAME' ALGERIE REYNA</p>
        <p>We defy them to distinguish Lucios Scien- tific Diamonds from the kind sold in exclusive jewellery stores every day for fancy prices.</p>
        <p>Come to our shop and see LUCIOS SCIENTIFIC DIAMONDS—</p>
        <p>the kind Madame Barrios wore.</p>
