      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>Ii7PE Vi ROSE</head>
        <p>Ii7PE Vi ROSE</p>
        <p>By GERALD BIS&amp; • • CHAPTER Vl.—(Continued.) " I have heard a lot about you the last two .d</p>
        <p>I lays, Lord Ben Nevis," she iaid, opening the -conversation. "From whom ? " he asked, forcing himself i back to his immediate surroundings. "From Charlie," she answered, laughing. "He is most enthusiastic about his new friend, and is never tired of talking 'about you." , "That's very nice of him," said Ben Nevis, smiling with conscious pleasure," but I'm afraid you must have felt bored with me before you met me." "That's better than feeling bored with you after meeting you, isn't it 1" she rejoined. That is more usually the case with stran- gers." "I hope to be an exception as far as you •are' concerned)" he said, laughing back at her. "You know I, on my side, have heard lots about you since my return." "From Charlie ? " she asked, almost in. differently. "From 'everybody," he answered. "Then I'm araid you must be bored with me," she said, closing the subject. Then she went on : "I notice that you have , been making love to my Aunt Grizelle." "So much so that she has asked me to dine with her the day after to-morrow." " I'm afraid, then," said Bina, "you'll have to put up with being bored with more of me, as I'm going to be there. I've got a night off, as mother has a meeting of her 'committee' and is entertaining them tc dinnes." • "I was told that you would be there," said Ben Nevis ," but it did not strike me as a great drawback I There is to be no party, I'm glad to say, and we are going quietly to a theatre afterwards." - " That's jolly," exclaimed Bina enthusiasti- •cally. "I enjoy my nights out as much as a housemaid, especially the ones with dear -old Aunt Grizelle. But they are few and far lielf ween in the season," she added, with a smapicion of a sigh. What is your mother's ' committee ' and •what does it do ? " asked Ben Nevis. "Oh, I don't know, exactly," answered Bina, laughing, "and I don't worry particu- larly. It only cane into being a few weeks ago, and its doin are kept mighty dark. It's father's latett f d," she explained, "and it is supposed to be busy organising a plan for the promulgation of some new political views which are to stagger creation when repro- duced in their finished form. But no one," she concluded with a smile, "takes them very seriously, except, of course, themselves, and Charlie and I certainly don't worry very much about it." Ben Nevis laughed. "Probably they are going to turn/socialists or Mormons or something equally absurd,' said Bina : "but they always have great schemes in hand, which seldom come to fruition." Ben Nevis was just beginning to feel thoroughly at home with the candid Lady Sabina, who inherited much of her aunt's . conversational style, when she was abruptly claimed by her next partner. " Good-bye," she said, when he expressed his intention of leaving, "we shall meet at Aunt Grizelle's the day after to-morrow." " If not before," said Ben Nevis. And they parted like two old friends, rather than newly introduced acquaintances. 'The attraction seemed mutual.,</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>CHAPTER VII.</p>
        <p>THE next day the papers were again full of the murder of Sir Henry Mornington, but threw little or no fresh light on the subject. They were abundant in their theories, and full of letters from the. public, playing detec- tive. But the only Teal fact of importance which they could adduce was the discovery of the :cabman who had driven the late Chancel- lor of the Exchequer from the direction of Hampstead to the Carlton Club, and he had simply produced himself in answer to the sen- 'sation' of the day before.' So therein the , press had little to boast of and nothiitg to , plume itself on. ~. Special, reporters, crime specialists, and descriptive writers, one and all, had failed in - .any way to get a scoop for themselves or their ..papers, or even the semblance of a scoop, ' There were no fresh facts, simply the facts with which the whole country was fully familiar, ' and all that energetic journalism could de ' was to embroider upon this foundation and 2. draw false deductions. This it did with its , wonted facility, and there was published • much that was most readable but equally r misguiding on the great topic of the day. : The "Morning News,' Which, under the ~ xlitorship of Nlr. Reginald Carruthers, a journalist of the new school, who had made 'nis mark young, was particularly daring upon the subject. It published a special .." article in which the crime, coupled with the '-' two previous " White Rose" Murders, was ▪ denounced as purely political and ascribed to F. a caucus in Ireland. It traced the history of Ireland for the past century and a half, show- ing how the Irish had during that time fol- lowed a policy of aggression, forcing, each point from the Parliament at Westminster .! by active hostility or open obstruction. The ' Phcenix Park murders were quoted as a parallel in point, and the whole article was Written in a most inflammatory tone. ‘ " Whether," it concluded, " the. Irish 1 Members of the House of Commons haye any ,F knowledge or cognizance of this wide-spread k conspiracy to force long deferred Home Rule „ or not, it is not for us to state 'at present, mindful as we are of how the "Times" got ba- t' , ten by injudicious candour a quarter of a cen- tury ago: but we are convinced that this plot , emanates from Ireland, and that one day its magnitude will be :aid bare. Probably it is .' worked by a committee of malcontents, financed from the States, who pull the -strings, hold the scales of life and death, and .; hope by this means to terrorise the present : Cabinet into submission. Mr. Duval was Murdered shortly after the publication of a , strong series of articles in the " Daily Cou- ; rier," on "Irish Methods in Politics." Lord Poole was a notorious opponent of such concessions to Ireland as he did not think .'2 jastilied. Lastly, Sir Henry Mornington, - only recently in his capacity of Chancellor of the Exchequer, refused any rebate in the Payments under the Land Bill of 1903. These „ facts are significant enough in themselves, - and Scotland Yard should look for another , secret committee across St. George's Channel. . - It looks ,as though we are in for an epidemic 431 'moonlighting' in London." This article certainly caused more public '. excitement than any other upon the subject, , fld the "Morning News" sold out early in the day. On all sides it aroused speculation. and on many indignation. . The other great topic of the day was the Penittg of Parliament by the King in person, •and it was generally surmised that some refer- ence would be made to the murders in the '.. ,sPeech from the throne. And the murder of ?tr Henry Mornington, it was known, would us discussed in the House during i the after- .- /Icon, both in speeches of condolefice and in answer to certain questions which were down • the paper. Mr. Rathconnell, the leader of • the Irish party, too, it was thought, would ,bring forward a motion to discuss the article , in the "Morning News." Altogether, the % situation was fraught with many interesting Possibilities, and the air was full of ex- citement 2 .. Despite the cold, vast crowds thronged the 2 gall and Whitehall to give the, King and 1 ' I;ueen a loyal reception as they drove in , state from Buckingham Palace to the House , nl Lords, and the cheering was taken up _ coal' along the route as the state coach appeared ✓ !lveYing their Majesties in their royal c:0 a.Des. It was a picturesque sight, a touch of ,n C ient pageantry, but by "no means un- -1:9-rrliar to Londoners during the preceding de Si However, it never sermed to nail. ,</p>
        <p>ano,..mc.i, occasion of state brought out en- thusias tic crowds to cheer the royal progress. ' Westminster there was the usual snseemly rush from the Lower House to the Upper to hear the King's Speech. The House of Lords was packed from ceiling to floors 6,nd many peeresses were there in full dress. The Duchess of Hampstead looked particularly handsome in her flowing red and ermine-trimmed robes crowned with a ducal coronet. On every face eagerness was depicted, and there was unusual animation on every side. The King's Speech, however, proved to be politically colourless, and the reference to the murder of Sir Henry Mornington, though expressing the deepest regret, was purely formal and ended with the hope that his murderers would shortly be brought to justice. His loss to the country, the speech said, was one which could not be overestimated.</p>
        <p>After the ceremony in the Upper House had concluded, the members of the Lower House returned to their own part of the legis- lative buildings in a more orderly manner than that in which they had arrived, and the Speaker took the chair. The House of Commons was packed and all the galleries were full. It looked very dark and sombre in the fading light of the Winter afternoon, and, despite the cold outside, it was close and warm, more conducive to sleep than to close thinking. Its panelled walls and beamed roof, with the overhanging • gal- leries, took away what little light of day re- mained, and it was almost impossible to see clearly the whole length of the • building through the prevailing dimness. In the half light the Council Chamber of the Empire looked impressive, and, except in the Press Gallery, all was still and silent. A few groups of members had their heads together and were discussing the situation. As the Speaker took the chair the lights were turned on and the scene lost the awe ot obscurity. Everyone seemed to breathe more freely, though the atmosphere had not improved Nand the tension on the nerves was relieved.</p>
        <p>It was years since there had been a fuller House, and the official benches were par- ticularly packed. On the front Treasury bench was gathered the entirq Cabinet, and on the front ! Opposition bench there Was an equally strong array of Ministers out of office.</p>
        <p>After the preliminary formalities, Mr. Ossory, the Leader, of the I-touse, rose with his thin lips closely compressed and a stern look on his thin, clean-shaven face.</p>
        <p>"Mr. Speaker," he commenced, bowing to the Chair and speaking in a low passionless voice, "before going on to the business of the day, I have a very sad, nay, a terrible duty to perform. The Chancellor of the Exchequer, the right honourable _ member for Saffron</p>
        <p>Walden-, has been murdered, foully murdered, by the knife of an assassin unknown: and, by his death, we, his colleagues, the rest of the House, and the whole of the country, have suffered an irreparable loss. His rise in politics was swift but sure, and it was realised that not since the early days of the late Mr. Gladstone, when he presided over the Ex- hequer, had the financjal affairs of the nation been in such capable hands. Under his eye many points .in our fiscal system were readjusted, and it has been universally ad- mitted that he, by his firm stand and striking innovations, was enabled to place the incidence of taxation upon a far fairer basis than ever before." In this strain the Leader of the House con- tinued to speak for almost half an hour, im- pressing on his hearers the full measure of the loss of Sir Henry Mornington to his party and his country. Then he raised his voice, showing more feeling as he worked up to his peroration. _ "This, sir, this is the man who has</p>
        <p>been brutally assassinated, for what end •or object we know not. This is the man who has been stabbed in the back in the dark. This is the valuable life which has been cut short by the cruel knife of a cowardly murderer. What it means, what it betokens, I cannot take upon</p>
        <p>myself to say, but I can promise this Ho-use, and the country, sensational developments Nvhen the truth comes to light. The late Chancellor of the Exchequer left behind him a document, almost a posthumous docu- ment, written within a few minutes of his death and not received until after his death, in which he stated that he was on the track of thern greatest sensation of the century, but . .</p>
        <p>his sudaen death came before he could make any further revelations. Mr. Percival. the Premier, a century ago, was murdered by a political lunatic. Lord Frederick Cavendish and Mr. Burke wei e assassinated as the</p>
        <p>result of a cowardly plot. But this, sir, is no common crime, and time will reveal the reason. We are groping in the dark: but the light will come. The assassins of the Chancellor of the Exchequer will be tracked down, if it be within man's power to track them. He, sir, is gone into the great here- after, but we, his friends and his colleagues, are left. To us he has left by his death a legacy not only of sorrow and gratitude, but a legacy of vengeance. His blood cries out from the pavement of Park Lane, and, as surely as there is a God above us, justice shall be done. It is not vengeance—for ven- geance is divine—but justice, human justice."</p>
        <p>And Mr. Ossory sat down, showing out- ward signs of greater feeling than the House had ever credited him with.</p>
        <p>Mr. MaSsingham, the leader of the Op- position, rose as he sat down.. "Sir." he said, "I rise on behalf of my colleagues and myself to echo the grief ex- pressed by the First Lord of the Treasury in his eloquent speech, and to join in his de- mand for justice. We, as a party, may have had many vital points of difference from the late Chancellor of the Exchequer in matters political and fiscal, but that did not in any way blind our ayes to his self-sacrificing patriotism, his marvellous ability, his sin- cerity of conviction, and his firmness in what he considered right. These traits of his character, one and all, were as keenly appre- ciated by us, his political opponents, as by his closest friends and warmest supporters. This horrid crime, this dastardly assassina- tion has sent a thrill of anger and disgust through the whole Empire. Following, as it does, close upon the heels of two similar crimes—crimes so similar that the three would appear to form a series—the whole country is up in arms, eager that justice should be done: and I trust that it will not be long before the murderer or murderers, as the case may be, are hounded down by the representatives of the law and brought to the bar of human justice. As my right honourable friend op- posite remarked, there has never been such a national sensation, and it is to be hoped that a clue may be found in this last document of the late Chancellor of the Exchequer, suffi- cient to put Scotland Yard on the right track."</p>
        <p>The leader of the Opposition continued to speak for some moments in this strain, eulogising the murdered minister, echoing Mr. Ossory's sentiments and paying tribute to the dead Chancellor.</p>
        <p>'But where I ask," he concluded, "are the representatives of the -law ? Where in this crisis is our much vaunted Criminal In- vestigation Department ? It seems as though they and their resources have broken down completely. When then is the tale of assassination in the open streets, this terror that stalks by night, to cease? One—two —three cases of a similar nature have occur- red during the last few weeks, and no one is a whit the wiser as to the identity of the assas- sins It would seem as though it were not safe for any public character to be out in the streets after dark: and yet the police know nothing. It is time something were done, and done quickly."</p>
        <p>In this vein of indictment Mr. Massingham concluded his speech, paying a last tribute to the dead: and then Mr. Rathconnel, the leader of the Irish Party, rose.</p>
        <p>" Mr. Speaker, sir," he began, breaking out volubly into a torrent of words, "in in position as leader of the Irish party it is both my duty and my pleasure to add a tribute to the eulogies on the late Chancellor of the Exchequer; yet at the same time I ant placed in an intolerable position by the scurrilous attack of a leading London Damn</p>
        <p>Thus my speech cannot fail to be as much a justification of my colleagues on the cross benches and myself as a token of sympathy with the dead. Sir, we, the rrpresentatives of Ireland, though probably Arore politically hostile to the late Minister tl,sssi any othet section of the riouse, never railed to recognise his great ability and his steadfast unity of purpose. Outside debate and the heat of discussion we would have been the last to say a harsh word of this 'honourable foe. And yet we are practically accused by a London paper of being the mainspring of this foul assassination. We are, in a word, branded murderers by a Fleet Street ink-slinger, malevolent and malignant." _ _</p>
        <p>Gradually the lea-der of the Irish worked himself up into a frenzy of rhetoric, and ended by demanding that the editor of the "Morn- ing News" should be called to the bar of the House, citing as a parallel the case of the " Globe" and a similar leader at the begin- ning of the century. Mr. Ossory rose in 'reply, thanking Mr. _Rathconnell for the warm sentiments he had expressed with regard to the late Chancellor of the Exchequer and strongly reprobating the article in question. The paper, however, he added, had by the wording of the passage kept without the jurisdiction of the House, and he could only denounce in the strongest way the impression the writer had endeavoured to convey. At the same time, he 'could not see his way to taking any steps in the matter. He had already discussed the matter with the Speaker, who agreed with his views. Thus all he could do at the moment was to express his regret and disgust at such an article. _</p>
        <p>So the matter closed, and the ordinary proceedings of the House were resumed. It caused the utmost interest throughout the country, .it tended in no way to allay public excitement. It was obvious that the Govern- ment were as much puzzled as the man in the street. Thus the mystery was only deepened by the debate.</p>
        <p>0 1 , : ~. -...., , , , , . , . 1.......,,' -..•-i; *., • :";'-', ..i', V ..,-; •S .§.tl .. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 2, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 2, 1905.</p>
        <p>" That's young Lord Branksea— on the motor," he continued, as a horseless vehicle drew up, "and that's the Master of Gareloch —funny names these Scotsmen call them- selves—with him, and the other is Reginald Carruthers, of the "News," who made such a stir yesterday with his leading article on the Irish party and the White Rose murders/ But he looks pretty cool and unev‘vocerned.ti he concluded, ,</p>
        <p>i Carruthers stood immobile, watching the others as they laid their wreaths on the statue, and then placed his own beside them, obviously careless. Then the motor silently sped on its way, picking its path in and out of the traffic with graceful ease.</p>
        <p>"Colonel and -Mrs. Mclver," announced the man who knew, a moment later, "and that's Godsmark, the great Jew millionaire. I wonder what he's got to do with Stuarts and traditions. Lady Mary Renfrew," he went on, giving the names at intervals and jotting them down, "the last of the Paisley family. The Earl of Portcullis, Stuart Mackenzie, M.P., and his old wife; Lord Islington, new peer and no connection with the Stuarts or his- tory of any kind I Hallo, the Marquess of Swanage and the Earl of Struan together in the ele.:Aric carriage, arid Mr. and Mrs. Knox, millionaires and nouveaux nches. Mr. William Brinckley of the same kidney; Sir Alan Atherley, baronet, Life Guards, good- looking chap, isn't he? That's- Sir Napier Cordingle§, blue blood and no money." And so on the journalist went, giving each prominent person his name, till about midday he moved off with a nod to his companion:</p>
        <p>7 "There won't be many more now," he re- marked, pocketing his note-book, "and I've got enough for a good article. There's been quite a boom this year compared with the usual; I wonder why e" "What a lot of lit)ols I" muttered the other, as he turned away. [To be contsnued.l</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>CHAPTER VIII.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER VIII.</p>
        <p>THE thirtieth of January is a date which conveys very little to the ordinary person: yet it is one famous in English history, and one celebrated annually in the very heart ot Londoitsby a small section of the public. The thirtieth of January is the anniver- sary of the execution of King Charles the First in Whitehall, and each year the bronze statue of the " Sainted Martyr," astride his horse in Trafalgar Square within a hundred yards of the scene of this royal tragedy, is decorated by the faithful few, who remain true to the fallen House of Stuart. The rest of the year Charles Stuart rides solitary and almAt unnoticed; but each year, as the day of his execution comes round, there are signs and tokens to show that he has not been entirely forgotten five and twenty decades and more after this unparalleled event in English history.. .</p>
        <p>For many, many years this annual celebra- tion,this tribute to a fallen house, has gone on, organised by a mere handful of royalists so- called and Jacobite societies, and winked at contemptuously by the authorities. So small a ripple on the sea of loyalty has never been regarded as worth a serious thought, and thus unheeded these conscientious objectors to Brunswicks and Saxe-Coburgs have been allowed to strike out their own feeble little line, laughed at by the conniving representa- tives of a house in the heyday of its glory. Gradually their organisation has spread , and grown under the fostering influence of con- temptuous cognisance, and they have by degrees grown more bold in a petty way. For instance, on the night of the death of Queen Victoria they actually issued proclamations denying the right of King Edward VII. to the throne, and proclaiming the representative of the House of Stuart in his stead. Sucb an act in days gone by would have led to the Tower and Tyburn Hill: but in the twentieth century of tolerance it was received with amused, if contemptuous, smiles. The height of fatuity was reached in the celebration at Hampton Court ,of the two hundredth anni- versary of the occasion when the good- horse. Sorrel, threw its master„ King William the Third, causing his death. These, and other instances of petty disloyalty, were passed over without reproof or tokens of displeasure: and thus encouraged by not being discouraged, .the Jacobite societies took heart of grace and added to their, numbers from the hosts of people who are ever in search of a new sensa- tion.</p>
        <p>Everything in England is built up by repetition and tradition, and so in due course it became to be looked upon by tolerant out- siders as a pretty and sentimental observance. With this it became more fashionable, and many'people, who would have been suspected of better sense, paid an annual tribute of un- seasonable flowers to the concrete representa- tion of the Stuart tragedy. The day after the opening of the House of Commons was the thirtieth of January, and all the morning long there was a little knot of people in the south corner of Trafalgar Square, Four extra policemen were on duty at the statue to keep the crowd from coming too close or from standing too long. . .</p>
        <p>" Pass along there, pass along, please," was their constant reiterated order, as the curious crowd pressed too near or halted too long to admire the wreaths, asking each other the reason and then recalling the historic tragedy. But the throng was, more curious than in- terested, and 'only stopped in the way that Londoners will stop and form a crowd upon the slightest pretext- or at the sight of the smallest novelty.</p>
        <p>In the pale, thin sunlight, scarcely strong enough to drive its beams through the over- hanging fog, the equestrian statue glinted cold in the midst of its many wreaths, to each of 'which a card was pinned, disclosing the identity of the donor.</p>
        <p>Between ten and eleven the crowd was at its thickest and had grown quite big in its small way, as Carriage atter carriage drove up and deposited its offering. " Quite suggestive of a funeral," said on. bystander to another. yes," answered. the other; "but what tommy rot I I wonder the police don't stop. it. It's nothing but disloyalty, not even disguised." The other laughed. "It's too petty to bother about ; that's the reason."</p>
        <p>A carriage drew up with a flourish, and a footman sprang down from the high box-seat to open the door. "Her Grace of Hampstead," said the last speaker, "and the Duke, too. Well, I'm d—d 1"</p>
        <p>Dressed in rich sables, the Duchess of Hampstead stepped up to the statue with an air oi queenly grace and placed a huge wreath of imrnortelles on the plinth, while the duke stood by with an unusual look of excitement on his phlegmatic face. Then they turned away after a momentary pause. As they were re-entering the carriage, Mr. lan Frazer and Mr. Ellis Gordon drove up together, each carrying a wreath of white flowers. " White Roses," said the former speaker, making a note. " Correct historically, but ill-omened to-day. But it's like Frazer, he's a cynical brute." The duchess welcomed them with an un- usually gracious bow, and they stepped up to the carriage.</p>
        <p>"A sad- anniversary," she said, as she shook bands.</p>
        <p>"Yes, indeed," said Frazer, looking stern: "We shall see you both to-night," re- joined the duchess, and the carriage drove off as they bowed. Then, as the two men went up to the statue and laid their wreaths at the base, the stern look on Tan Frazer's face seemed to give way to one of ill-concealed contempt. "Looks ashamed of himself and his folly,'l remarked the other bystander. Then he added: "You seem to know the names of all these people." The other smiled, _ .</p>
        <p>" It's my trade ; I do the society stuff for the 4‘ Daily Reporter,'" he answered. I; That's why I'm here to-day, Halo! here's another." he went on, scribbling a note. Lord Stepney. President of the Local Government Board.'l</p>
        <p>Another wreath Was added to the EstA</p>
      </div>
