      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SEPTEMBER 2nd 1905.</p>
        <p>Japan has made PEACE IN THE EAST. peace by waiving a</p>
        <p>claim which was per- fectly just, but not essential to the consolidation of the victories she has won. Undoubtedly this concession is regarded not without bitterness by many of the Mikado's subjects. It is opposed to the views of Baron KOMURA. ; it is in a sense a diplomatic triumph for Ruasia;it is in all probability extremely distasteful to Marshal OYAMA. By the, world in general, Russia excepted, it is hailed as wise and humane. M. WITTE'S SUCCORS, and the incurable delusions of the Slavonic mind, will convince most Russians that Japan gave way on the indemnity because OYAMA was overawed by LINIEVITCII. The idea will be carefully fostered in Russia that the CzA.a's army came out of the war unconquered, and that the Japanese shrank from the crowning ordeal of strength. Already the Japanese pessimists are saying that Russia I will steaddy'prepara for the next war,that she will 1 double the Trans-Siberian line, and that in twenty years or so she will challenge Japan by land and sea. But it will take Russia all that time to look after her internal affairs, and her historic mission," even if it should be resumed, will have to suffer considerable delay. Japan meanwhile, will not be idle; and her strength, military, maritime, and political, will make her as 1 formidable as her adversary is likely to be in the course of the next generation. To-day she stands possessed of everything for which she undertook the war. She is mistress of Korea, guardian of Manchuria, and the dominant Power in the Far East. Moreover she is the ally of England; and the alliance, it is believed, has been ren awed on so broad a basis that the guarantees for the joint interest of the contracting Powers far outweigh in significance the surrender of the indemnity,and the restoration to Russia of half the island of Saghalien. To the world the end of the war is an immense relief, because the struggle threatened to last indefinitely, and to keep the neutral States in a</p>
        <p>constant ferment of anxiety. It is difficult to over-estimate the obligation which humanity owes to President ROOSEVELT. In the extraordinary ravings by Count ToLsTov, which occupied more than a page of Tuesday's " Times " Mr. ROOSEVELT was held up to execration as the wickedest man in history. According to TOLSTOY all statesmen are monsters of iniquity because all Governments are criminal. They are especially bad in countries, which possess what are called free institutions. Totsrov cannot contain his wrath at the spectacle of a democracy choosing a President or a Prime Minister; and so a ROOSEVELT is an odioes villain, and a GLAD- STONE is, if possible, still more odious. This precious message to mankind was published on the very day that peace was assured, largely by the courage and unflagging zeal of the stateman but for whom no Peace Conference would have been held at all. Perhaps the deluded persona who imagine TOLSTOY to be an inspired teacher will have some misgivings when they contrast his wild and whirling anathema with Mr. ROOSEVELT'S achievement. The world is governed by states- manship, however imperfectly, because there is no other means of ensuring an orderly development. The notion that anarchy would bring about the reign of brotherly love, unaccompanied by taxes; is worthy of the philosopher who proposed to abolish marriage in order to extinguish the human species. To-day the world is freed from a great burden. Peace is with us once more. Whether all the lessons of the war in the Far East will be laid to heart by the peoples whom they chiefly concern is doubtful. Russia has more to learn than Japan ; and with chastened hopes we shall watch the growth of that freedom of which the very modest germ now visible for Russians in their new Con- stitution is owing to the MIKADO'S victorious soldiers and sailors and not to the wisdom of the CZAR.</p>
        <p>The Conservative THE WROLE-HOGGEE.S Labour party's meet- SAD PLIGHT. ing on Wednesday evening fell very "flat." The audience was not large and it never became enthusiastic. The somewhat lengthy speeches were listened to with a goon-humoured sort of tolerance, but occasionally there were perti- nent interruptions. Tariff reform had to take quite a back place in the programme—in fact, only one speaker deigned to mention it, and that in a perfunctory way. Chamberlainism has indeed fallen on evil days in Warrington and Tory , speakers have to cast about for more attractive schemes to dangle before the electors. We should imagine that Mr. Robert Pierpoint M.P. must, by now, be deeply regretting his sudden plunge into the Protectionist whirlpool. Warrington people, who are fast recovering from the Chamberlainite fever which threatened to become epidemic, have a sharp recollection that 'our member" once boasted of being a " whole-hogger." That animal looked like being set-up as a deity at one time but it has since fallen into disrepute and rarely is its melancholy voice heard in the land. Free Trade will be the watchword at the next election, and we believe that Warrington will declare its allegiance to the candidae who has remained staunch to the sound economic principles which have for so long ensured the prosperity of our island nation.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas Mae- CANADA AND farlane's address at PROTECTION. the Town Hall on</p>
        <p>Thursday to the members of the Chamber of Commerce was an interesting contribution to the fiscal question, but we doubt whether it would help many of his hearers to a better understanding of the problem as affecting this country. What we have to deal with to-day are the proposals of Mr. Chamberlain. Now Mr. Macfarlane is not exactly enamoured of Mr. Chamberlain. Ho professes to be against extreme Protection—whatever that is—and like- wise against unrestricted importation. He favours a closer union within the empire and a 2to 5 per cent duty on all imports into the ar€ a of the Empire, but Canada and the other self-governing colonies are to stand aloof. They are only to be considered a part of the Empire when it suits them' but the mother country must still pay for their defence. Mr. Macfarlane offers us a draught which he would not drink himself. There is a sharp contrast between the Canadian and Chamberlainite view. Mr. Chamberlain advocates preferential tariffs as a means to ends—to the commercial union of the Empire and ultimately to its political union. Canada is in favour of such tariffs on their own merits just so far as they do not contribute to such ends, to which she is fundamentally opposed. Canada's view, and Mr. Macfarlane's too, is essentially selfish. Protection and Preference are favoured only so far as they will pay. The unifi- cation of the Empire by commercial means is quite another matter upon which the self-govern- ing colony of Canada will•exercise her option. In his criticism, too, of free imports, Mr. Macfarlane falls foul of Cobden, but with this aspect of the fiscal question we shall deal more fully next week.</p>
        <p>Perseverance is said COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON'S to bring its own VICTORY. reward. Councillor</p>
        <p>Pemberton has cer- thinly well-earned his by a long•continued straggle with the Water Committee. Practically the whole of the:three years he has been in the Conn- cil,Mr.Pemberton has striven with might and main to see the documents in possession of the Water Engineer relating to Mr. Lawson's contract, and which as a member of the Committee he was fully entitled to see. The matter has been frequently discussed in the Council, and Mr. Pemberton has been told publicly more than once that he could have the papers, but still they remained under lock and key. Now that the contract Is completed the papers have been produced to the Committee and instructions given for Conn- cillor Pemberton to have access to them at the Town Hall. This is in itself a victory for the Councillor, but it may prove a barren victory. Whatever his object was in desiring to see the 'contract, plans, bills, in connection with the work at Dalph-lane, their value to him has largely decreased now that the works are completed and the wells full of water. Nevertheless, Councillor Pemberton is determined to make the best of the matter, and on the minutes of the Council for next Tuesday's meeting he has a special motion request- ing the appointment of a Committee of investiga- tion into "all the accounts and transactions between the C irporation and Mr. Lawson in c nection with his contract at Delph-lane." Truly, of investigation Committees in Warrington there seems to ;be no end. So far, we know of none which have redounded to the town's credit.</p>
        <p>China is at last follow- CHINA'S COMING ing Japan. The East is PARLIAMENT. to be "civilised " though the East, which has so much to teach us, would probably haggle a little at the term. This is one of the causes of Japan's quiet confidence. She and Russia are agreed as to a commercial open door ; she has entered into a new and closer alliance with Great Britain; and she has, without doubt, a tacit understanding with China, based on sure know- ledge of the Europeahisation of that vast empire, whi:h will come about at an earlier day than many worthy people are inclined to think. The Dowager Empress, it is declared intends at the New Year to issue a decree for the establishment, twelve years hence, of a Chinese Parliament. China will be before Russia if the Czar does not mind. Only a day or two ago a farewell banquet took place at Pekin to the mein- bers of a Chinese Commission about to start on a tour of the world to study Parliamentary institu- tions of the Twentieth Century. That is the</p>
        <p>beginning only. The Government is intent, in addition, on maintaining abroad a body of students who will prepare their own country for Parliamen- trry government. This is a parallel—and well should the world note it—for the tour of the knightly Kamurai from Japan, thirty-five year ago, laid the foundation for the victorious first- class Power that we have seen grow up in a later day.</p>
        <p>We are pleased to WARRINGTON AND THE hear that the bright RIVER. prospects which were held out to War- rington's river trade as the result of the passing of the Manchester Ship C*nal Company's Bill of 1904, appear a little nearer realization. It Will be remembered that the Corporation obtained a series of clauses which at that time were termed the Magna Cnarta of Warrington's rights on the river. The most important provided for the carrying out of extensiveimprovements on the river at Bank Quay and these the company intend shortly to proceed with. The Borough Surveyor has received from the company's engineer the plans and work- ing drawings of the new works, and it is hpped that a start will soon be made with a scheme which must considerably facilitate traffic on the river within the Borough. Briefly stated, the company have undertaken to construct a dam, lock and sluices at Bank Quay, and the effect will be to give Warrington a dock nearly two miles in length with a constant depth of water of 8 feet. Tnia will provide all persons having rights of frontage on the river with great facilities for loaning and unloading,and the use of the wharves. Culverts will be pro- vided to prevent the apprehended nuisance caused by the water being impounded above the darn, Si it will be seen that the scheme is a large and cost- ly one and fraught with great possibilities to Coo commerce of Warrington. As we have previously contended, there seems to be no reason why tile whole length of the new dock should not in the course of a few years be bounded by wharves and warehouses and a considerable impetus should De given to local trade on the river and canal to the advat.tage of the town generally.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>CONSERVATIVE LABOUR PARTY"Nothing to do with the Official Leaders." CRITICISMS OF THE I.L.P.</head>
        <p>THE CONSERVATIVE LABOUR PARTY- "Nothing to do with the Official Leaders." CRITICISMS OF THE I.L.P.</p>
        <p>, , ----, The Conservative Labour Party held another open-a'' , mooting at Bridge Foot on Wednesday evening, Councillor W. J. Wilson presiding. Mr. FRANK FEARNELEY in the COurSo Of a 10115 ' address, said he wondered where the Warring,tel,l prophets of evil had landed themselves that night. time the party held a meeting the Progressive Liberal . League—he preferred to call it that—was also having , meetings, and they heard a great deal with reference to , the things the Government had left undone and of tbe / things they had promised to do which were also to b,4: left undone. He could not help thinking when he real , those speeches how terribly put about the politica. bagmen who did Warrington the favour oilter' Liberal side of unfolding their goods must be '— subjects to introduce. Mr. Fearneley pointed out tbol, little was heard about sugar now as it was at its norrael price although the Sugar Convention still existed. Tlle. Convention had no more to do with the raising price than than he had. That was one bogey gone. Wiltr". had become of Chinese labour? They did not beef . much about that now since the day-light had been le,s into the subject. His view was that he was not at 11,' in favour of Chinese labour stopping in South Africa ta, moment longer than was , necessary but he did Ta 4" want to make apolitical bent of it either in support the Conservative party's action or in support of hi!, own views. He could, however, talk about the subject' now and their Liberal friends could not because it wae a blown bubble, a "bursted up" indignation, and the result was that the political bagmen with regard to' these matters had made an awful mess of it. Contesting : the assertion that the past session had been a barred d one, the speaker pointed out that the Goyernment ha, psssed the Aliens' Bill and Unemployed Bill. He attn.' , buted the Liberal and Labour support of the latter., measure to the fact that the Irish leader had declared that it was absolutely necessary for the working classefl of England. He would like some Liberal with a geel;, memory to mention any period of Liberal Governmeab ' when the Government ever passed two measures like , those on behalf of the working classes in one session. Mr. Fearneley also alluded to the formation of a Cola; mission to inquire into Poor Law administration.o" said our present system was about as rotted, as any system could be. Referring to the Progressive Labour League's recent meetings* Mr. Fearneley said a friend of his went tcP , one of them and referred to legislation that had be__ee Co passed by a nservative Government, but Mr. Hew", said" Never " meaning that they never had passed then measures. He would suggest that Mr. Howes she"; think sometimes before he spoke, because that Was very unwise utterance for a man in his position ton%ke; There was some mention of a challenge to a debate es 4 discussion and Mr. Howes said that he (Mr. FearneleY, had said the Conservative Labour Party speakers wel , the representatives of the official Conservative Part! Warrington. Now he (Mr. Fearneley) thought were men present who did not agree with his Pel'ttto ' but who would contradict that, because when , question was asked him—he thought by Mr. Ile '„:, 1, himself—he distinctly stated, and he stated it 3024 - that they had nothing at all to do with tl'is official leaders of the Conservative party in hey town beyond that they were friendly when t ra met them—if they knew whom they were. TbeY Nv4im there in the interests of the Warrington Conserve. Labour party and either he or his friends Mr. Macartarl, , and Mr. Orrell on their respective subjects werooorn. prepared to accept any challenge to debate upo :ff. public matter with, anyone of prominence in or with anyone out of the town—in other words tniTra, did not limit whore the man should come from 01.:cf o , he should be provided he was a representative man , , party as they were of the Conservative Labour Par'rd„, Mr. MACAR.TNEY also addressed the meeting,contea,dsr ' lug that insocial matters the Liberal party was utte;.t.. hopeless while in social matters of immediate ltaP°.,rhe : ance the socialist party was entirely impracticable. , Liberals had made up their minds to be returned next election and unfortunately those who had on Prfbo ious occasions announced that their aim was to stnasll43o Liberal party were assisting them. The reasoa : was ,coo , they knew that unaided they could not climb into.tisi , unless they could catch stray votes from the l'i party—the party that they were one day prepare ...d ,00i damn, and the next day to support, because they hev-3r that by scratching one another's political backs !le i couldsneak into Parliament somehow or other on I: of .a pretences. The majority of the working classes , Conservative opinion, and although they might 133'0-00 l moment of political mental aberration they Iv_ast Toth y sooner or later return to those who had done the for them' and record their complete satisfaction _ sJlthe work of the Conservative Government, who aftertio, . had done all that had been done in a social way benefit of the working classes, d gated Mr. ORRELL also addressed the meeting an tinge , that a speaker at one of the recent Liberal raeresastas said the Liberal party had no policy to put fe port'!" , The Liberal party and the Independent Lebo ur_e to ' were he contended, running in hand in ord ;ado, defeat the Conservative party. They could he e.',. single candidate brought forward by the Labour: 0 , sentation Committee who was not a Radical stituencies Conservative working men trade unio 1." Socialist and that, he held, was not fair as in sornen„ , - ought to be brought out. ld acit Answering questions Mr. Fearneley said he COU honour the Independent Labour party until ittca iLri itself the Socialist Independent. Labour pm funtilor thiet pfluerdtgheedraintsoeel fotfoittaskecenoosemwoni tehyoforto people know plainly that it was a Socialist organizont!ra , The party was to day taking money from trade_ 2,;-',O , branches on the plea that it was an Independent La''' , Party and never mentioned its socialism. Labour - Explaining the position of the Conservative_ova- party, Mr. FEARNELEY said they agreed with Lierabour 1 tive principles but at the same time they as,'"o more t men desired to get the Conservative leaders to a for Labour even than they had done. he Conseo „vere A VOICE : Who is the candidate for t 2- tive Labour party in Warrington ? lme tat ni Mr. FEITLNELEY : Why, I did not know w° " running one. Further questioned, he said they stood on the Veil old Liberal plank of "measures not men."</p>
        <p>J Cruelty to Horses. . . Thousands of maggots we aid to infest a wo"3 Green, who was fined 4oosa. manesd acoats at Newtoh-le" Willows on Thursday for cruelty to the animal on those, occasion of the recent Newton fair. For another h Il ea of cruelty to animals at Winwick, Joseph ' was fined 10s, and costs. re sC ldwell, of IlindleY , , - on a horse belonging</p>
        <p>The Rev. Newman Hall, a nephew of the late Bev. , r Dr. Newman Hall, who founded Christ Chorch, Wes'. ' minster Bridge-road, has left the ministry of the CI: , gregationalists in order to join the Church of Englau eFionticrludheor ienhtobideerrsento,fh‘aVvoelvleorshat their votes are Less ordered by the magistrate to pay the overdue I'S their landlord having omitted to do so. rbseet €5OO</p>
