        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>th" We don't hear much about Chinese Labour now b•tat daylight has been let into the subject. It is a • "own bubble." Thus Mr. Frank Fearneley,the lead- 'lls spokesman at the Conservative Labour Party's eting. Really this is news. We were not aware .t9aat the British workman's indignation at the importa- 'oo of yellow labour into what was to be" a white "colony had abated in the slightest. It has repeatedly made itself manifest in no uncertain way alad will doubtless be felt with increasing force at the . !let election. There will be more " daylight " let tut f\ 0 the " bubble " on September 19th when Mr. 'lllthwaite speaks at Warrington with all the force of 11,1 authority on the subject. * * * ohn Shaffer Phipps and Henry Carnegie Phipps, 8 of the American millionaire who tenants Beaufort tle, Invernesshire, may cOnsider • themselves ."•17ttinate young men. They were charged kt Edin- Rl,lll'gh with shooting at and wounding three salmon • -,rters, by which one of them lost the sight of an eve. 4di° of the men gave evidence as to the shooting. One ..,.,""ed that he had since received seventy guineas COM- , nsation, and the other said he had been granted an 't atlitY of Vs. per week, whereas his regu!ar wage dear 16s. It is hardly nenessary to add that both ..erldants were found not guilty. It may be" better ne born lucky than rich," but it would have taken . of luck to have accomplished what millionaire ••• PPS's dollars made quite simple. ** * ' 4, he correspondence on, "What is wrong ? " in the sin 41. Y News" shows clearly, from the many expres- ila's of opinion, that something is wrong, but no one 14 Yet been able to say how the wrong is to he vglate4l. Perhaps Mr. G. Holyoake was near the mark he stated at the close of his interesting letter /en : know of no better rules to follow than those laid ItZl by the greatest social writer of America—the • Dr. Edward Everett Hale : "Look before. Look LT:vard—not down. Look forward—not backward. 4e,1,1(1 a hand." It is surprising what good can be ~ornplished in an earnest but unostentatious way. It .4e3' not altogether coincide with present-day ideas of Z..otheere". A big shout, plenty of noise, little work, brains—all these combined togethBr in a solid , faced with what is vulgarly termed " cheek," *_4ll too often the stock-in-trade of certain of our 'wig called social reformers.</p>
        <p>What has become of the Y.M.C.A. ? Dead? * * * "Mar ' know that --vel from</p>
        <p>--ny people do not A one can tra,. tlzte Thames to the Mersey by canal."—Mr. J. L. C. Booth in the "World's Work.' * * * 'The Conservative party can show a good record" aaid Mr. Robert Orrell at Wednesday's open-air meet- iPig• "Of salary drawing " was the ready retort of a listener, and the crowd laughed. *** • IVhat are "political bagmen " ? We ask the ques- tion of Mr. F. Fearneley whose frequent references to , them suggest that he has an intimate, and perhaps Personal knowledge of the species. * * * "Is there a branch of the Social-Democratic -i'ederation in Warrington ?" a correspondent writes to ask us. We have not heard of any. If there is, Perhaps the Secretary will write us ? * * * 1,5, which the horses of the Sanitary Works Committee won in prizes at the Walton Show, are to be handed over to the Borough Treasurer. Could not the money be divided among the men whose care of the animals has a lot to do with their competitive value ? * * * The Bishop of Ripon has become President of a League of Social Service which has been established, ' the object of which is to take in hand such questions us education, sanitation, housing, temperance and social purity. * * * "Result of the Japanese war ! "was the shrill cry fl some Warrington newsboys on Tuesday when the 'welcome news of peace appeared in the evening PaPers. It reminds one of a football match, and certainly the Japs are many points to the good as the result of the conflict. * * * The auditor's report has at last been issued, the documents relating to the Water Committee's con- tract with Mr. Lawson having at last been produced , for inspection, and thus obviating the necessity which _compelled Messrs. Bradshaw and Bolton to withhold :I;lieir signatures to the accounts. * * * "It is a mistake to talk about the claims of capital ' and labour. Capital has no claims. .Capital is a mere tool in the hands of labour. Capital [produces nothing, and never did, nor can produce anything. Labour does all the work, and is entitled to all the product of the work. We should never make the mistake of asking how wealth should be divided between the gardner and his spade, the author and his Pea, the merchant and his money."—Robert Blatch- ford, in a letter to " The Standard." * * * ' a motor-car case at Preston this week, one of the svitnesses, a newsagent, said that the draught behind ids back, caused by the defendant's car passing him , was as if someone was tickling him." This veracious person added that be was so terrified by the narrow escape he had had from instant destruction, ; that he sat down upon the pavement to recover. We ' are glad to learn that the witness was a newsagent. A man who can tell a tale like that ought to be connected with the press in some way. * _ The quick-'itching 'arness " which the Town Council refused to sanction at its last meeting had been, it seems, already purchased. The explanation ls that the tender having been accepted by the cora- tnittee, the Borough Surveyor had presumed it to be urgent and sent the order to Mr. Venn who delivered the goods. Now the committee requests the Council to confirm the purchase. If it does without a very strong protest, it will be neglectful of an obvious duty. Committees are too prone to buy first and ask for per- ' Inission afterwards.</p>
        <p>* * Mr. IL L. Outhwaite, who is to oppose Mr. Joseph Chamberlain M.P. in West Birmingham, will speak lilt Warrington on Tuesday, September 19th. As we 'Lave previously stated,M.r. Outhwaite is an Australian with a special mission. At the other side of the ,'world he was the founder of the Free Trade League, and is the recognised champion of White Labour. He is in this country for the purpose of ')Pposing yellow labour and upsetting its author "Joe." Afore power to his elbow ! * * * Chinese Labour is cheap, and like many other things 7asily obtained, also inclined to be nasty. There have oeen loud complaints from the Cape about outrages :2•lad thefts committed by the stray" " who tlave escaped from the compound, and the difficulty ''es in getting these wanderers back into the fold. An aPPeal to the Government has brought forth the decree that " any White shall have power to arrest Chinese." 'rhe Yellow peril is being appreciated in South Africa and we are perhaps not far from a state of things in ithieh:the wandering Chinaman may be shot on sight. I The Town Council, through the Finance and Estate Committee has received another snub from the Local ?overnment Board. In forwarding sanction for a °air of £l,OOO for land which has already been acquired, the assistant secretary to the Local Govern- Illent Board remarks acidly: "I am further to quest that in any future case where it is proposed to acquire land by means of a loan, the Town Council °till be careful to defer the completion of the purchase •limn the Board's sanction to the loan has been 13btained." This is what the man in the street vulgarly calls" one in the eye." * * * A certain Warrington alderman made a determined ~....frort on Wednesday to see the eclipse and failed. tlurrying home with a good supply of smoked and oloured glass he established himself near a window searched the heavens industriously but fruitlessly. he eclipse seemed so complete that he could not laricl the sun at all, and after an hour's vain endeavour .0 returned to town much disappointed. Meeting a ksriend he inquired if he had seen' the eclipse. Certainly," said the friend "most distinctly." The alderman was dumfoundered, and then in a hoarse 1111isper he said shyly, "I must have looked out of wrong window ! ' ** * , Warrington people can vouch for the fact that the eclipse came off according to programme. In ;!,lllat respect they were more fortunate than their nchester neighbours who saw nothing but the t total eclipse" to which they are almost daily accus- :.,t le,atled, the sun being completely obscured by clouds. ,tring the two hours of the phenomenon Warrington looked up" with a will, and even in the main streets !people could be seen scanning the heavens through 71:la0ked glasses or punctured postcards. Although !att sky was clouded Old Sol occasionally beamed sth through rifts and the passage of the moon across ae sun's disc was clearly visible. * * *</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Index to Inside Pages. PAGE. 2. Women'a Chat and Fashions. Our Local Churches. Swimming and Water Polo. " How Flowers Marry," by Dr. J. G. McPherson 3. Cricket and Bowling. "The White Rose,' by Gerald Biss. 6. Education Committee. Police News. Magazine Cuttings. Science Notes. Children's Corner. 7. The Free Churches. "The Vicar's Venture," by Athol Forbes, Notice. _ _</p>
        <p>The Warrington "Examiner" is now published on Friday aftbrnoon, price one penny, and is on sale at the various newsagents at about 4 o'clock. Should any difficulty be experienced in getting the paper the proprietors will undertake to deliver a copy at any address in Warrington by 5 o'clock on Friday after- noon without extra charge. Address all communica- tibns to "Examiner Office," Bewsey-street Warrington Tram-Car in Collision.</p>
        <p>An accident, happily not of a serious character, occurred in Winwick-street on Wednesday morning. The 11.30 car for the Cemetery driven by John Knowles had left the terminus, when the driver noticed a horse and spring cart approaching. He pulled up but Mr. J. Orrell, who was in charge of the horse and cart,was unable to check the progress of his vehicle in time and a collision occurred. The car is slightly damaged and the shaft of the cart was broken, but, fortunately no one was injured. LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES, UNDER THICCLOCK,BRIDGE-STB.EET.--AdVI. The Revision Court</p>
        <p>_ Mr. J. T. Foard, revising barrister, will attend at the Town Hall ott Friday, Saturday, and Dfonday, September Bth, 9th, and 11th, for the purpose of revising the lists of voters. Last year the claims, Conservative and Liberal, numbered 171, and the objections 92, or a total of 263; this year the figures are— claims 159, objections 85, or a total of 244. The number of persons on the lists entitled to vote is 11,815 as compared with 11,693 last year, or an in- crease of 122.</p>
        <p>KITCHEN RANGES, Fire Grates' Files &amp;c., largo stock.—J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Aovr. • Juvenile Templars Trip. On Saturday upwards of eighty members and , friends of the Young Mission Temple 1.0.G.T, left : Bank Quay for Frodsham. Upon arriving there they • made their way to the Mersey View Pleasure Grounds and here the afternoon was spent ia games of various kinds. At 5.30 all sat down to a substantial tea pro- vided under the supervision of Mrs. Brandwood. After tea the party were photographed by Mr. A. P. Walmsley and then indulged in rambles some through the beautifulbappy valley—and others ventured to study the mysteries of the caves. The return journey was made at 8.15 p.m. all arriving safe after a most en- , joyable days: outing. The arrangements were in the hands of Miss Ada Littlewood (superintendent) and Mrs. J. Brandwood to whom great credit is due.</p>
        <p>Warrington and District Canine Society. A meeting of the above sJciety was held at the Lion Hotel on Tuesday evening, when the prizes in connec- tion with the show held on July 13th were distributed. Captain Avery presided over a large attendance.—The Chairman stated that after all expenses had been paid there was a balance in hand of £23 12s. 41d. (Applause.) Twelve new members were elected.—Mr. White (secretary) said it was intended to make the next show more interesting by providing more classes for novices.—Captain Avery said it reflected great credit upon the society that during the past few months it had existed it had enrolled 115 members. In a few years he hoped it would be one of the finest members' societies in the country. (Hear, hear.) —Mr. Milling considered everything was satisfactory, and he thought they could not have two better gentlemen at their head than Colonel Edelsten and Captam Avery. (Hear, hear). PUBLIC BENEFIT.—IINDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCA.SHIRE.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Death_ of Mr. Joseph - Robinson, St.. Helens.</p>
        <p>A telegram was received at Wilderspool Brewery on Tuesday morning announcing the death of Mr. Joseph Robinson, of Fairfield House, Crank, managing director of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co.'s St. Helens branch. Mr. Robinson was seventy-five years of age, and died at Llandudno from pneumonia. He had been in failing health for some time. Deceased was one of the most highly respected men in St. Helens, and had also many friends in Warrington. 'He had been over fifty-five years connected with the St. Helens Brewery of Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Company, succeeding his father, lkfr. Samuel Robinson, in the management about thirty-eight years ago. He was a prominent Conservative and a vice-president of the St. Helens Conservative Club. For many years Mr. Robinson had been a trustee of the living of St. Helens, and he was treasurer of the 897 Lodge of Freemasons, besides rendering a similar service for abont twenty years to the Rose of Sharon Lodge of Oddfellows, who had made him a public presentation. Five y-eras ago, on his seventieth birthday, Messrs. Greenall's staff presented to Mr. Robinson his portrait and an illuminated address. CINDER ma CLOCK for Good BOOTS.—Advt. Primrose League Fete. The members of the Cl rappenhall Habitation of the Primrose League held their annual fete on Saturday in the grounds of Grappenhall Heyes, the residence of the Ruling Councillor and Dame President, Mr. and Mrs. J. Charlton Parr. There was a large attendance of members from Grappenhall, Stockton Heath, Appleton Thorn, and Latchford, and an enjoyable time was spent. The ponds were thrown open for fishing and there were many competitions for the prizes offered. The awards for the best catches fell to J. W. Houghton, 1; L. Leach, 2 ; and J. 'W. Leigh, 3. The sports were also very interesting, and iesulted as follows :-120 yards flat race.-1, H. Bailey ; 2, L. Beckett ; 3, F. Mclntyre. Mile cycle race. —l, J. Walker ;2, L. Beckett ; 3, J. H. Shakeshaft. Sack race. —l, A. Kelly •, 2, F. Robin; 3. G. Evans and ,T. E. Peters (equal). Ladies' race.-1, M. Mclntyre ; ,2, M. Walker • 3, K. Davenport. Tug-of-war (six a I side).—Mr. liayes's team. Walking competition.- 1, J. Massey ; 2, F. Lamb ; 3, T. Pittam. Youths' race (100 yards).-1, W. Mclntyre, jun.; 2, F.Hobin ; 3, G. Evans. High jump.— J. Massey. Tea was , served in a large marquee and at the close the prizes were distributed to the successful competitors by 'Mrs. Parr.</p>
        <p>Royal Liver Picnic. • The Warrington staff and friends had their first annual picnic to Liverpool and New Brighton,the party numbering 26 accompanied by their District Manager Mr. P. Delaney visited the chief office in Prescot-street and was shown over the entire building, commencing with the board room, where Mr. Edmond Marsh and other members of the Committee of Management put their work on one side in order to describe the various carvings and fittings of the room. Next the party were introduced to the heads of the various departments. At Mr. Middleton's department that gentleman ex- plained every detail of the workings of the Society, and also the working of other officers. A move was then made for Wilkins Yale Cafe, Liscard Park,where dinner was served in grand style. The afternoon was spent in visiting various places of amusement includ- ing the sands,where severalextracted much merriment from a game of golf. Tea was served at 5 p.m. giving great satisfaction, after which Mr. Delaney expressed his 'thanks to Mr. Hitchin for the able way he had arranged the outing. Mr. Flitchin suitably responded. A start was soon made for home, Warrington being reached at 9.30 p.m. all agreeing that a delightful day had been spent. Before dispersing a vote of thanks was given to the Committee of Management and to Mr. Delaney for the way in which he had helped to brighten the day's outing.</p>
        <p>CRISP AND r CREAMY.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>Tile Examiner, Satutday, September 2, 1905.</head>
        <p>Tile Examiner, Satutday, September 2, 1905.</p>
        <p>The Mayoralty. The meeting for the selection of Mayor is to be held on the afternoon of September 25th at 4-30. Water Polo —Cheshire Beat Middlesex. The final tie in the Water Polo C iunty Champion- ship was played at the Lister Drive Baths, Liverpool, on Thursday evening, when Cheshire defeated Middle- sex (the holders) by five goals to one. C. Ashton, of Warrington, played on the wing for Cheshire. MAIL CARTs Jand Bassinettes. the latest designs. J. S. GARLAND. 51 to 55, Bridge-street, Warrington. —ADVT. Earlestown Bookmaker Fined. Matthew Seton, an Earlestown bookmaker, was fined £5 and costs in each case for street bettiag on two occasions, the 25th and 26th ult., at Earle-street. It was stated that defendant had been fined the full penalty of £5 and costs on eighteen previous occasions and a reduced penaly on two other occasions before. WASHING AND WRINGING MACHINES try J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55 Bridge-street, Warrington.— ADVT. Street Improvements. Colonel W. R. Slacke, R. E., will hold an inquiry at the Town Hall at 10.30 a.m. on Tuesday into the Town Council's application t the Local Government Board for permission to borrow £7,483 to defray the excess expenditure incurred in connection with street widening and improvements. H. M. AsirTos's LIFE TONIC takes away thvt tired feeling.—(Advt.) Warringon P.s.A. Society. The weekly meetings of this society were resumed in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street, on Sunday. Mr. Mills occupied the chair. Miss F. Leicester sang "Sun of my soul" and "Oh, doubtinr, heart "very ' sweetly. Mr. H. Sherburn gave a capital address, the subject being "A young man's religion," HAMS, BACON AND Cimirsz are Dearer.—Advt. Bold-street Wesleyan Church. The anniversary services in connection with the above Church will be held on Sunday when the preachers vill be, in the morning the Rev. William Mellor, and in the evening the Rev. Sidney Pitt, ths newly appointed ministers to the circuit. Antheme and special music will be rendered by an augmented choir.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS, %WIRRAL WATERS.—Advt.</head>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS, %WIRRAL WATERS.—Advt.</p>
        <p>Bible Class Picnic. The members of the Golborne-street Baptist Bible Class had their annual outing on Saturday last, the place selected this year being New Brighton. The party, which numbered 35, left Warrington by the 1.50 train, arriving in New Brighton about 3 p.m. After a short stay on the sands, tea was provided at Mrs. Walker's Cafe, Victoria-road, at 4.30. After tea the party entered the Tower, the ballroom, and other places of amusement being visited. A start was made for home by the nine o,cloclo boat, Warrington being reached soon after 11, all having thoroughly enjoyed themselves.</p>
        <p>BuTTER IS ,SCARCER, and keeps Dear.—Advt. Practice for the Fire Brigade. A false alarm of fire was given on Saturday after- noon at three o'clock, by instruction of the Fire Engine Committee, with a view to enable the members of the brigade to test the appliances for extinguishing fires at Messrs. Walker's Brewery in Dallarn-lane. Nearly all the members of the brigade pat in an appearance, being met at the brewery by Alderman Smethurst (chairman of the committee) and Councillor Brough. Superintendent Turner and the deputy superintendent took with them the reel, fire escape, the steam engine "Captain," and other appliances, and a satisfactory test resulted. ASK for CErENDIOHAWS lIIGH-CLASt. MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. Fatal Jacksharping at Warrington. A sad drowning case was investigated by Coroner Brighouse and a jury at the Warrington Police Station on Monday afternoon. The evidence showed that on Sunday morning George Machin, the nine year old son of George Machin, boiler fireman, 30, Howard street, went for a walk in the country accompanied by a companion named John Kerrigan, aged thirteen when the lads came to the St. Helens and Sankey Canal they leaned over the water and tried to catch jacksharps with their hands. Unfortunately Machin overbalanced and fell in. His friend tried to reach him but failed and Machin sank. An alarm was raised and the body was recovered a little later and taken to the home of deceased's parents.—A verdict of " Accidental Death " was returned. PUBLIC BENEFIT. 'UNDER THE CLOCK FoR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Suicide's Pathetic Farewell. At the Warrington. Police Station an Saturday Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner, held an inquiry con- cerning the death of William Peacock carrier, of Lower Walton.—The evidence was to the effect that on Thursday morning a workman named Gerge Long- shaw found deceased lying in Arpley Meadows bleed- ing froin a wound in the throat. By his side was a blood-stained razor. Deceased was conveyed to the Infirmary but died on the way. He had been much troubled in mind for some months and a note found in his pocket read as follows :—" Dear Brother and Sister. You will forgive me for this mad act but I have gone out of my mind. I hope you will be good to Ciss and everyone else. It was all my fault. If there is anything left after settling up, give it to her.—Police-constable Metcalfe stated .that when he was called to deceased he was quite conscious, and when he was asked why hei had done it he replied, " You don't know all my trouble. lam better dead." A verdict of" Suicide whilst of unsound mind " was returned,</p>
        <p>H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC takes away that tired feel ins.—(Advt.) A Burtonwood Right of Way, Four members of the Lancashire County Council, with Mr. J. Shuttleworth as chairmah, together with several county officials, held an inquiry at Burtonwood, near St. Helens, on Tuesday afternoon, as to the public rights over a lane known as Kiln-lane. The path haid been stopped by the owners of the farm, Messrs. J. Fairclough and Sons, Warrington, and the Parish Council contested the point.—Mr. J. C. Satton, c!,erk to the Warrington Rural District Council, stated that, with the exception of the chairman of the Burton- wood Parish Council, the members were unanimous in declaring that the matter was not worth fighting about, and that from a financial point of view they woald not give 10 for it. It was suggested that the fight arose through pique on the part of the Chairman of the Parish Council and the owners of the farm on which the path lay, but that was denied. Fences put up by the farm owners had been pulled down by the Council chairman, who, however, had since apologised to the owners of the farm for what had been dene. HAMS, BACON AND CHEESE are Dearer.—Advt. Health Committee's Visit to Delamere. On Thursday the members of the Health Committee of the Corporation visited Manchester and Liverpool sanatoriums for the treatment of consumptives in Delamere Forest. The party included the Mayor, Councillor Evans (chairman of the Health Committee), Councillor Wilkinson (deputy-chairman), Dr. Gornall (medical officer of health), Mr. T. Longdin (borough surveyor) and Mr. T. Hallway (deputy Own clerk). They first drove to the Crossby sanatorium, where the Manchester patients are treated, and made a thorough inspection of the premises and various appliances under guidance of ?Dr. E. Lloyd. Afterwards the Liverpool Corporation's sanatorium was visited, the visitors being conducted through the building by Dr. Wood. They were, we understand, very favourably impressed with what they saw, and gained much useful informa- tion. At the Liverpool sanatorium 'Dr. Wood kindly entertained the committee to tea. The return journey was afterwards commenced and Warrington was reached about nine o'clock. There are in Warrington about one hundred known cases of consumption, and it is considered probable that steps will sooner or later have to be taken by the municipality to treat them. The alternative methods are for the Corpora- tion to enter into an arrangement to send patients to other sanatoriums, or to provide bungalows for the treatment of cases within their own area.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S CREAM CRACKERS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>FISCAL QUESTION.</head>
        <p>THE FISCAL QUESTION.</p>
        <p>DISCUSSED FROM A CANADIAN STANDPOINT.</p>
        <p>Address by Mr. Thomas Macfarlane.</p>
        <p>"A Canadian view of iuternational trade" was the subject of an address delivered before the members of the Warrington Chamber tal Commerce on Thursday afternoon in the Town Hall by Mr. Thomas MacfarLane, head of the Government Laboratories, Ottawa, and a prominent member of the Canadian Manufacturers' Association. Mr. J. Fulton Smith presided over a representative attendance. The CHAIRMAN observed that of all the possessions of the British Crown, Canada was undoubtedly the land of great promise, and anything which would contribute to a wider and more authentic knowledge of its resources and capabilities for trade developments, should prove acceptable to all concerned, whether capitalists or worktuen. The Association of which Mr. Macfarlane was a member, was composed of the most notable and influential captains of industrY in Canada, who were not only manufacturers of goods, but who, he thought, menu- lectured Canadian enthusiasm and sentiment, and a spirit of independence and national pride. Mr. MACFARLANE said Canada ought to be very dear to everyone present not only oa account of what she might have recently done but because of its being from the Atlantic to the Pacific tiritish soil. It was a country rendered doubly samed by the blood of such men as Woolfe and Brock who counted not their lives dear unto them so that they might extend the bounds of the empire. There was perhaps another more material reason why they should be proud of Canada. It repre- sented a large stun of their national debt. By the expendi- ture of large sums in times gone by Canada was con- quered and made part of British Empire. The acquisition by England of foreign or unoccupied territory and the subsequent transference of the same to the people who now occupied it had gone on for a long time. Now it could scarcely go on any longer, and if the empire was to continue to grow—and it might continue—we should have to find some other means of paying for and manag- ing annexed territory, because this country could no longer afford to meet, p:esents to the outer empire of pieces of territory which had cost immense sums and oceans of blood almost to acquire. If we did make aanexations it must be by means of a com-non fundl and common organization. He was a member of the Canadian Manufacturers' Association which represented the Protection element in Canada—the element which was perhaps not favour- ably viewed by people on this side. But if the Associa- tion was Protective in its character, and if Croatia as a whole was protective inits policy, they were at any rate no worse, in fact a great deal better tht u the adjoining oountry, the United States, which had, they frequently thought, been favoured to a greater extent even than Canada with the confidence of England. Instead of being merely Protective, the United States were ultra- Protective. They had got to the condition of so man tg- ing things as to cause their own people to pay for the goods which they manufactured for foreign countries. Mr Macfarlane instanced a case in which an American firm obtained a contract for steel rails in competition with English and other manufacturers by selling at a price eidolselower than they charged their own people for the sense goods. Unrestricted Protection therefore had the effect of causing the inhabitants of a country for whose benefit the system was invented. to pay more for the goods produced in their own country than foreigners paid for them. Protection had got to such an extent both in Europe and America that the rules of Free Trade were set at defiance, as it were, and as rainy obstructi ms as possible were placed in the way of Free Trade. Free Trade was no denht the policy which suited this country bast fifty or sixty years ago, and no doubt this country attained great advantages from practicing it. But about that time other countries found that they could provide the money for governing themselves much more readily by taxes on imports than by direct taxa- tion and for n while the system they adopted was one that England could not object to. Mr. Macfarlane pointed out that as the result the trade of the home manufacturers in foreign countries increase i enor- mously and ultimately they came to produce more goods than the people 'at home were able to consume. They therefore had to look abroad for a market, and naturally the market that presented itself to ' them was the almost unlimited Free Trade market of England. That was the point, he considered, at which the Free Traders failed to do their duty. They should have given notice to the other countries that if they went on sending their surphisigoods in that way a duty would be imposed to prevent it. instead of that. the Free Trade party adopted the ,principle of "fighting foreign tariffs by free imports.' That was the phrase In which an impossibility was defined. It wis at that time that the Free Trade party forsook the teaching of Cobden who never anticipated such results and would never have aporoved of such a development. Canada now practised a Protective policy and so far they had not produced such an amount of goods as to cause them to seek for markets elsewhere than inside Canada. The Dominion was not unrestrictedly Protective but Pro- tective so far as regarded the raising of the revenue which was necessary to carry on the Government. Mr. Macfarlane pointed out that England was Protectionist in that it undertook the naval protection of its colonies and depentienciesechilelin'the matter oflmilitary defence shelwas also largely protective,having saved Europe from tyrants from time to time. It was only in the matter of commercial defence that she was not Protective and left her interests entirely undefended and open to the attacks of foreign nations. He had had omission to tell Free Traders who haul vigorously condemned the conduct of foreign nations that foreign Protection could not exist and could not he practical if it were not for the Free Trade market of England. That was what, for instance, made it possible for other countries than England to supply Ctuada with her goods. If they looked at the trade and navigation returns during the last twenty-live years they would find that while the value of imports to Canada remained almost stationary in the case of England, it had in in the case of the United States, while n the Case of Germany and France it had increased to a similar eetent. How was it that these Protected countries could increase their imports into the country and com- pete with England and even somplant her to some extent in the Colonial markets? No explanation occurred to him except that foreign countries were protected in their home markets to such an extent that they could do with less profit in supplying the Canadian market and sent the gooas cheaper. That had been the else until the last four or five years when the prefer- ence which was conceded by Canada to the mother country came fully into operation. He had often thought it would be well for the mother country to say to her colonies: "Come let us reason together and see how we can arrange this matter." Some of them ia Canada no (Janet desired a preference in the markets of England,but that was not the first thing they considered necessary. Canada had been conceded the power of arranging her own tariffs in her own way and she was much more anxious that the mother country should look after her own affairs than look after the tariffs of Canada. What he believed the members of the Canadian Manufacturers' Association wished England to do was simply" Attend to your own commerce, do that which you think will be best for your own people and raise your revenue in the way you think proper and never mind us. We are perfectly able to stand and do our own duty." Although he had heard people say that the matter of preference was largely agitated in Canada for selfish purposes, he di 1 not think that could he the case, and they would rather be left oat of the account altogether just now. They would like to see the mother country, without reference to the self-governing Colonies, taking care of her own affairs and they thought she could do so and do it with- out hurting herself by making a closer union with her own empire. The mother country was not dependent upon the self-governing colonies in the ouster empire. such as Canada, Australia and South Africe, in them did not consist the whole of the empire. She had got India, the crown dependencies, and other Possessions seattered here and there throughout the world. It was quite possible for the people of Enelancl to form an empire of their own, independent altogether of such self-governing colonies as Canada. She could unite all those dependencies and possessions and say if have got to defend all those and therefore we shall cause a duty of from 2 to 5 per cent, ad vatorcua to be levied on all imports into that area." The duty should be levied on everything in the shape of imports from foreign countries, and the money received should go towards a special fund to be used fpr the naval defence of the empire. The effect of that would be that a certain amount of taxation would be derived from the duties on imports but, the dusty being levied at the sense rate on every class of goods and there being no attention paid to the protection of any particular industry, the levying of that uniform rate could not in any way be called a Protective duty. That could he done, and would Prob- ably satisfy all the political parties. It would simply transfer taxation which was now borne directly to imports, and the internal taxation would he dminished. The duty would be levied on the self- governing colonies and they wonld be ' treated in that respect as foreign countries. It was, how- ever, quite possible that the mother country might say to the self-governing colonies," If you pingo, you can come into this ring provided you will allow all importations into your territory to be taxed to the same extent and pay the promseds of thet dury into out Exchequer so that we may have some remuneration for the burden of defending you." In that way it seemed to him that all political parties could agree upon a common system of taxation which would first be applied to the imperial nucleus and the United Kingdom and ultimately. to the whole Empire in order that Great Britain might be released from the burden under which she at present staggered of defending her enormous territory. Of course," Free Trade within the Empire " was the goal they should strive to attain. if we made any change at all we should take care that it was one to bring us nearer to that goal and prevent any possible increase of the present duties which were in all conscience high enough In any of the colonies, lie disclaimed any</p>
        <p>desire to depreciate or appreciate any of the political parties of this country. Individually he was against extreme Protection and he was against unrestricted importatidn. All of them should have as their first object the good of the whole country. (Applause.) Mr. P. MONKS considered Mr. Nlacfarlane's address a contribution to the fiscal controversy. Ile could see gentlemen in that room who would not agree with all that had been said but in this country we were all supposed to recognize the liberty to differ one from another. So far as he was concernel he was not a party man now, although at one time he was a very strong Radical. He was as great a Liberal as ever he was but he quite agreed that the question had not been treated by the political parties with that fairness to which it was entitled. He thought that for a man to blindly shut his eyes and say that a policy that did for his father sixty years ago would do for him, was going back to the old Tory principles which they younger Radicals tried their best to upset. Whatever opinions they might hold with reference to Mr. Chamberlain and his policy, he deserved that oonsideration which all fair men were willing to give when he had considered the matter from a national standpoint and when his expressions of opinion were the convictions of his own mind as to the necessity for a change of policy. He thought that for one party to taboo that and accuse Mr. Chamberlain of all sorts of improper and unnational motives was not fair treatment of the question. He (Mr. Monks) was certain the ques- tion was sure to give rise to a heated discussion before many years were over and that it cyanid have to he settledhe was quite satisfied, because we were already feeling the pinch of poverty. There was, he believed, a great deal of want of employment owing to the unrestricted imports which were affecting our industrial position. During the last five years at his own works they have been working on an average not more than four and a half days a weekiand the loss to their workmen had been very considerable. He was silly enough, if they liked to call it that, to believe that if by speeding a shilling a rain could earn fifteen it was his duty to spend the shilling. The Rev. JOSEPH HOWARD, who has lived in Canada, supported the views advanced by Mr.l Macfarlane, re- marking that the subject was of tremendous inportance to our future as a country. The time might come —and we snould all vastly deplore it—when we might find ourselves at the feet of Canada pleading for our own interests and for our very existence as a nation among the continental nations. We did riot wish that to he. Mr. E. L. Ikr,ot ENG- said he wished a wall could be put ronnd the whole of the colonies, end a 2 to 5 per I cent, duty imposed on imports, so that we might use it as a weapon against highly protected nations that would , not allow English goods to go into their ciluntries, and in order to help colonies like South Africa that might be inundated with materials from the United States. The tariffs of America were doing great injury to us as a nation.</p>
        <p>The CIIAIRNI tN moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Macfarlane, and said one lesson they could draw horn the address was that the time had come for the Govern- ment to inaugurate a system whereby accurate informa- tion with regard to the colonies should be supplied. The question of employment might very soon b 3 settled when our people knew where they could find good work and receive good wages. Alderman BENVETT seconded, and said the one great cause for regret with reference to the fiscal coat oversy was the way in which it had been made a Dtrty ques- tion. Ile had felt for many years ta at a schem some- what on the lines Mr. Macfarlane had proposed would be the simplest solution of the ditliculty. In acknowledging the vote of thanks Mr. MACFARLANE said they in Canada felt very much indebted to Mr. Chamberlain because although they did not approve of the actual measures he had pronosed, still he was the first and the only British statesman who had brought the subject before the country in such a way as to lead to its settlement—and it must be discussed and settled.</p>
        <p>On the motion of Rt. MACRA.REANE seconded by Mr. A. J. Jolley, a vote of thanks was accorded to the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BRIDGE.</p>
        <p>Sir John Nares Cannot Re-consider His Decision.</p>
        <p>PROBABLE NEW SCHEME.</p>
        <p>The question of widening Warrington Bridge is once more at a standstill. At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee on Monday, Alderman Smethurst reported that a deputation from the Corporation had waited upon Sir George Nares, the acting conservator of the Mersey, on the previous Fridayat Liverpool,with referenee to his refusal to advise the Mersey Conservancy ' to approve of the plans for the proposed widening of Warrington Bridge, and that the views of the Corpora- tion were placed before Sir George who explained that from his knowledge of the river and his personal inspec- tion of the bridge ho was unable to re-consider his decision.</p>
        <p>The deputation, we understand, consistered of the Mayor, Alderman Smethurst, Councillors Wilkinson, Lord aud Benson, the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyou Whittle) and the Borough Surveyor (Mr. Thomas Longdin). Speeches were made pointing out the great necessity for widening the bridge to cope with the increasing traffic, and the cantilever scheme was strongly advo- cated as the best method of meeting the difficulty. Sir George Nares, however, appeared to consider this side of the question as being out of his province, the Only point which influenced his judgment being the impeding of navigation. As stated above, Sir George said he had personally inspected the bridge and was satisfied that navigation would be interfered with. We understand that Sir George intimated his willing- ness to consider a modified scheme, and it is the inten- tion of the advocates of economy to bring forward another proposal which, while meeting with Sir George's approval, will oh date the erection of an entirely new structure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Business of Next Week's Meeting.</p>
        <p>The September meeting of the To am Council will be held on Tuesday nsxt at the Town Hill, when the following among other business will be transacted :</p>
        <p>- - THE DPSPOSAL OF LICENSE 9.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Finance Committee, the Town Clerk submitted correspondence which had taken place between himself and the Clerk to the Licensing Com- mittee in reference to the disposal of the licences of the Baths and Queen's Hotels.</p>
        <p>GARDEN' PLOTS AT _HOWLEY.</p>
        <p>The Borough Treasurer read a letter frorn Mr. Hafry Fairclough on behalf of several residents in Howley asking for p.lrnaission to lay out portions of Wharf Meadow in garden plots, the consideration of which was deferred. PurtellA.SES" IN KING-STREET.</p>
        <p>The T,lwn Clerk reourtel that h? had purchased premises numbered 30, 32 And 34, King-street, and the plot of land adjoining for £3lO, which was approved.</p>
        <p>THE SEWER FA:TA.LITY.</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk reported thit sttkftory arrange- ments had heea nude with the Fi*th Mittress Ca.. Ltd., and the Insurance Co. for the plyment of a earn or money to the widow of James Baxter.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>NEW SUR-LIM/ARUN.</head>
        <p>NEW SUR-LIM/ARUN.</p>
        <p>The Museum Committee has resolved that Mr. John McAdam be appointed Sub-librarian at the salary of 230 a year, rising £5 yearly to £129, a month's notice to terminate the engagement.</p>
        <p>UCTIoN- SALES I THE MARKET.</p>
        <p>The Mukets Committee after t:e:Co—a—iirie. ring their resolution re the applicatian of nessrs. Ellis Vane and Co., resolved that Messrs, Ellis Vane and Co., are quite satisfied with the accommodation allotted to them the Committee do not see their way to take any further action in the matter. STOCKTON' HEATH POTATO</p>
        <p>The Town Clerk repo,tei t'oat aacording te the instructions of the committee, he had laid a case before Mr. F. N. Ke3n for his opinion as to whether the Cor- poration ware justified in taking proceedings to stop the invasion of their market rights by the establishment of a new market at Stockton Heath, and that Mr. Keen had advised that such proceedings be taken. Resolved. that the Town Clerk be authorised to institute pro- ceedings in order to proteet the rights of this Corporation. THE QUICK-HITCHING HARNE3S. At a meeting of the Fire Engine Committee the Town Clerk reported that the resolution with relation to the acceptance of the tender of Mr. George Venn for quick-hitching harness at the price of ,ClB 10s. had not been confirmed by the Council, whereupon it was explained that the Borough Surveyor, acting on the assumption that the purchase of the harness was urgent and the tender twine''s been accepted by the committee, had given the order to Mr. Venn, and that the harness had been delivered. Resolved that the Council be requested to confirm the same. AREITIATION ON THE D &amp;NE CONTE. WT.</p>
        <p>At a Wafer Committee meeting the Town Clerk re- ported that he had had an interview with Mr. James Lawson with reference this claim aglinst the Cor- poration in connection with the Delph-lane contract, and that while having settled many of the items, it would be necessary to proceed to Arbitration on some, and that he had agreed with Mr. Lawson that the refer- ence should be a friendly one seas to minimise the cost as much as possible. Resolved, that the action of the Town Clerk be approvel,aud the choice of an Arbitrator left in his hands.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR PEMBERTON AND THE WA.PER COMMITTEE. , riocum EATS PRODUCED.</p>
        <p>The Water Engineer laid before a meeting of the committee on 21st August the contract, plans, bills, accounts, correspondence, and papers in connection with Mr. Lawson's contract, and the committee requested Mr. Councillor Pemberton to give a list of the detais which he required, and which would be furnished to him by the engineer. Mr. Councillor Pemberton de- clined to give this information, and afte- further dis- custion it was resolved, that the documents and papers he deposited in the Deputy Town Clerk's officetandthat Mr. Councillor Pemberton be allowed to examine them and to make extracts therefrom providing the same are shown to the 111ayor and Chairman, and, that Mr. Councillor Pemberton's solicitor ba allot,: to accom, pany him. At a further meeting on the 23th August, it was resolved that in Air. Councillor Pemberton' s examination of the papers in connection with Lawson's contract he be allowed to be accompanied by any member of the Council provided that such member) if not a member of the Water Committee) does not have access to any of the papers concerned. SPECIAL MOTIONS. Councillor Stott will move that the following motions become a Standing Order of the Council No able bodied man employed under the Corporation and having reached twenty-one years of age, shall be paid 1 less than at the rate of twenty-four shillings per week. 2, The foregoing Standing Order shall become operative on the first day in October neat. _ .</p>
        <p>Councillor Pemberton will move : That a committee be appointed by this Council forthwith to investigate and report to the Council upon all the accounts and transactions between the Corporation and Ir. Lawson i n connection with his contract at Delph-lane.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>GROSFIELD'S ANGLERS' ASSOCIATION</head>
        <p>GROSFIELD'S ANGLERS' ASSOCIATION</p>
        <p>Annnal Competition.</p>
        <p>To the frivolous man fishing is no doubt a sad plea- sure—indeed for him the angle is buts thing with a fool at one end and a fly at the other and the angler but an institution for replenishing the world's stock of hilarity. But we laymen are careful when and where to laugh, for your true disciple of Isaac Walton is very serious ; likewise he is quick to anger and very sudden. We remember he is only unconsciously a humorist, his tales are not meant to be funny. Besides, what mind could be so vacant as not to be impressed by the sight of some sixty-seven men perched like the proverbial nine hundred and ninety-nine crows in a row, but not of course on a rail, sixty-seven backs at the angle of repose and sixty- seven pairs of eyes apparently absorbed like Narcissus in the contemplation of their own images. There is something almost sacramental in the solemnity of such a spectacle ; one al meat uncovers. The boldest heart is quailed ; the laugh dies on the loosest lips. One feels in the presence ot superior beings in whose solemn joys we common mortals can have eo share. Yet this was the spectacle that presented itself to one who on Saturday afternoon last between the hours of three and five followed the quiet path by the canal at Thelwall. Under the calm surface of the scene there was, however, a silent but strained excitement. One noted, too, a certain orderly and subdued bustle and when one had r sufficiently recovered from one's first feeling of awe to ask, one was told that this was the occasion of the annual competition in connection with Crosfields' Anglers' Association It was hard to believe so much vigour could be in so young an instinution, yet one was told this was but the second event of the kind. The day was very fine and consequently the sport was poor. Bites were rare and the fish were mostly of small size. But " hope springs sternal in the human breast "; for there is always the unexpected to expect, if one may , use an Irishisrn. and your angler is no craven ; so towards the end the excitement reached the point of perspiration, as well it might seeing that a single dram I might turn the day. At last the sand had run out end Mr. Newell, whose onerous duty it was to Neigh in," was able to announce the prize list as follows :-Ist. T. Clare: 2nd. W. Hudson; 3rd, J. Holden, 4th,A. Overend; sth, John Payne; 6th, S. Emery; 7th. S. Hankinson ; Bth, T. Fleming and .T. Critchley ; 10th, W. Kirkham ; 11th, R.Lawless and J. Tullenberg; 13th, W. Hougliton ; 14th, E. Pownall. It will be observed that the weights are not given: we do not wish to play into the hands of the irreverent, but it was a pleasure to be assured professionally that the catch was, considering the stringency of the Association's Rule, a very satisfac- tory one. It was a pleasure, too, to meet so many fishing worthies. Amongst these we noted Mr. P. Knight, the veteran Secretary of the parent Association, who was assisted on this occasion by Mr. S. hart and by the Secretary of Crosfields' Association, Mr. Atkin- son.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>MR. T. S. STEEL'S MARRIAGE.</head>
        <p>MR. T. S. STEEL'S MARRIAGE.</p>
        <p>Presentation From St. Peter's Church Men's Class.</p>
        <p>Mr. Thomas S. Steel was on Sunday afternoon the recipient of a beautiful silver-mounted smoker's cabinet subscribed for by the members of the St. Peter's men's service as a mark of esteem on the occasion of his marriage. The presentation was made in St. Peter's Church on Sunday afternoon. The Rev. D. R. JNNIES (curate) said Mr. Steel, during the three years he (the speaker) had worked in the parish, and for a considerable time prior to his coming to Warrington, had taken a deep interest in their men's services, and in all things appertaining to the welfare of St. Peter's Church, of which he was an officer. He had assisted them in many ways, and having taken to himself a wife they had thought it a fitting opportunity of showing some mark of their appreciation of his services. Although the members were all working, men, he as sure it would be very gratifying to ft.. Steel to know that when that presentation was first suggested, it was taken up with great enthusiasm. They trusted that now he was married he would nut desert them, but would continue to take as deep an interest in them RA he had done in the past. They most sincerely wished Mr. Steel and his bride long life and much happiness. (Applause.) Mr. J. J. Heins, the oldest member of the men's service, in making the presentation, said nothing had given them greater pleasure than to have that oppor- tunity of showing in some small degree the great esteem and respect they had for Mr. Steel.. The gift was quite spontaneous, and everyone had shown a great willingness to subscribe, because they felt they were honouring one to whom honour was due. Mr. Steel had gained the esteem and respect of every member, and they felt they were deeply indebted to him for the kindly interest he had taken in them and in their services. (Applause.) Mr. STEEL, in reply, said he could assure them he appreciated their kindness more than words could express. If anything were needed to remind him of his happy connection with St. Peter's, that elaborate pre- sent, which he had great pleasure in accepting, would serve as such reminder. The words which had been used towards him were too kind, and he felt he was not deserving of one half of what had been said. As they knew, he had taken a great interest in men's services generally, and particularly in that held each Sunday afternoon in St. Peter's Church. He believed in men's services, and his interest in them would not diminished. He felt very happy amongst them, and he hoped that gathering that afternoon would tend to cement still more the good feeling that had always existed between them. • _ _</p>
        <p>The cabinet which had been supplied by Messrs. Eustanco and Co., of Sankey-street, was beautifully mounted, and bore a silver plate with the inscription : Presented to Mr. T. S. Steel, on his marriage, by the members of St. Peter's Men's Service. August 9th, 1905."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>THE.LIGHTING OF THE VILLAGE.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Stockton H;;;;P—a.rochial Committee was held at the Hall Cafe on Thursday evening when there were present :—Messrs. G. Pilling (in the cha r ),Pearson,liallows, Pai rhurst,Mcln tyre,T. R. Westbrook, Warburton, Bouchier, Clarke, H. J. West- brook, G. F. Welsby, G. F. Ashton (clerk) and J. Swinton (surveyor).</p>
        <p>THE PRESSURE OF WATER. A letter was read from the Warrington Corporation stating that the request of the Parochial Committee with regard to increasing the pressure of water in the mains, had been laid before the Water Committee. The comm ttee was prepared tolincrease the pressure in certain hydrants in case of fire only. The committee reserved to itself the right to withdraw the pressure at any time it desired to do (so. Mr. firfclntyfe : They are entirely useless as they are at present. The Surveyor was instructed to meet Mr. Deas with regard to the matter.</p>
        <p>THE 1./OUTING OF THE VILLAGE. Mr. Bouchier reported that the Parith Council had met the Town Clerk and members of the Warrington , Corporation to consider the question of lighting dangerous electric standards from Victoria-square to Ellesmere-road. They also considered the question of fixing lamps at present close to the standards to the standards themselves. A report would be presented later. The Town Clerk had also promised to supply the Parochial Committee with a statement as to the cost of keeping the lams in Stockton Heath lighted throughout the night, as compared with the cost of extinguishing them, as at present, at midnight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON,</head>
        <p>WEEKLY REPORT ON THE HEALTH OF WARRINGTON,</p>
        <p>WEEK ENDING AUGUST 2Sth, 1905.</p>
        <p>Daring the week, the following births and deaths were registered :—Warrington Registration District births, 43, deaths, 24. The excess of births over deaths was 24.</p>
        <p>The birth-rate was equal to 36..0 The death-rate from all causes was equal to 1.8•0 per 1,000 against 206 the average o f the previous 4 weeks and against 198 the corresponding period of last year. The death rate from the se yen ch ie f zyrnotic diseases was equal to 5•2 per 1,009, against 6-9 the average rate of the previous 4 weeks and against 99 the corresponding period of 1901. The ages of mortality were:- 0-1 1-5 3-15 13-25 23-55 63—up. Total 8 6 1 8 1 24. The deaths included-1 from measles,6 from diarrhoea. 2 from phthisis, 1 from other tubercular diseases, 1 lung diseases, 1 frora heart disease, 1 from accidents, 11 from all other causes.</p>
        <p>The following cases of infectious diseases have been reported during the week :—Scarlet fever,2 cases. Total number of patients in hospital, week ending 2Gth August, 1905, Newton 9, Warrington 11, total 20. METEOROLOGY.—Themean temperature was 58. Fah and therefore 2 deg. lower than the preceding week The maximum temperature 70F., was recorded on the 26th inst., and the minimum tempsrature 49F., on the 26th inst.</p>
        <p>The rainfall measured 1.10 inch. The general direction was S.W. _</p>
        <p>J. GUEST GORNALL, Medical Officer of Health.</p>
      </div>
