      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>Liable to a £2,000 Fine.</head>
        <p>Liable to a £2,000 Fine.</p>
        <p>Thomas Lyon, a St. Helens youth, was summoned at Newton-le-Willows. on Thursday for exposing 3501bs of unsound fish for sale at Earlestos-n Market on July 28t1i.—Mr. E. A. White (Warrington), who prosecuted, said it was the practice of some dealers to hand their bad stock over to others to take to small towns to) dispose of it in order to avoid being p1a0.4 in an awkward position. If the fish escaped thet notice of the sanitary officer and was sold the stock was paid for, if seized it was not paid for.---The Chairman of tin Bench stated that defendant had rendered himself liable to a fine of £2,000-20 for each of the hundred fish—but would be mulcted in. 40s. and costs and advocate's and medical officer's fees. He hoped the man who sold the fish to defen— dant would pay the expenses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>WORD TO THE WISE.</head>
        <p>A WORD TO THE WISE.</p>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>BASS INGRATITUDE.</p>
        <p>- Thomas Broderick and Patrick 'Richard Moran, described by Superintendent Monghan as notorious loafets, appeared in the dock at the Warrington Police Court on Stturday, charged with stealing from the dwelling house of William Norbury, Dolman's-lane, an iron bedstead, two tables, and various other articles of the value of 20s.—Superintendent Monaghan said that on Monday the prosecutor W. 13 removing from Dolman's- lane and decided to spend the night with a friend who lived in Elizabeth - street. He, however, allocsed the prisoners to sleep in his ho ise and they remained there forseveral days. On going to the house on Thursdly Nor':airy discovered that nearly all the furniture was gone, and it was after- wards ascertained that prisoners had been selling it and giving it away. They got drunk on the money realized. —lt was stated that Broderick had been before the Court five times, while Moran made his 26th appearance. —Prisoners were sentenced to three months' imprison- ment.</p>
        <p>SCORCHING RIINCoRN CYCLIST. At the Runcorn Petty Sessions. on Monday, before Messrs. F. J. Norman (presiding) and John Littler, Benjamin Leach. manager to the firm of " Black and White," teamen, High-street, Runcorn, was charged with furiously riding a bicycle in High-street on Friday. —He pleaded not guilty.—Police-constable Hulse said he saw the defendant come over Savages' bridge on a bicycle at a most terrific pace. A railway van stood by the Post ()Mee, and he had a narrow escape of running into it. He steered away from it and his machine swerved yards. He went down High-street at a great speed. Three tradesmen came to witness and complained. They said that if the defendant was not stopped the police would "have to fetch the ambulance for him one of these mornings." Witness followed defendant down to his place of business, and defendant there told him that he had been warned before for reckless riding. He said that he did not call it reckless riding, however, if it were not more than twenty miles an hour. Witness told him that for his own sake and for the public safety he would report the tuatter.—Defendant said he had two powerful brakes on his machine, and could pull up at once in case of emergency. He was not furiously riding on the [morning in question, but pleasantly free wheeling. Efe had his hands on the brakes ready to apply them if they were needed. He was keeping strictly to the right side of the road for a cyclist. The machine was absolutely under his control. The speed was not more than fourteen miles an hour at the outside. —The Chairman said that the Bench thought that the defendant had given his case away completely. If he had been warned by people he should have been more careful. The costs were lie. Cd.,and the Bench thought that if they imposed a fine of 155., including the costs, they would hare taken a lenient view. ALLEGED BRASS THEFTS.—WARRINGTON MARINE STORE DEALER SENT TO THE SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>At Widnes on Monday, Peter Grady, a young man Erin,'' in Margaret-street, was brought up on remand charged with stealing one patent brass lubricator, brass valve, and 621 b. of brass, value £lO 12s.' the property of the United Alkali Company (Golding Davis Works), on Friday, the 18th inst.—Joseph Sutton, marine-store dealer, Turner's-yard, Warrington, was charged with receiving the same, knowing it to have been stolen.— Superintendent Strickland stated that owing to many complaints of these thefts he instructed Police-con- stable Howarth to watch Warrington-bound rag- gatherers. About five o'clock he stopped Sutton with his cart, and upon examination the brass produced was found hidden amongst the rags. Sutton was brought to the Police Station, and from a description given of the man from whom he bought it. Peter Grady was appre- hended later on.—Thomas Osborne, foreman titter at the works, identified the brass as the property of his employers, and its worth was £lO 12s.— Michael Gri ffen, 9.,,,'ed fourteen, Turner's-yard,Warring- ton, said he had been three weeks in Sutton's employ gathering rags. They came to Widnes on the day in question, and were about eleven o'clock in the vicinity where Grady lives. Shortly afterwards Sutton took his cart to the Corporation tip in Moor-lane.and Grady followed immediately. The latter asked Sutton if he would buy some brass, and told him to bring his cart to a field near by, where Grady unearthed the brass and placed it upon the cart. The price asked was 7g. Cl., but only is. was given.— Ebenezer Pitchford (clerk), in the employ of Winstan- ley, Warrington,where a portion of the brass was found, stated that Sutton had been down to the shop on Jnly 22, when he brought the brass valve and other brass. He paid him 4d. per lb.—The Chairman (Aldeiman Waring) said the bench wished to state that they con- sidered witness's people to blame for not asking where the brass came from. He would be getting himself into trouble if he bought material like this for old brass. It was actually offering an inducement to this class of people to steal brass.—Prisoners were ordered to take their trial at the next Liverpool quarter sessions. THEFT FROM A WARRINGTON 110 TEL. At the Warrington Police Court on Tuesday William Pailing, Bank-street, was charged with stealing six metal prongs value 55., the property of John Lee, !land- lord of the Lion Hotel.—Superintendent Monaghan stated that at six o'clock on Monday morning prisoner called at the Lion Hotel Vaults and was there engaged by the manager to do some cleaning. After he left the forks were missed. Prisoner was later arrested by P.C. Bennett and the prongs found in his possession. Atone time prisoner held a respectable position but he had latterly given way to drink.—Prisoner was fined 10s and costs or in default 11 days' imprisonment with hard labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Warrington Borough Pollee Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Pollee Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY. Before Dr. 14/ox (Presiding) and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes DRUNK. IN CHARGE.—James Dempsey, 6. Ashton. street, was charged with having been drunk in charge of a horse and carriage in Haydock-street on Monday night.—P.C. Rylatt said he received information that he was wanted at 0, Ashton-street. He went there, and saw the prisoner's father bleeding from the face. It was at once bandaged up. and the injured man taken to the Infirmary. The prisoner who was drunk was in charge of a horse and landau, and the &amp;neer told him to leave tho box, but he behaved in a very disorderly manner. He had previously assaulted his father.— A fine of 10:. and costs (13s. 6d. in all) was imposed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>..,--,.;,;,.'.t....,4 ~,7,.::....,---:....:...--,±.:,;4iiii4'k^.;..f"..t5•,'.....•,•• ',.-7,4:,,,t1:..,„.1:,,.,t,;!..::,e.,,,:....,,:-,,,,...---.... --- –</head>
        <p>..,--,.;,;,.'.t....,4 ~,7,.::....,---:....:...--,±.:,;4iiii4'k^.;..f"..t5•,'.....•,•• ',.-7,4:,,,t1:..,„.1:,,.,t,;!..::,e.,,,:....,,:-,,,,...---.... --- -</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday September 2, 1905. MAGAZINE CUTTINGS AND SCIENCE NOTES ANDGLEANINGS. REVIEWS.</p>
        <p>all equal. No one had suggested that the appoint- ment was in any way intended to disparage Miss Warburton. No one who knew her would desire to say anything that woul hurt her feelings, hut the members of the School Management Committee did not attend the meetings for the purpose of satisfying any particular personal feeling but with the idea of securing the most efficient discharge of the duties. What the opinion of Mr. Denninn had to do with a matter of that kind he (Mr. Jolley) was entirely at a loss to guess. Mr. Plinston also quoted Mr. Gow in support of Miss Warburton, bct he (Mr.Jolley) could only say that Mr. Gow distinctly expressed the opinion that if an office of that kind were established, Miss Frost was the person to fill it. The present headmaster was also in fawn'. of Miss Frost. The Rev. John Yonge, who at the last meeting was inclined to favour Miss Warburton. after attending the School Manage- ment Committee came round and admitted that it was not desirable to appoint Miss Warburton. (Mr.Plinaton: "No, no.") Mr. Plinston was not present but he (Mr. Jolley) was in the chair and knew • what Mr. Yonge said. He also knew Mr. Yonge sufficiently well to say with great confidence that he was not in the habit of saying what he did not mean. He, however, distinctly and emphatically said to him ( Mr. Jolley) that Miss Warburton's claims were out of the question. Mr. PLINSTON : I rise to a point of order and I say at once that the Rev. John Yonge has seen me on the matter. I have mentioned his name to night with his permission and he is not in agreement with the action of the sub-committee. I will not have Mr. Jolley throwing at me assertions which are incorrect. He has charged me many a time with making statements which are wrong but I have not the right of speaking after him or I would do so. Mr. JOLLEY : I should be extremely sorry to accuse Mr. Plinston or anyone else of making inaccurate state- ments if I could not prove what I was saying. Continuing he said that perhaps it would satisfy the more reasonable members of the committee if he assured them there was a strong meeting of the School Management Corn- mittee. The circumstances attending the appointment and the work of the various, female officers were sub- mitted at great length and in a confidential way to the members and at the conclusion of the discussion the minute was passed without dissent. The Rev. John Yonge did not dissent from it. Mr. G. WALKER said that Mr. Jolley with his ability was nearly able to make them believe that black was white. It was quite true that under that authority none of the three teachers could claba seniority of service, but Miss Warburton had also served the town under the old authority. That afternoon he (Mr. Walker) saw the Rev. John Yonge as he was going to Buxton, and Mr. Yonge said " I fought against that appointment in sub- committee, and was single-handed. I was the only one who fought strenuously against its adoption. If I bad been there to-night I should have spoken equally against it." Mr. C. E. PARKER said it was awkward to discuss the action of a man who was not present, but what Mr. Jolley had said about Mr. Yonge was perfectly correct. The committee heard a very strong pronouncement by one of their officers on the merits of the two ladies. That pronouncement was so very strong that the com- mittee had acted in the right way that after hearing it Mr. Yonge said "After hearing that I can say nothing further.' He,however,made a proviso that the appoint- ment might be put upon the opinion of the officers rather than of the committee, but he (Mr. Parker) immediately said that was hardly fair and the com- mittee must certainly take the responsibility. Mr. Yonge agreed with that, hut he seemed to think it should be mentioned that the committee had been guided by the officers' opinion. There was no reflection whatever on the ladies' educational ability but the evidence before the committee was that the lady they had appointed was decidedly the better one for the position. The MAYOR said this matter served to show that the Education Committee was composed of too many co-opted members. If they had more Town Council representatives they would have done the business in such a way that it would not have necessitated two or three discussions. (Laughter.) The CHAIRMAN said the appointment was made simply on the matter of personal qualifications for the duties. The committee thought they were acting in the best interests of the purpose for which they were making the appointment. Councillor EVANS : What about the statement of Mr Parker that the official guided the committee. The CHAIRMAN : Quite obviously in a case of that kind the official knows more about it than anyone else. Councillor WILKINSON : May I ask who the official was that guided the com.nittee ? Mr. JOLLEY : Officials. Councillor WILKINSON: How many officials have you who knew anything of the abilities of the ladies previous to the authority being formed ? You as a weak-kneed committee say you hare left yourselves to an official. The CHAIRMAN remarked, that the Committee had gone in the matter very carefully, and it was wide of the mark to go into it again and make accusations against the Committee. Councillor WILKINSON : I am not accusing them. After the Committe have failed they appeal to an official.</p>
        <p>Mr. JOLLY : That is not true. - Mr. PARKER said Mr. Wilkinson bad misinterpreted his remands. Naturally the Committee were somewhat guided by the officials, but their opinion was formed on the general aspect of the question all round. Councillor BURTON thought it was a rainful thing to the ladies that the discussion should have taken place in public. The CHAIRMAN: They have only their friends to thank for that. Councillor WILKINSON: That is a funny way of putting it. I don't know the ladies. The Cif tIRNIAN : Well, their advocates. Councillor BURTON said that what had been stated had been stated with the desire that justice should be done to the ladles. It would have been more becoming if the matter could have been thrashed out in com- mittee. Councillor FULTON SMITH, replying on the discussion said the criticism was not fair because it was not based on actual facts,lant on hypotheses. It simply amounted to fault finding with the committee, nothing more or less. The standing and reputation of the ladies WAS in no way affected by the appointment. Miss Frost was not put over the other teachers in any way. If the appointment were mit made the committee would be faced with the responsibility of providing a girls' techaical sch4ol, as the Warrington school was almost the only one in the country where boys and girls were taught together. Surely the Committee had given sufficient time to consider the matter. The tendency apparently among many members was to over- rule what had been done after the greatest patience and labour by the sub-committees. The appointment bad been made in the best interests of the school, and with- out injnstice to any teacher. He thought it unfair that recommendations should be thrown ruthlessly aside because the Committee could not gi‘e every petty detail of all that happened in committee. The CHAIRMAN observed that the discussion was very much to be deprecated, as the subject had already been thrashed out sufficiently, and the discussion was calculated to create an impression in the public mind which did not exist. There was absolutely no reflection on the ladies. and it was purely a matter of qualification for the duties involved. The Committee had a high opinion of all the teachers. On a vote being taken, the figures for and against the amendment were announced to be equal. The Chairman , gave his casting vote in favour of the committee's I recommendation. Some exception was taken to the fact that the Mayor ' recorded his vote just sthe result was being announced, I but the Chairman pointed out that even if the Mayor's vote were not included, the result would have been the I the same, as he (the Chairman) would have given his I vote and casting vote for the recommendation.</p>
        <p>a substitute for terracotta and plaster, is a mixture of 45 parts of calcined gypsum, 30 of Water, 10 of albumen, 9 of alum, 4 of sulphate of magnesia, and 2 of borax. wlt is stronger an 4 lighter than terra-cotLa or plaster.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>NEW SCHOOL KII:ENDANCE INSPECTOR.</head>
        <p>NEW SCHOOL KII:ENDANCE INSPECTOR.</p>
        <p>The School Attendance Committee's minutes which were approved without comment, contained a recom- mendation that Mr. George A. Donaldson be appointed assistant school attendance inspector at a commencing salary of £C:S per annum, rising by annual increments of £6 10s. to a maximum of £9l.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>NEW SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>THE NEW SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>The Sites and Buildings Committee recommended that the tender of Mr. Harry Fairclough, amounting to £lB5, for the extension of the lavatory accommodation at the Secondary school, be accepte4 ; also that the " Bolton " Council school be lighted by means of elec- tricity and that the Borough Electrical Engineer be requested to supervise the work of lighting the "-• Bearnont " and Bolton Council school. I The MAYOR moved and Mr. WALKER -seconded the ' confirmation of the minutes. Councillor ELLISON asked for information as to when the erection of the Bolton school was likely to be , commenced. I The MA.yon said he wished he was in the happy position of being able to give the information. Mr. , Murray was doing all he could to get the specifications land quantities drawn up, but they bad not arrived yet. The committee had particulars respecting the Beamont ; school but they were only in the embryo stage. The CHAIRMAN said be knew the Mayor was most ' anxious to accelerate the work and at the last meeting of the Committee he gave very strong expression to his views. Everything that could be done to get the ;schools in process of erection was being done. 'I he minutes were approved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>– – THE LATE MR. WILLIAM JEFFERY.</head>
        <p>- - THE LATE MR. WILLIAM JEFFERY.</p>
        <p>-- - At a meeting of the General Purpose Committee the announcement was received with regret of the death of Mr. William Jeffery, a representative manager of the National Schools. It was decided to recommend Mr. T. P. Winskill as a representative manager in place of the late Mr. Jeffery. The minutes were adopted.</p>
        <p>The funeral took place on Saturday of the town clerk of Exeter, Mr. George Roberts Shorto, who from being a private soldier, had risen to be a foremost authority on English municipal law.</p>
        <p>Blackburn ratepayers learned. on Saturday that the tramways department showed a net profit of £BOO on the past quarter, as compared with a loss of £4OO on the corresponding three months last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>CAPTAIN BYNG'S MIDSHIPMAN.</head>
        <p>CAPTAIN BYNG'S MIDSHIPMAN.</p>
        <p>Have you ever heard tell, by the way, of Captain Byng's midshipman? I forget his name, but he started his first night aboard ship by kneeling down and saying his pravers,as his mother had taught him, and the crowd of his iellow-midshipmen found it against the custom of the service and gave him the strap for it. This, however, raised him up a champion in one of the taller lads, who protested that their conduct was tyrannous : "and," said he very generously, "to-morrow night I too propose to say my prayers. If anyone object, be may fight me." Thus being a handy lad with his fists' he established the right of religious liberty on board. By and by one or two of the better disposed midshipmen followed his example: by degrees the custom spread along the lower deck, where the dispute had happened in full view of the whole ship's company, seamen and marines; and by the time she reached her port of Halifax she hadn't a man on board (outside the ward- room) but said his prayers regularly. At Halifax Captain Byng took aboard out of hospital another small midship- man, who on his first night no sooner climbed into his hammock than the entire mess bundled him out of it. "We would have you to know, young man," said they, 'that private devotion is the rule on board our ship. It's down on you knees this minute or you get the strap."—From "Sir John Constantine," in the Corn- hill Magazine " for September.</p>
        <p>MODELS FOR SCULPTORS. The sculptor who deploreshis inability to And worthy models for his art is advised to take a walk down the towpath during Henley Regatta. Unless he is singularly blind or singularly unlucky, he should not have far to go before he meets the young Greek god in the life. To a perhaps too enthusiastic fellow countryman it is doubtful whether any age or any nation could show more beautiful or more refined types of the youthful male than these young oarsmen of Eaton,of Oxford, and of Cambridge, with their clear-cut features and slender but well-knit frames.—" The Academy."</p>
        <p>IRON MOUNTAINS. To a fall from the sky the Eskimos of Greenland for generations owed the iron supply they used for knives and harpoon-heads. Sir John Ross, in 1818, discovered a tribe living in the inhospitable region of Cape York, and. 'what "as more remarkable, found them to be in possession of bone implements with cutting edges of iron, a metal unknown in Greenland. The Eskimos told him that the metal was obtained from certain mysterious " iron mountains." pose searched for the mountains, as did every subsequent expedition which passed, but for three-quarters of a century their secret was kept. Lieutenant Peary, the Arctic explorer, was the first white man to svt eyes on them, and found them to be, as he had expected, not " mountains," but immense masses of meteoritic iron. On a little island off the eastern coast, a friendly Eskimo guided him to where the three masses lay. The simple natives have a legend of their celestial origin, according to which they were originally an Eskimo woman, with her dog and tent, who had been hurled from heaven for some impropriety, and fell in that region. The largest mass, the "tent," measuriag as it does twelve feet by six feet. is the largest meteorite in the world and weighs some ninety tons. It was brought home by Peary to America,where the three" iron mountains" have now been transplanted. All three bear marks of scorings where the Eskimos have beaten off the soft iron with stones, to use for their harpoons and knives, and they are evidently of common origin. No doubt is entertained of their meteoritic character, and they must be of great antiquity.—From an interesting article entitled " Things that Fall from the Sky" which appears in the September ." Windsor Magazine," a notably fine issue, including contributions by H. Rider Haggard, Robert Barr, Justus Miles Forman, W. A. M. Goode, H. C. Bailey anl Eden Phillnotts. No fewer than twenty of Albert Moore's pictures are finely repro- duced with an appreciation of that artist's work. and Ernest Thompson Seton begins anew series of articles on " Woodcraft." Many other articles of interest are remarkably well illustrated, and the number altogether is very full and varied.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>FROM AN ALPINE SUMMIT.</head>
        <p>FROM AN ALPINE SUMMIT.</p>
        <p>Mr. George D. Abraham, the well-known Alpine climber and photographer, gives the following descrip- tion in the new number of the "Pall Mall Magazine ' of a climb to the summit of the 3E511611, one of the great peaks of the Bernese Oberland : After a sensational jump from he steep upper slope to the lower lip of the crevasse, we tramped across the glacier, and were soon at the foot of the actual peak of the Monch. The wind was roaring in the crags above our heads, and we could see that all the fresh snow had disappeared from these, and higher up a ridge of hard blue ice continued to the actual summit. The rocks were soon passed, and we gained a sharp arete of hard snow that gave access to the steep icy ridge beyond. We were now high up on our mountain, and great care was necessary, for "Old Boreas " held rule that day i n , the upper world. Our position was exciting in the extreme. We were balanced Illondin-like on the sharp crest of the snow-ridge, with some thousands of feet of almost perpendicular snow on either side. The gale blew steadily across from the left, and as I stepped deliberately along the " knife edge' in the steps of 1 those ahead, it was curious to notice how their bodies leant far over the abyss to windward. It appeared as though a sudden lull in the storm would precipitate them into space. Here we munched some dry bread and frozen chicken and drank some sour wine to brace us for the work ahead. Truly it was work indeed, especially -for Christian, who for three hours hacked and hewed with his iceaxe at that gale swept slope. SteadilY we mounted higher and higher up the icy staircase,until the great cornices on our right allowed us to walk on theit frozen crests. When quite close to the top an ominous crack warned us that we were too near the overhanging portion, and Christian almost pulled us off our feet with his sudden jump from the dangerous situation. Meanwhile, the wind bad gradually sunk to a breeze and thick white clouds rolled up from below as from a huge cauldron. We saw enough to convince us that, , the IMOneb is the finest view-point in the Oberland. It is encircled by all the great peaks, and its thirteen thousand odd feet of height enabled us to look over all I its neighbours. Framed in gigantic clouds, we bad peeps of the far-distant Pennine Alps rising beyond the Juagfrau and our old friends Monte Rosa and the Weisshorn seemed almost to dwarf the spiry peak of the Matterhorn some flty miles away.</p>
        <p>. Although not due to the same cause, we came very near repeating the adventure of the well-known guide, who had become" three sheets in the wind" by over- indulgence at the Biiregg. Like us, he groped along by the broken rails:which guard the unwary traveller from walking over on to the glacier (lye hundred feet below. Coming to the end of the rails, he not unnaturally con- cluded that the path continued straight ahead ; but there is a sharp turn, and he disappeared over tbe edge. He must. have fallen at least a hundred feet on to a 4 narrow ledge, but after a night's rest he got up, shook himself, and strolled down to Grindel aid little the worse for his marvellous escape. The ate Sir Leslie Stephen submits the choice of two morals regarding the above : "Firstly, don't get drunk when von have to walk along the edge of an Alpine cliff. Secondly, get drunk if you are likely to fall over it." .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>CANNOT AFFORD TO MARRY. A Bachelor's Real Grievance.</head>
        <p>CANNOT AFFORD TO MARRY. A Bachelor's Real Grievance.</p>
        <p>The man who remarks that he cannot afford to marry is usually afraid of having to give up some net luxury. But here is a genuine grievance from a IN.lanchester bachelor, who states :—" I am 29, and would have married before now, but I have spent a small fortune upon doctors and medicine in search of health. lam under no such disadvantage now, for Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people have given me full health and strength." Mr. R. E. Jones, of 3 Clague-street, Stuart-street Manchester, explained further One doctor frankly told me that I needed blood, and that many men, suffer through this. Doctors and hospitals treated me, and at last an eminent physician.. . .</p>
        <p>"But my illneis appeared to defy the best treatment. I I began to vomit blood. Food would not digest, I I became excessively weak, and my weight decreased rapidly from 11 st. to 8 at. My legs grew so weak and shaky that on going upstairs I used to tumble over. I could sleep only fitfully. "One day, when I could scarcely crawl, I read in a pamphlet of a man who had suffered from bloodlessness until cured by Dr. Williams pink pills. I tried them, and felt greatly relieved even after one box, but while taking a third box a great change was manifest. The vomiting stopped, my appetite began to improve. could sleep better, my legs regained their strength, I could get about, and the improvement in my general health continued daily, until I was able to resume work." That men as well as women become anemic is an un- disputed fact ; in both sexes the complaint becomes aggravated by disregard of early symptoms—such as pale gums and lips, breathlessnesi, languor, and an in- creasing feeling of debility. Dr. Williams' pink pills supply new, rich blood to the starved veins, and impart energy. They have cured also indigestion, palpitation, bile, eczema and skin disorders, rheumatism, sciatica. St. Vitus' dance, paralysis, locornotor ataxy, and ladies' ailments. Price 2s. 9d. a box, or 13s. 9d. for six boxes, of dealers,or from Or. Williams' medicine co., Holborn- viaduct. London. Ask for Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people, and be firm with the shopkeeper.</p>
        <p>market°potatoes in Preston 5 gr Gd. per load-2s. to less than last week. Some dealers even offered their wrealised an n large average s n olVies Only produce as low as 3s. per load, and prices were the lowest for many years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>'A FOOT POUND.</head>
        <p>'A FOOT POUND.</p>
        <p>This temi is often used in modern mechanical Writings, but all readers do not understand its meaning. When it is said, for instance, that 100 foot- pounds of work is done, the meaning is that an effort has been put forth sufficient to raise 100 pounds one foot; 1 pound 100 feet, 2 pounds 50 feet, or any number of pounds to such a height that the product of weight and height equals 100.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WIND PRESSURE GAUGE.</head>
        <p>A WIND PRESSURE GAUGE.</p>
        <p>Since the blowing of a train from their bridge some years ago, the Furness Railway has in- stalled a high pressure gauge, with suitable re- cording apparatus, on the western side of the bridge. 'This not only keeps a constant record of wind pressure, but automatically sounds ' an alarm so long as any gale is strong enough to endanger passing trains.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>HEAT OF THE SUN.</head>
        <p>THE HEAT OF THE SUN.</p>
        <p>It is impossible except by illustration and com- parison to grasp the idea of the heat of the sun. According to calculations based upon the energy of the sun's rays on the earth, the temperature at its surface is computed at about 18,000 deg. Fahren.heit. No artificial temperature of this degree can be created by any known. means. Efforts have been made to collect and utilise the tremendous heat power of the sun, but cloudiness, mist, and darkness have prevented anything like marked success.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>UNCHANGEABLE MOON.</head>
        <p>THE UNCHANGEABLE MOON.</p>
        <p>The old idea that changes are taking place un the moon has been carefully investigated by M. Puiseux, a French astronomer. Going over all records, from the earliest observations to the latest, he concludes that the reality of the sup posed changes has not been proved, and that the varying sensitiveness of the retina to faint objects is sufficient to account for differences seen, while different conditions of exposure might explain all appearances in the photo- graphs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>GROWTH OF FISH.</head>
        <p>THE GROWTH OF FISH.</p>
        <p>The result of the scientific investigations of the Scottish Fishery Board goes to shew that fish do not grow so fast or reach maturity so soon as is commonly supposed. It was found that cod at two years of age measure from 10 to 13 or 14 inches in length; at three years from 17 to 19; and at four years about 27 inches; so that they would spawn as a rule in their fifth year. Plaice from 2i to about 4 inches were one year old, from about 4 to 64 inches they were two years old, while those at three years in ches they up to 12 inches. At 13 or 14 they were mostly four ,years of age, while some in which the lines of growth indicated five years measured 114, 141, and 18i inches, and one measuring 20 inches was found to be four years old.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>REMARKABLE THERMOPILE.</head>
        <p>A REMARKABLE THERMOPILE.</p>
        <p>The invention of Mr. Robert L. Mond, M.A., of Berkeley-square, W., and Mr. M. Wilderman, of Elers-road, South Ealing, is a very remark- able one if the expectations of the inventors are fulfilled, says the Times. They manufacture thermopiles without heating, .'brasing, soldering, or fusing for effecting a junction between the two metals or plates. A compound wire, strip, or plate, consisting of a core of one frietal, and a covering of. another metal, is drawn to the re- quired degree of thinness. Portions of the com- pound metal are then immersed in an acid until the outer metal is removed, and the core is laid bare at the immersed portions of the wire. The alternate compound and single portions of the wire are then wound in grooves on a circular rod of porcelain, ebonite, or glass. Although the thermopile described is for the purpose of measuring the intensity of light, yet the in- ventors say that this invention may be used for measurements of temperature, instead of the Wheatstone bridge and platinum thermometer, and that. it may serve as a pyrometer for the measurement of high temperatures. It may also be employed instead of accumulators on motor- cycles.</p>
        <p>FLAME TEMPERATURE OF WATER-GAS.</p>
        <p>Some tests have been made, according to Le Gaz, to determine the flame temperatures both of water-gas and of constituent gases. For the purpose of the experiments the water-gas was prepared by mixing in equal volumes carbonic) oxide and hydrogen. As the result of nine tests, the temperauro of the flame of dry carbonic oxide containing only 1 per cent, of carbon dioxide was ascertained to be 1,747 deg. When the percentage of carbon dioxide was raised to 15 per cent., the flame temperature was only 1.552 deg. The temperature of combustion of dry hydrogen, without the admission of air to the Bunsen burner, was I,7oodeg. With full ad- mission of air to the burner, the temperature was 1,866 deg. The temilbrature of combustion of the mixed gases, 50 per cent, of carbonic oxide with 50 per cent, of hydrogen, was 1,775 deg. It may, therefore, be assumed that the flame temperatures of the above gases are approximately 1,750 deg. for the carbonic oxide, and 1,865 deg. for the hydrogen, while they are slightly below 1,775 deg. for the mixed gas. con- sisting of carbonic oxide, hydrogen, and car- bon dioxide. These tests were made at the gasworks of St. Quentin.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>WHY WE DIE.</head>
        <p>WHY WE DIE.</p>
        <p>The weight of a dead body does not differ in the slightest amount from the weight of the same body just before respiratibn ceases. The death process is not, therefore, - accompanied by the loss of any material substance. What is it that departs when life goes? Nobody in the A i present state of human knowledg is even able t to srmise. So far as appearances indicate, no- thing goes; the machine simply cc ses to work. "But at the bottom of all the p onomena of life," says Professor Mathews. " there is one thing fundamental—namely, breathing. Living matter, as long as it is alive, breathes. That 13 to say, it continually consumes oxygen and gives off carbon dioxide.., All other functibns depend upon this. Life, we may say, depends upon tly. continuance • of this process in all the tissues of the body: Under perfect conditions it should I continue indefinitely, and we should be im- mortal." That we die seems, in the opinion of ! Professor Mathews, to be chiefly due to the fact , that we do not know what and how much food Iwe ought to eat. • The tissues get sick: the cells stop breathing and we perish: We die, in truth, of self-poisoning. The only remedy, .in the present state of our knowledge, is careful- ness and moderation in eating.</p>
        <p>PHYSICAL PROPERTIES OF TANTALUM. Dr. Werner von Bolton, in a short note that appears in a German scientific journal, upon the hardness of wrought tantalum, states that in earlier tests a mass of metal raised to a red heat and than worked under the steam hammer had shown a, degree of hardness approaching that of the diamond. Further experiments were there- fore carted out in order to determine how far this apparent hardness Was duo • to the presence of oxides and other impurities in the metal, and how far it was due to the. inaccurate method of determining the coefficient of hardness. This test was made by attempting to bore through the wrought metal in sheet form with a diamond drill, and the makers of diamond cutting tools have informed the author that with tough metals this method yields inaccurate results. A specially pure lump of the metal was therefore prepared. and was tested in the special department of Messrs. Siemens and Iralske's works. devoted to the manufacture of boring tools, with the fol- lowing results: . _ _</p>
        <p>~—..,. 1. With a file of a specially hard told fine steel it could be filed, but the file itself suffered in the work; . . _</p>
        <p>2. A small chi.el a specially hard tool steel, with a point 4mni. in breadth, was blunted at everY blow of the hammer. • 3. A water-hardened steel tool marked the mass of tantalum, b`nt the tool speedily lost its edge. and was unable to make any further im- pression upon the metal. 4. Two boring tools were made from fine grained 'east steel, hardened in qiiicksilver and finished at a clear yellow . heat. One of these tGols was of the usual section. the other was tri- angular. With the first of, these tools the thin slwet of tantalum only lmin. in thickness re- ottired sevlm minutes foe perforation, while with tlio second tool ten minutes were required, tur- pentine being used in each case as a lubricant for the cutting edge. LI each case the cutting edire. of the tool war blunted.' From these tests it' follows that tantalum, con- taining Only sinall amounts of itnpurity, ham- inered into :beets at n ,red heat, is equal in hard- to •th:, best and most carefully-finished st.c.,el. Tantalum. • however, greatly exceeds this hard steel in toughners.for while these hard tool are brittle. tantalum can be :oiled POO ,•i!ts Wit}lollt ;11;11,' 'll,', ',OW metal. in rt,i ! purest state unites therefore the 111,1A:103s or .10 Lest steel with a greater toughness and ductility than is known to be possessed by an Wiz</p>
        <p>al CHILDREN,</p>
        <p>You will remember that a fortnight ago I told you that I intended to have a small flower show, and give prizes for the best 3peci- mens of flowers. I find that this idea has given great satisfaction, and I am sure that out of our number of members I shall have a neat many entries. Of course you will have to d) all the work yourselves; you must have no real help. It will be very simple, as on the packets of seeds you will find full directions given as to planting, etc., and anything you don t unde, stand I shall be very pleased to explain. You will be allowed to grow the plants any- how you like, in the open ground, in pots, greenhouses, etc. There will be no conditions attached to the competition, except that all competitors must be members of our Children's Corner and under the age of sixteen. The flowers will have to be grown from seeds the names of which we will give later on. I have received several sketches of dogs, and have awarded the prize to George B. Richmond, who also wrote an interesting letter. I have also sent a prize volume to T. Wills being the 250th member to join our Corner. Wills, new members have joined during the past week; you wilr find their names below. No one has sent the correct solutions to the puzzles given below. _ _ _</p>
        <p>I expect many of you have started school again after the summer holidays. I hope you have had a very pleasant time during the last few weeks. _</p>
        <p>Well, I must finish for this week, with all good wishes from UNCLE NED.</p>
