      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>ATHOL FOR,BES (Author of " Cassock and Comedy," " A Son of Itimmon," 4c.)</head>
        <p>ATHOL FOR,BES (Author of " Cassock and Comedy," " A Son of Itimmon," 4c.)</p>
        <p>I was dressing one morning, and happened to look out from the window of my bedroom at the Vicarage, when my attention was attracted to a man, evidently a sailor, who stumped up and down the pavement outside the gates with the regularity of a pendulum. He noticed no One, but would stop occasionally aria gaze up the pathway leading to the entrance hall, then give himself a jerk, turn round, and begin his pacing up and down again. I could sea the man was in a deep -study, and evidently grappling with some difficulty. Now and again he would rquirt out into the road a quantity of tobacco juice, which was spat front his mouth with unusual vehemence. Ile laid a red pcniket-handkerchief around his neck tiea ii the usual sailor's knot. When he stopped, his legs took the position of a pair of compasses. His clothing was ill fitting, but he was not badly dressed. When I came downstairs I had cool her look at him. One of the maids was (lusting the library, and she informed Inc that the man had been walking up and down when she got up at six o'clock. " I expect be wants a marriage licence, sir," she said. It struck inc this was the correct solution, and I sat clown to breakfast. Very often people who have applied to me to marry them, have spent hours outside the vicarage, no doubt for it final bird's-eye view of thinga matrimonial. Once I had gone out and spoken to a young MOO, who had distinguished himself by walking up and down the whole of one aftertmon. My act was kindly meant, but it was very unkind to the girl, whose banns lie had come out with the in- tention of putting up, for the moment I spoke to him, he turned and rrull like a hare. He wits ii fisherman, and she could not prevail upon him In come up to the church until his next voyage wm over. I never hurried • then after that., so 1 de- termined that my sailor visitor should walk tie and down until he came to too of his own free will and accord.</p>
        <p>I finished breakfast, and wont back to my lib- rary. I saw he was still there. He had him hand!, now mcrewed down into his pockets as far as be could get them. It's a marriage licence sure enough I thought. I knew the gesture However I was very wide of the imirk indecd ; in fact I could not have made a worse gues lit the cause of the luau's visit and his ngitution.</p>
        <p>- Then, in answering the various letters, he paseod out of my mind. About an hour afterwards, one of the curates came in. •"There's an odd-looking chap outside there who cannot make up his mind to face the ordeal," he said as be sat down. •‘ Poor fellow, he is in quite a perspiration over it ; why does't he send the girl ?" I laughed, and then we proceeded to go through our business. ThiN over, I walked out with my junior colleague as far as the gate, just to have a closer view of the doubting man who hesitated at the slop, but when I appeared, he walked away a little dis- tance to some railings. Here he k,topped and gazed seawards, never turning round once while I stood, and all the time he kept shifting his weight from one leg to the other, keeping his chin down on his arms. which he had folded, and resting on the rail. When I came in he resumed ids quarter deck kind of walk on the pavement, again at this side of the highway. I could not afford to look at him all the morning, so I ie. sinned my work. When I finished lunch, he was still keeping his watch, walking exactly the same distances, going through the same manceuvres. This was kept up until about three in the af- ternoon. People used to stop and stare at him_ When this happened he always walked away to the railings, as he did when I looked at him, and there be would stand gazing out to sea until the over curious people had gone on their way. I was wondering how long this state of things was going to last, when one of the maids Caine in and said that a man wished to know if he could speak to me. _ _</p>
        <p>"It is the man who has been outside the Vic- arage all the morning," she volunteered. " Very well, bring him in here," I answered. " Thank you, ma'am," he said to the servant dhe shewed him in, and he closed the door be- hind her.</p>
        <p>It was a good-humoured square sort of face which met mine, with deep sunken eyes, and the wrinkled skin spoke of years of hard work and rough weather. He was in a word a typical Eng- lish seaman.</p>
        <p>" Sit down," I said. " Thank you, sir," and he seated himself on the extreme edge of a chair to which I motioned him, and began to turn his cap round and round in a nervous way. I dipped my pen in the ink, and prepared to take down the names and ad- dresses, and other information required when people have made up their minds to be married. His dark eyes were eagerly scrutinising my face, so I thought I would help hint by a leading ques- tion.</p>
        <p>"W 11 I said in my pleasautest way, " what's her name E"</p>
        <p>For a second or two there was no answer, but a great sigh of relief escaped him. He dropped his cap and gazed at me in amazement. " Well," I repeated, as be made no effort to answer. .</p>
        <p>"Now who would have knowed that you knowed about it, since I haven't mentioned it to a living soul," he jerked out suddenly. Then he pulled out his pocket handkerchief, a similar one to wbst be was wearing round his neck, and mopped his forehead. " Not a blessed soul," he repeated in solemn tones, and he looked at me curiously. " Oh, never mind that, my good man. She must have a name," I went on. " so out with it." Ho glanced at the door and all round the room as if afraid of someone else hearing him " Is it straight " be ejaculated. " Yee, quite straight," I responded, " but tell ma her name, and I want yours."</p>
        <p>" Oh mine's William rev," he replied. " Awe ?" "Fifty-one, eir " . " Seaman ?" " Yes, " Now then I want lieu name " Betsy Ann," be whispered, and again cast his eyes round the room. " Betsy Ann what ?" I asked. " Betsy Ann, sir. She haven't got no other." " But she must have a surname," I persisted. A look of amazement catne over my auditor's face, and he shook his head mysteriously, and re- peated that she had no name of the kind I men- tioned.</p>
        <p>" She has a bar," he suddenly jerked out, Its if that were light on the subject. It v% as now my turn to be surprised. " Where does she live ?" I enquired. " Live ! she is not living ; she is lying in the river at the South Quay." " You don't want to be married then ?" I was beginning to see that I was mistaken in my surmises, and that he was referring to some ship, so I swung round in my chair and prepared to bear his story. " Dor ! no, sir. I don't wish to be married, 'cos I am married. Yen know my missus as lives in Blackwall Reach, Mrs. Pegs—l'm her hus- band." I nodded. I knew Mrs. Pegs very well in- deed. She was a most respectable woman, awl rt fairly regular attendant at my church. I remem- bered now to have seen a photograph of the man in front of me hanging tip above the fireplace of his little parlour. He drew his chair nearer to me, and whispered in my ear. " You can keep a secret, sir; you daren't tell anyone anything thit I oonfems to you. Well, there's a fortune in the Iletey Ann." " Do you mean the old tub that is for sale 2" I asked. _ _</p>
        <p>_ ..„..._. sir. She iti all old hooker, air, but there is a fortune ta lisr if you will stand hehiml me in it," and he nodded vigorously. " Why site lola heel condemned by the Bourd of Trade, reg.," I ittpllel. tol I Itioutrht roller bitterly of Hum., of toy 0011 money iovested in *lipping whiell tool not p..id sny dividend for aoma years.</p>
        <p>" ,•,- ".- ifiloar• s hundrfsl years old can make any. 11111l1Id for. tune 11l our days ?" " Howsoever, it is true what I say, as true as true can be. If you helF me to buy that ship, sir, there will be sufficient money to keep us for life. I needn't go to sea again, and you need never Preach another marmot]," and he slapped his knee by way of emphasis. I smiled at his allusion to my share in the en- terpri,e. " I'm not joking, sir. It's God's truth. I'm 3e earnest, sir. I have pawned everything I pes- tle", and lawyer Hallet has lent we forty pounds."</p>
        <p>" Is 103 speculating in it ?"</p>
        <p>A1..-.0 tau sate MI he furni- ture of our little honse. I dia not tell hint why I wanted the money. I have now sixty-eight ,pounds, thirteen shillings, and I want to buy that brig, sir. It may be that she won't bring much but I must have something behind me. We ;lave waited for this sixty years and more," ho added, " and I have raked up every penny. Wages has been small these last few years or I Hbould have had more." " Sixty years ?" I said . .</p>
        <p>" Ay, more than sixty. I have been in her thirty years and my father wa4 in her about thirty years' and my gramthither in her before that. I tell you, sir, there i a fortune in her. She will only fetch break-up price now, and I must have her."</p>
        <p>" Well, Pegs, what will break-up price come to 2"</p>
        <p>"It all depends. If the foreigners bid for her she will bring more, but all things considered I should think she will go for about one hundred and fifty pounds—not more. I am in earnest, sir. I have put everything into her that I have in the world."</p>
        <p>" Well, what do you want me to do, Pegs r, I ak,ked.</p>
        <p>" Stand behind me, sir, for a hundred pounds. That, with what I have, will do I think. Her old stores will fetch you that, sir." " I should like to have something more defi- nite about this fortune that is in her. At all events I should like to think it over, so you can call again this evening, or to-morrow." " She is to be sold to-morrow," and I saw that he looked crestfallen, aud there was a hun- gry anxious look in his eyes. ' See me to-night, then." He was very anxious to close there and then. I was in fact surprised at the man's agitation, the more so when I remembered the sacrifices he was making for his venture himself. After he had gone I walked along to the ship —about ten minutes' walk from the vicarage. I knew the vessel, but had not paid particular at- tention to her before. She was flush in the bows, with a high square stern, with the old-fashioned windows. A type of vessel that one rarely sees in these days under the British flag. The atuc. tioneer was on board with his clerk, and as I knew him I joined them and put a fen questions.</p>
        <p>" She will go for an old song," lie informed me. "The fact is she should have been con- demned long ago, but it is surprising the strength of the vessel even now. She will never go to sea again : the Board of Trade won't allow it.'</p>
        <p>asked if he knew Pegs. " Very well. Ile has been in this ship over thirty years, a thing unheard of, and what is more, his father and grandfather before him. He is a capital seaman and es straight an a die. Ile is very much cut up about the side of his old friend. He has been at me every day to ask what people were after her. I quiteunderstand it. It's just like selling his old home." " What do you think she will fetch</p>
        <p>" Perhaps a hundred pounds. You see it wilt be a business breaking her up. Although she is copper fastened and sheathed she has been al- lowed to run down in the way of stores. She might bring more, and she might bring less. There was a Dane looking at her this morning. He and a Swede thought of buying her, but they khook their heads after going through her. It would pay a man well to buy her and break hex up himself. Ho could hire his own time, and the copper would pay him for his outlay and trouble handsomely." " Pegs wants to buy h&amp;c," I wild. " Yes, I guessed that. I expect lie is sick of the sea, and wnnis to earn a bit by breaking lieu. up. He is just the man for a job of this kind and as honest as daylight. He is so very mysterious about it in his simple way," mud the auctioneer laughed. " I could see that he was smelling after it, though he did his best to throw Inc oil the scent. _Perhaps he thinks I would run him up." Here Ids assistant called his attention to some- tldng, and I took my leave. _ _ .</p>
        <p>''U he wants you to hold him the money I should do so," he shouted oflor me ns well dowit the side. " That is, if she goes for a hun- dred or so."</p>
        <p>"PerhipM you woulkl like to lend him the money yourself," I replied, surprised ttt the Ins ICS (:1111.110MN.</p>
        <p>" Wdlingly / woula, if I had it," find I saw he meant it. " Good day, Vicar," and he turne,l :o;‘,.y to speak to the younger 11111.11 who was with</p>
        <p>'When I arrived at the vicarage, there was Pegs pacing up and down more agitated than Laura.</p>
        <p>" I have got another twenty pounds, sir," lie Fahl as I got up to him. " I sold nay insurance polj,ey tc INIr. Stubbs." . .</p>
        <p>• This was a man who collected rents and a,- misted in a solicitor's office, and I found that thil, W /IF his price for a paid•up policy for IMO Into dred pounds. That makes eighty pounds, thirteen shil- lings, sir," Pegs said, as I took him into iny library.</p>
        <p>" Now, Mr. Vicar, you must stand behind in for the remainder, and you won't look behind you again. If we get the Pets?, Ann you VRII give up the church to-morrow and be it gentle- man." " But in what way do you propose to make our fortunes ?" I asked, falling in with the hum- our of the thing. " You may take my solemn David for it, sir. It is true what I say. My father would have bought her years ago, sir. He saved up till his life, and he IT mild have done it, but a building society bust up, and ho lost over four hundred. I have lived to buy that ship. Why, sir, I have sailed in her for three pounds Lou shiillirg.a ii Month, when I could have got four pounds mol even four pounds ten in other vessels besides that, I have had chances ashore. I tell you, sir, there is a fortune for you and me in her. See; here is my money," and he produced a tobacco pouch from his pocket and counted out the money in five pound notes, with some gold, and should think between three and four pounds in</p>
        <p>"6 Yes, I'ege. but can you not give Me some idea of where the foriune le to come from P"</p>
        <p>" No, sir. My father made me take an oath, and I have kept it. I wish I could tell you. It ;punt% go out of the family " There was something manifestly honest in the man, but I had lent money to parishioners before and had never heard of it again. An idea struck me. I would test the man in another way. " Will you leave that money with me if I sun willing to give you a full cheque when you tell me what the vessel goes for ?" I asked. I saw he was puzzled, and I explained to him what I meant.</p>
        <p>" Yes, sir," ho replied with great alacrity when he graspea my meaning. " Yes, sir— willingly," he shouted in his excitement. • .</p>
        <p>, I saw nothing would nring his secret from him, if secret there were, and mystery always had attraction for me, so I promised to provide the money, but he Was not to go beyond one hundred and fifty pounds. The man had watched my face the whole time, and when I told hint that I was willing to ad- vance the money, he grasped my band with a grip that calmed my finger tips to tingle for hours afterwards, then he put his hands up lo his lace, and his big frame shook with sobs. Things were becoming now more mysterious than over. " Beg your pardon, sir," and he made orous effort to control himself—'' I was thinkjaw of my dear old did, I wish he could have lived," and the tears csm' Imito his eyes again.</p>
        <p>bii you haven't got the p yet. She might bring Inure than one hundred and fifty pounds." " No, sir, I al witym felt that she would belong to toe some ,1:1v, ,ql,l J ii sere or her now," end be got. lip to go.I t‘ II eomo lir, sir, RS SOO” as the Aide ov,r I 1,11</p>
        <p>There was s-1-p l ~..d!oss look about his eyes which made Me feel anxiow. About Iho mon. ,o 1 called toy hon.ekeeper. and told her to take Pegs into tho kitchen and give ii un a rump of tea. Ile protested 11t he wits ill tight, but 1 insisted, and more to please moo than anything else, he went. _ _ .</p>
        <p>On enquiry afterwaribt I founa be tolieu hitt lea and bad Ono eaten MOllle loit. TtlY houmelteeper volunteered the information 'hut ,•lie thought Pegtt was mail, and reel» d o le hurt will•li 1 offered no comment. _ _</p>
        <p>I wrote a note to the auctioneer end told him :bat it was toy in to help Pegs to buy the brig, nd that, he muld go tootle hundred and fifty Olinds, but I did not wish him to lose the ship for the sake of live ',minds. Next day I walked d'own to the vessel about half past eleven. The bidding had just eon- :notated. Pegs was looking pale and anxious. " Ono hundred and forty," shouted Ihe auc- tioneer as I stepped on deck. " Five," culled out someone) after a slight " One hundred and forty-five," cried the salesman. Tliatdc yon," in reply to a nod from Pegs. " Now, gentlemen, she etaudm at one hundred md litty. One hundred and fifty," be repeated several times. I glanced at Pegs. His eyes were</p>
