      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 2, 1905. THE FREE CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 2, 1905. THE FREE CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>two live co:lim pis ilh M:11,.. it. I' H., it, ri +:•CPr. " Five," cried out the voice that 1 loul heard IL tst.</p>
        <p>'• One hundred and fifty-five bid. One hun- dred and fifty-five only." Poor Pegs. There was a commotion. He tried to gasp out something, and fell face forward with a thud on the vessel's deck. A seaman put a bucket over the aide, and sluiced him with the cola water over the bead. I saw there were sue. licient to attend to him, and as the auctioneer had gone on repeating, " One hundred and fifty - live," I caught his eye and nodded, and the ves- ,ll was knocked down to me at one hunched and sixty, plus five per cent. commission. Pegs still lay on the desk motionless, but some one had now procured brandy, and this was be- ing poured down his throat. Then slowly be began to revive—his eyes fell upon me and tried to speak. I knelt down and lifted up his head. " All right, Pegs," I whispered, " she is yours," and I took his hand. I am no a rich man, but I think that smile on nix face, ugly as hie countenance was, amply re- paid rue for what I had done. He grasped my hand, not with the vigour he had done before, but he understood, and it was his way of express- lug his gratitude. A boy who was playing about the deck ran for a cab, and I had my sailor Friend conveyed to the vicarage. Poor chap, I found that he had eaten nothing for the last three days with the exception of what he had had in my house the day before. He had sonic tea and toast, and then at his wish I walked with him to his house in Black waif Reach. There Mrs. Pegs was waiting for him. She looked at her husband, but I saw the question froze on her lips as she saw his pale face. ' It's all right, Mary," he said, " we have got lier."</p>
        <p>She clasped him round the neck, and as they ;tasisted upon me remaining, we all sat down to tea and shrimps. I never enjoyed a meal more, nor shall I ever forget the little room, the table ith its snowy white cloth, and the kettle that sang so merrily throughout it all, as if it understood the whole affair.</p>
        <p>Next day, Pegs called upon sue at breakfast time. He informed me that he had made ar- rangements to tow the Betsy Ann down the ems: to a quiet little spot where he and his son could Freak her up without being molested. His son was in the dockyard at Chatham —a ,carpenter— and it was understood that he should give up his work there to help his father. I could see now that there was a mystery con- nected with the brig, but as Pegs vouchsafed no information likely to lead up to a solution, I was as much in the dark as ever, but each interview had only confirmed my impression of my Bert friend's honesty and straightforwardness, and I was quite satisfied that he should keep his Own counsel.</p>
        <p>Next day, the Betsy Ann towed out to sea, and he passed. the vicarage I saw old Pegs waving his band, and as a salute to me the ensign was dipped three times. About a week after this I received a short let- ter from him, telling me that the work was go- ing on, and that the fortune so far was very good, but no further information. He asked nm to call and see his " missus," and tell her it was " all right." I immediately ,sent for Mrs. Pegs, and read to her the brief communication. She thanked me, but offered no comment of any kind, except to thank me for my part in the matter. She said it was a great joy to her to know that Pegs would not have to go to sia again. She seemed qinte confident of this and of the fortune. . . . .„</p>
        <p>A month had elapsed since the Betsy A2lll sailed on her last voyage, and I received one morning a bulky packet, which was registered. To my amazement, when I opened it, there were in Bank of England notes one hundred pounds. I could scarcely believe my own eyes. There was enclosed a short, matter-of-fact note from Pegs, asking me to acknowledge the receipt of it, and promising more in a few days' time. I caught the next train to the little place on the coast, where he had beached the vessel. There found Pegs and his son hard at work. He greeted mo with great heartiness and put a ladder over the side. Then lie told me the story. In his grandfather's time the brig had been armed as a privateer. One of the captures they effected was a huge Spanish galleon from which they had taken four chests full of doubloons. While lowering one in the hold of the ship it had fallen and burst open. Each of the Jacks had liberally helped themselves, but grandfather Peg took the precaution of boring holes in the ship's casing, and hiding the greater part of his share by slipping the coins through. How he intended to get them out was a matter which he evidently did not pause to consider. Possibly he hoped one day to own the brig. However, the story had been banded down from father to son, and they bad kept the secret well. He led me to what re- mained of the cabin, and .showed me a case about</p>
        <p>one-third full of the. doubloons. It seemed to Inc</p>
        <p>a 4 if I were in some dream.</p>
        <p>"I took a box of them up to London a few days ego and sold them for just on three hun- dred pounds sir. I told you there was a fortune in her. We will share all proper among the three of us. There will be lots more of them as we get further down into the easing of the ship. My son and I eau do all the work. It would not do to have strangers about," and he gave a sig• nifioant shrug of his broad shoulders. I agreed, but gave him to understand that all I wanted bsek was the amount I had lent—and no more. This he strongly objected to, and we have not come to an agreement yet. In all, Pegs got something like twelve hundred pounds out of he doubloons, and quite another two hundred for lie old stores and the copper sheathing. Probably he would have got more Coins but the work was cut short by a furious north-easter coupled with it spring title which buried the remains of the brig in the sand. - - -</p>
        <p>Pegs and his wife live now in comfort, and the copper kettle miiig. merrily on the hob an if fully aware of the good fortune that has overtaken the house. Ho slips AY ooeanienally, and only the " missus " and myself know whore/ be has gone. Pegs is 6sure the sand will slat Aome day, and there will be more Spanimh toad.</p>
