      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>HOLIDAYS AND WORK.</head>
        <p>HOLIDAYS AND WORK.</p>
        <p>The holiday season is now drawing to a close, and soon the Free Churches will be making their plans for a vigorous campaign in the Autumn. For the present, however, attention is more concentrated on our seaside and health resorts than to the work of cities and towns. At practically all the great seaside resorts, the Fre° Churches have combined for special efforts during the summer months. United Open Air services are held with considerable success, and every endeavour is made to reach the class of people who are not usually in attendance at places of worship. In addition to work t..f this kind the holidays are now being utilised for gathering of workers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>nEv. F. B. MEYER'S CONFERENCES.</head>
        <p>nEv. F. B. MEYER'S CONFERENCES.</p>
        <p>The Rev. F. B. Meyer, 8.A., has just con- ducted a series of Conferences at well-known resorts, starting at Morecambe. At this popular resort Mr. Meyer presided over five or six meetings. The principal items in the pro- gramme included a sermon by himself, an Early- Morning Prayer-Meeting, a Conference over tea, a Free Church Demonstration, and a Talk to -Ministers and Lay Preachers. This latter has. been one of the great features of Mr. Meyer's visitation work. For some two hours he talks in a frank and brotherly manner to his fellow-ministers, giving them most practical hints, both on their own con- duct of life and upon methods of work. Everywhere Mr. Meyer has gone he has left a deep impression with the ministers. The Con- ference over tea, too, is a feature in which Mr. Meyer excels. Calling the local Secretaries to his side, he puts them through a cross-examina- tion as to their work, the status of the Council, its finances, operations' &amp;c. This is done with his usual tact and humour, and helps the audience to realise, in a way not otherwise possible, what Free Church Federation actually means. It can easily be realised that, with such a huge organisation as that possessed by the National Free Church Council, it is frequently necessary to overhaul the machinery, to tighten the loose screws, and see that everything is in the state of high efficiency. Mr. Meyer has done valuable work in this direction, and the Councils locally are far stronger and better equipped as a result of his services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>DOUGLAS AHD BLAMPOOL.</head>
        <p>DOUGLAS AHD BLAMPOOL.</p>
        <p>Last year Mr. Moyer conducted Conferences at Scarborough and Blacicpool. This year, in addition to that held at Morecambe, he is visit- ing Douglas (Isle of Man) and Blackpool. On each of these visits Mr Meyer is accompanied by Rev. Thomas Law, the Secretary of the Federa- tion. l'he Revs. J. E. Rattenbury and J. G Stevenson are also rendering assistance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>MIDLAND CONVENTION.</head>
        <p>THE MIDLAND CONVENTION.</p>
        <p>In the Spring of this year Dr. Horton, the President of the National o:tincil, commenced a series of Conventions which have already provoked considerable attention. His visits to such centres as Plymouth, Llandrindod Wells, Sunderland, Lowestoft., &amp;c., have made deep impressions. After a brief interval for holidays Dr. Horton is commencing again with a Conven- tion at Cheltenham. This centre has been fixed as being the most convenient for the Midland towns, and representatives will he present from all the Councils around. The meetings continue for three days, and include Early Morning Prayer Meetings, Morning Conferences, Afternoon Rambles to places of interest in the neighbourhood, Conferences over tea, and Evening Demonstrations. September 18 is the opening day, and on the Tuesday fol- lowing Rev. 11. J. Campbell, M.A., will preach what is known as the Convention Sermon. At the Great Free Church Demonstration, to be held on the Wednesday. the speakers will in- clude Rev. Thomas Law, Rev. F. B. Meyer, and Mr. J. Compton Rickett, M.P.; while the Rev. Silas K. Hocking, Mr. R. W. Perks, M.P., and Mr. H. R. Mansfield, M.P., are expected to speak on the Thursday.</p>
        <p>DR. HORTON'S JUBILEE. It is interesting to note that on the opening day of the Cheltenham Convention Dr. Horton will celebrate his Jubilee, having been born in London fifty years ago on September 18. Dr. Horton has had a distinguished career, and possesses a wide influence. For twenty years he has ministered to the Congregational Church at Hampstead, but his fame has been extended greatly, not only by his personal visits to other places, but by the books he has found time to write. Of the works which he has recently issued, one of the most notable has been "The Open Secret," which is intended to be a Free Church Manual of Devotion. This has already had a large circulation, although it has only been on the market for some months.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>METHODIST UNION.</head>
        <p>METHODIST UNION.</p>
        <p>There seems to be every prospect now of con- siderable progress being made in the scheme for Methodist reunion. The Annual Conferences of the Methodist bodies, which have just held their meetings, have carried the matter a long step forward. Within a short time we should see. the accomplishment of the union between the Methodist New Connexion, the Bible Christians, and the United Methodist Free Church. These should form a strong and united body. Of those who worked for the consummation of this end there were none more prominent than the late Rev. F. W. Bourne, whose death comes as a sad blow to all Methodists. Mr. Bourne's saintly life and sterling qualities endeared him to all who were privileged to come into touch with him, and his memory will long remain amongst a wide circle of friends.</p>
        <p>A BRIGHTON PRESIDENT. Amongst the new Methodist Presidents, the choice of the Bible Christians is a happy one. The Rev. S. B. Lane has for many years been acting as secretary of that Connexion. His elevation to the highest office in his denomina- tion has come as a reward to long service. Mr. Lane is a man of many activities. Amongst some of the most important of his labours have been those in connection with the Free Church Council Movement. He is Secretary of the Brighton Free Church Council and the Sussex Federation of Councils. In this capacity he had the undertaking of the local arrangements when the National Free Church Council held its meetings at Brighton some time ago, and his energy, tact, and ability commended him to all attending the gatherings.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>BOURNEMOUTH ENTERPRISE.</head>
        <p>THE BOURNEMOUTH ENTERPRISE.</p>
        <p>The Free Churches of Bournemouth have united in the founding of a Free Church Girls' Guild Home, which should prove a valuable addition to that pleasant town. The Home was recently opened by the Mayor and Mayoress, and is intended to serve as a centre for the girt-life of the Free Churches of Bourne- mouth.</p>
        <p>WORK FOR YOUNG MEN.</p>
        <p>Among the many departments of service in connection with the National Free Church Council, one which is receiving considerable attention just now is the Young Free Church- men's Auxiliary, which was commenced last Autumn with the intention of banding together the young men of the Free Churches for mutual help and active service. Although, of course, little can be done during the summer, two new branches of the Auxiliary are reported, and it is anticipated that during the Autumn renewed stimulus will be put into the arrange- ments, and an ambitious programme of work entered upon. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>SURPRISED HF.R HUSBAND.</head>
        <p>SURPRISED HF.R HUSBAND.</p>
        <p>- At the St. Helens Police Court on Monday, John Costello, a labourer, of Parr-street, was considerably surprised when he was sent to prison for four months' hard labour for assaulting his wife, who at first declined to give evidence, against him, and said she had nothing at all against him, as he was a very good husband when he was sober.—The magistrato4, however, insisted on her giving evidence, and it appeared that he concluded a drinking bout by a day in bed, during which his wife had to take him five pints of beer to bed. He came down in the afternoon and struck an old ' woman who was in the house, and because his wife remonstrated, he threw a glass tumbler at her, inflicting a wound on her head.</p>
      </div>
