      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>TREND OF THE TIMES.</head>
        <p>THE TREND OF THE TIMES.</p>
        <p>[By Mr. H. Whitam.]</p>
        <p>- - When we get behind all that has been written,read and said in favour of Protectionave still remain some- what puzzled as to how it comes about that we should have so very much poverty in lands that work it for all that it is worth. The United States is given as a sample of what Protection can do for the worker, and yet when I take up my paper this morhing I read the following NEW YORK, August 9.—The bread riots of the East Side reached their crisis to-night. Midnight found the streets in the affected section filled with infuriated mobs, numbering thousands of frenzied men and women, who stoned shops, stoned wagons containing kosher bread sent from Hoboken and Philadelphia and trampled the loaves in the street. Scores were clubbed by the police all during the day and evening and sought drug stores to have their wounds dressed. Arrests were of momentary occurrence and the damage done to shops was estimated at thousands of dollars. On the European Continent things are in much the mama state. I am of the opinion that matters, so far as rioting goes, would be very much worse than they are in England or the United States were it not that throughout the European Continent the standard of living is very much lower than what obtains in England or America, and the repressive power of the Governments is so much more despotic. I have tried hard to find out wherein the American workers benefit by Protection. Instead of benefiting the workers, I find it creates a selfish grasping class who will not part with one cent, more than they are forced to. I venture to say that there is not a large city in the United States but what is swarming just at this moment with unemployed. There may be work to be had in the country among the fruit or among the grain, but the conditions of fruit picking are such that a very little satisfies, and as for the grain fields, the job is only fit for the professional tramp. I have just read Robert Hunter's book on poverty published by Macmillan and circulated in a cheap edition by the Socialists. Herein we see what a hideous, disgusting, selfish, and brutal civilization we are living in. The tale told of child labour is simply horrible, and yet of such is the kingdom of heaven. Of old men and women of no further use in the world,' and I speak of what I know when I say that one of the largest companies in the States will not engage men over thirty fi-e years of age, and takes hold of every opportunity to discharge the men who have grown old in its service. A week ago I paid a visit to one who has worked 29 years for the company. He was taken sick with rheumatism, and when he turned up again for work he was told they had no hither use for him. The President of this same company is now before the courts charged with having paid himself 7.5,000d015. to which he had no right legal or moral. Still worse are matters in Russia. Immense wealth is wrung from the poor workers, the said workers having, no remedy, nothing but civil war to fall back , upon. In Italy a strike or disturbance in the labour market brings the soldiers out. In Germany, the peculiar and glaringly unfair system of voting pre- ' vents the Socialists from reaping the benefits they ought to have from their growing numbers. The war lord is master—at present. No wonder there are bread riots in New York. The immigration figures from all parts of Europe and of the worst classes, have grown to such immense pro- portions as to call for the anxious attention of the emigration officials. The remarkabla feature that goes along with this tale of woe is the immense wealth that exists and is in the hands of such a comparative few. I nowhere hear or see this immense wealth referred to in an envious spirit. Envy is put on one side when a body of men begin to feel the power and the lash that draws blood. When this power is used a big D --takes its place. Let me give a personal example. Some eighteen months ago the men with whom I worked attempted to form a union. I tried hard to keep out- side the business, feeling myself getting altogether too old to take any further active part in trade unionism. The men did'nt get on very well in their object, and at last I had to take part. At length they were organized, and I stood on one side refusing to take any official position. They were but a very raw crowd of ignorant Irishmen, with not the least ex- perience in such matters. In the meantime I had had</p>
        <p>_ a rise to a better and an easier job. As a looker on I saw the men with their new union were drifting lilt() a strike. How a big railway strike is no joke. Especially is this so in America where all sorts and conditions of nationalities</p>
        <p>get very much mixed up. I gave the matter much anxious thought. At this time what is known as the Citizens' Alliance was becoming powerfully active throughout the whole nation. This Alliance is made up of such employers and citizens as are actively dis- posed to take steps to destroy trade unionism. The member i were active strike breakers, and I was sure they would crowd their men then into our ware-houses should a strike be decided upon. I therefore wrote two long letters to their champion paper—l wish they could be reprinted in the Examiner" for your</p>
        <p>readers to note the conditions under which the 'men worked—in which I appealed for a hearing with the view of averting a strike, and to which I put my name. All was done honorably and above board. No notice wa.s taken of the appeal and a strike was declared that lasted for weeks, and nothing was gained. I lost my job, although I had taken not the least part in the strike, and was put on night work. I may say that before the strike was declared, the men finding they had no one to state their case to the Company, prevailed upon the secre- tary of the Labour Council to see me and get me to do it. I agreed to this and applied to the super- intendent for his permission. He turned upon me and savagely asked what the I had to do with it. I told him the men had my sympathy. He gave me a good cursing and turned away. When I think of all this and see the literature of the simple lire" that is urged upon all men for their acceptance and am told that Christianity teaches peace on earth and goodwill towards all men I ask, "where, oh where, is Christianity? where are the Christians?" Again I read--</p>
        <p>NEW YORK, August lOth.—Ten thousand bakers, comprising the entire force of bread makers of the city of New York, are on the point of a symr,m‘letio strike with their Hebrew brothers and the met 7", polls faces a crisis in history which has no parallel In commercial conditions. The city is menaced by a bread famine. This condition was precipitated by the meeting. of the joint executive board of the Bakers' Union late to-night, at whicU it was agreed thAt every union man in Greater New York should strike in sympathy with the "kosher" bakers of the East side and by the united action compel the bosi bakers to accept the terms hid down befere them by the erunioyees. When Mr. Thomas Lawson started his campaign against Rockfeller's Standard Oil Trust, and Mr. Russell his against the Beef Trust they started a ball rolling that neither they nor any other monied power on earth can stop. There are parsons who are not only willing to receive the tainted gold of the modern robber barons, but can find an excuse for the robbers by saying they will" use the devil's earnings to pro- mote God's good work," but there are others who will have none of it. Condemnations without end are now being published every day until one of Rockefeller's closest friends has said this good Christian man who was constantly saying Teach me Lord what I meat do To spend my gold and pleasure You— To hold the earth and heaven too " has aged five years during the past five weeks." This man, Rockfeller, While his unspent gold cloth vex his head, While a million children cry for bread, _ . . . . -</p>
        <p>was speaking in the Baptist- 'Church, aleaveland, and said, "The world needs teachers of the faith. However humble we may to?, whatever our place, it matters not, we can show the best side. We must show the most winsome and convincing side. In fact, we must show that we love God." This man, then, what does he do to show his winsomeness? The helpers on one of his estates who have been paid 1-50dols. a day have just been reduced to I-dols. As there are 25 of these men he will save 1-25d015. a day. He has now got hold of all the flax seed of the world and raised the price from 1-2 Idols. a bushel. to 1-48. All the independent mills of Buffalo and elsewhere producing linseed oil from flax seed are closed. Truly this man is making the best use of his talents and will surely enter into the joy of his Lord. Now the question before us, is what are the people going to do about it? We are having object lessons given to us every day. We are seeing that the capital- ist and employing classes are entirely lost to all sense of honour. In the United States and the same may be said of the nations of the world generally, that not content with controlling all local authorities they con- trol the nation's legislatvre, and undertake to shape, through the tariff and other ways, the policy of the Government so that this policy, in place of strength- the interests of the community as a whole, works for the enrichment of those skill- fully organized combinations. It is clear that these trusts or combinations have undertaken</p>
        <p>to control the markets, to fix prices and depend for their power upon the tariff. We also see that the employers' guiding motto is :—" Business is busi- ness." There is not to-day anything in the United States to eat or to drink for adult or baby that can be bought pure and wholesome ;. all is adulterated. It is said in to-day's paper that countless thousands of Rocky salmon are rotting in the traps and on the scows of the Combine that controls the salmon indus- try of Puges Sound, and that this Combine is so audacious as to can this putrid mess, and in a few months it will be offered for sale in the markets of the world. The fish in the Sound is so abundant this year that they cannot get rid of it, and yet the work- men are required to place their empty dinner baskets at the gate, where they are inspected by the watch- man, so that the men miy not take a fish away. The same anxious greed is displayed in every line of birsiness. Drags for the army, the workhouse 1 clothing or shoes, armour plate for the war vessels, all, all are subject to graft. Get-rich-quick—that'slthe ' idea. Wherever the economic feature enters, whether it be in church or workshop, pulpit or press, family circle, or in social friendship its entrance spells moral rain. Let us get rid of it. Emerson was no wild talker or thinker, but he saw the evil of our day and voiced it. In his essay on wealth he said, " Socialism has done good service in setting men on thinking how certain civilizing benefits now only enjoyed by the opulent, can be enjoyed by all." —A. child four years old earned nineteen cents in one afternoon, and the mother said, " I'm so sotry Will isn't here. He is seven, but he gets tiled. I left him at home sick." This was in a factory in New York, the State where in one factory, by the foreman's own estimates, there were three hundred children under , fourteen—the State where children work until two and three o'clock in the morning. A child works sixty-eight hours a week, or over eleven a day, and receives 12s. The inspectors of New York are all cor- rupted with the gold of the employers, and there are no prosecutions. I was pleased to read in a recent" Examiner" the views of the Rev. J. F. Matthews on the labour ques- tion, and his solution of the problem, which I ask you to reprint. The rev, gentleman called for " The return of men to Parliament who had had their noses on the grindstone. Men who know what the struggle was, and who were familiar with industrial and economic pressure from within. So long as they sent lawyers, millionaires, manufacturers, and country squires to Parliament, so long would the present status quo be maintained. They might just as well put a fox to guard a hen roost, or a wolf to take care of a lamb. Not that these men were unsympathetic, they too were victims of the same economic system, but they did not know that phase of the problem, so familiar to the man who earns a weekly wage. The appiont- ment of a labour department with a labour minister as a necessary item in the solution of this great problem. Let them work for the coming of that time when the nation would unitedly recognize that every man and woman born with a mouth to feed and hands to work should have the latter permanently secured to him,that the first might always be met." This is the only way to remedy things. So long as rich men, simply because they are rich, so long as the influence of employers is allowed to dominate in the selection of a candidate, and so long as the churches are allowed to use their influences in electing nice rich gentlemen who can lav corner stones, take the chair at tea parties and head subscriptions, so long will the labourer 'amain a mere beast of burden.</p>
        <p>Socialism is growing fast in the United States. It is also growing in England. It may be an indefinite movement, but it is at all events an evolutionary idea that makes for the betterment of the masses. I don't think for a moment that the masses will bring it about. It will come from the upper thinking classes. In the meantime the only bodies of men who preserve us from revolutionary anarchy, I mean the socialists, the trade unionists, and the co-operators must keep on with their edacational propaganda such as it is. It is pitiful to note that the trade unionists and the co-oper- ators seem to rest satisfied with the mere declaring of a dividend. It is time they mules forward movement or they are likely to get left. H. WHITHA.II.</p>
      </div>
