      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>WARRINGTON ARCHITECT'S SAD END</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON ARCHITECT'S SAD END</p>
        <p>Found Drowned in the Woolston Canal.</p>
        <p>Considerable regret was occasioned in Warrington on Tuesday when it became known that Mr. Charles Robert Barton, a well known architect residing at 244, Manchester-road, and having chambers in Mill- street had been found drowned in the Woolston canal. The deceased, who was sixty-one years of age, had a Ilarge circle of friends by whom he was much esteemed., Before comniencing business for himself he was engaged I in the offices of the late Alderman J. E. Wright, an ex-mayor of the 'Borough. It appears that he and his wife had decided to go to Blackpool for their holidays on Tuesday and after making preparations for the departure Mr. Burton left the house at 11.30 on Monday night, remarking that he felt weary and would go for a little walk. As he did not return after the lapse of a considerable time, search was made for him but unsuccessfully. At 7 o'clock the next morning his body was found floating in the Woolston canal.</p>
        <p>' THE INQUEST. Mr. S. Brighouse, County Coroner, held the inquest at the Police Station on Tuesday evening. Elizabeth Burton, deceased's widow, said she and her husband had intended going on their holidays that day. At 11.30 on Monday night deceased left the house, remarking "1 feel weary. I will go for a little walk." Witness waited for him some considerable time, and, (hiding deceased's hat in the lobby, informed a friend—Mr. F. G. Taylor— of the facts and he went in search of deceased, but unsuccessfully. At 9.30 on Tuesday morning she heard that deceased's body had been found in the canal. Two years ago deceased fell down some steps at Latchford, and on and of' since then had complained of severe pains in the head, He was very much depressed at times—more so on Sunday last. She had never heard him threaten to take away his life. John Flathbone, lock keeper at Paddington, said that at 7 o'clock on Tuesday morning he was attending to his duties on the bank of the Woolston Canal, and saw the body of the deceased floating on the water. He recovered the body and sent for the police, who con- veyed it to deceased's home. The j -ry returned a verdict of " Found drowned," the foreman (Mr. F. J. Whittle) announcing that they desired to express their deepest sympathy with Mrs. Burton in her sudden bereavement. Most of the jury had known the deceased for a great number of years, and felt his sad end very much indeed. Mr. F. G. Taylor returned thanks, and also expressed the gratitude of the family to the Coroner for having arranged the inquest at such short notice. He acknow- ledged the courtesy and assistance of the police, who were deserving of special thanks, in view of the difficul- ties attending the removal of bodies in country districts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>MOTORING EXTRAORDINARY.</head>
        <p>MOTORING EXTRAORDINARY.</p>
        <p>More than one car has demonstrated its ability to ascend the flight of stone steps in the Crystal Palace Grounds, and a similar performance has been effected on the steps of the Capitol at Washington. What is perhaps the most remarkable achievement of this kind, however, was lately brought off at Odessa. The boulevard steps of this town are one of the longest flights in the world; indeed, it is statei that when viewed from below the effect is quite awe-inspiring, and that for on automobile to make the ascent would appear quite impossible. An American recently laid a wager of 1,000 roubles, however, that a 7 h.p. Oldsmobile would get to the top, and explained that the only thing that would stop the car would be the interference of the Russian police. The wager being accepted, a car was borrowed from a private owner, and the little runabout was brought to the foot of the steps and the attempt begun. Contrary to expectation, there was no interference on the part of the police,who seemed to take a lively interest in the proceedings. The car successfully reached the top, and was received with cheers by the crowds which had assembled at the summit. The owner of the car immediately took possession of the tiller, and he and his wife went for a long drive. Notwithstanding the continuous succession of shocks to which the car was subjected, it sustained no damage whatever, not even a slackened bolt. The steps number 193 in all, and have each a height of with a width of 161 in. ; the total height, therefore, is 84 ft. end the gradient thus formed is one of 30 per cent. The most interesting feature of the performance is that, owing to the length of the wheel-base, both front and rear wheels were obliged to jump simultane- ously.—" Country Life,"</p>
        <p>- ' Works Association individual merit handicap was con- SECOND DIVISION.' eluded on Saturday, and it is hoped to complete the P. W. L. D. Pts. second and third rounds this (Saturday) afternoon Prescot Earlestown of would, anticipating however, t o St. Helens Recs... Newton ll ..: 01 ::: 1101 mthoeremooreneg-usiildtoydogaf the ittwowoo Warringtonhard to St. Helens l2 .. r:)5 ..: :/ 12 ... 4 ... 7 ... 1 ... 9 imagine, as to his own great surprise Thornley allied Stanley I ----- There was an exciting contest between Walker's , There has again been some little friction with Brewery and Moore and Dare3bury. the villagers triumph- regard to accommodating players connected with the ~, log by six runs. The batting was very poor but for Warrington District League who on the date of a works , fine bowliir,performance, TRUSSES.—The new Water Pad Truss, never get the victors H. Taylor gave a . fixture are also figuring in a League engagement. Both }lard.—The new Washable Waterproof Trusses always capturing eight wickets for 13 runs. associations early in the season mutually agreed, so far -- , as was possible, to help each other in this respect, and clet.n, cool and comfortable.—The Triangular Fluid Pad , re had there Truss is effectual in most difficult cases. Trusses of Grappenball 2nd entertained Barton and were victori- up to Saturday ---e been a total absence of inners_ every known description .—WHITF, at WRIGHT. Surgical oils by 51 runs. S. Jenkins VMS the top scorer, his venienee of the slightest description, on Saturday, Instrument and Artificial Limb Makers, 68) Renshaw innings realizing 20, while Mottram and Hallows bowled however, to the surprise of all, League players who had at. Liverpool. (Advt 2.) , very effectively, entered for the Works Main were given half an hour in which to put in an appearance and meet their opponents</p>
        <p>Boys were playing on the banks of the Bridgewater canal near Worsley, on Saturday, when one of them named Allen, of Winton, fell into the water. A large dog was sent into the water and brought him to a place of safety.</p>
        <p>in the first round. To some League players this was a practical impossibility and the result was that the persons they were drawn to meet were given the games. Why the authorities of the Works Association should adopt such a drastic line of conduct I cannot for a moment imagine especially as they would not have been inconvenienced had they called upon League representatives to be present after—say six o'clock. It is to be hoped that they will in the future recognize the unwisdom of taking a step likely to cause unnecessary feeling as at the presant time the attitude ' of the two bodies towards each other is of the most friendly.</p>
