      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, September 2, 1905.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, September 2, 1905.</p>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Rugby Football.</p>
        <p>[By " Ovallst."l</p>
        <p>The football campaign for 1905-6 opens in real earnest to-day (Saturday), and Warrington enter upon a list of fixtures which will, I think, prove as interest- ' ing as any in the history of the club. It must not be forgotten that the re-constitution of the Northern League still embodies the competitive system. Points will be at stake in each match as before, and though each club does not play every other there will doubt- less be as keen a struggle for the leadership as there is in first class cricket. In addition to the ordinary league games", and the West Lancashire Cup Com- petition there is also a Lancashire Cup Competi- tion: which opens on October 14th and closes in December, so as to leave a con- siderable interval before the Northern Union Challenge Cup Competion which is the piece de resig.ancl tif Northern Union football. Warrington, as the holders of the Cup will command respect where- ever they go, and there is no doubt that their prowess last season has enabled them to secure such an attrac- tive list of engagements under the new. rules.</p>
        <p>Very fortunately, Warrington's three opening matches are all at Wilderspoia and should give the club a good send-off. To-day the visitors will be Leigh, and though the locals may not repeat their sensationalllG points to 2 points victory of a year ago they should have little difficulty in opening with a substantial win. Neither side has altered much in perso,nel during the year, and with both teams at full strength the balance of power should always rest with Warrington. It must not be forgotten, however, that the Leigh men snatched a lucky win at Wildes- pool in the return game Last December and will doubtless strive hard to repeat the performance. With the memory of that galling experience fresh in their minds, however, I have no fear of Warrington settling the account handsomely.</p>
        <p>1 There is one much-to-be-regretted factor in Warrington's opening essay. Fish will very probably be an absentee. The " irrepressible " has signed articles to run a race with an old foe, " Bucky "Green, of Wigan, on September 23rd. and is assiduously training for the event. This does not debar the crack threequarter from playing football, but he is fearful of injury, and from all accounts he intends to let the game slide. It is possible that he may be absent for the same reason from the first four matches, but there is a chance that wiser counsels and a sense of loyalty to the Club may result in Fish re-considering his position. I have not the slightest doubt that he will forfeit much of his popularity if he fails to assist Wallington in the very important engagements with Leigh, Oldham, Brough- ton Rangers, and Wigan. A good start is half the battle, and Warrington will require their full strength to extract points from all these clubs.</p>
        <p>The Warrington team against Leigh has been chosen as follows : Hallam, full back; Fish or Kenyon, Isherwood, '",Dickenson, and Harris, three-quarters ; Brookes and Flockeahall, half backs ; Boardman, Preston, Shugars, Thomas, Belton, Jolley, Swift, and Naylor, forwards.</p>
        <p>Leigh will be represented by: Clark-son ; Johnson (captain), Bennett, Threlfall, Neville; Burgess, Whittaker; Berry, Blackburn, Lee, O'Neil, Roberts, Silcock,Webster, and W.Wiastanley. Reserves,Roscoe, back ; Buckley, forward.'</p>
        <p>There is ample evidence in the recent practices of the Warrington men being in good form. Quite a feature of the trial games has been the rejuvenation of Isherwood. Last season he appeared to have lost pace and dash, but in the preliminaries so far he has shown a vigour and smartness equal to his best form. Notwithstanding his ten or eleven year's service with Warrington, the popular " Dan " is a good man yet and can give most centres a wrinkle or two. That he will once more keep Warrington's left wing up to concert pit &amp; is the fervent hops of all enthusiasts. Harris was also under a cloud last season, probably due to his injury, and is now giving evidences of a pleasing return to form. Hockenhall has quite re- covered, and along with the energetic untiring Brooks he should make Warrington's half back position a con- stant menace to the opposition. With steady Jack Hallam behind, and a. powerful pack before, the War- rington team ought to go a long way towards honours in the campaign just opening.</p>
        <p>The "A " team visit Leigh in a friendly fixture, the Mather-lane juniors not being entered for the Combination this season. Warrington are therefore taking the opprtunity to give s-nne of the new hands a trial run. Thirteen aside will be played and the selection is as follows: Knight, full back; Short, Jordan, Marshman, (Salford AdelphD and Bibby, (Flixton) threequarters ; Lloyd (local) and Garside AdelphD halfbacks; Forster, Harmer, Coleman, Ernest (Salford) Lunt, Ashton, and Cook forwards. --- Jack Davies, the reserve half back, has not been able to turn out yet owing to a fortnight's suspension which was imposed on him at the end of last season for a little affray at St. Helens.</p>
        <p>Association Football. The matches to-day in the Warrington and District League are :</p>
        <p>_ FIRST DIVISION. Cu:l2er v. Vulcan, referee T. P. Forbes.</p>
        <p>SECOND DIVISION. Vulcan Res. v.Castner Kellner Res., referee,J.Mather.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Cricket.</head>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>To-day cricket gives place to football locally and although ‘Varrington have still a match to play all interest is now centered in the winter sport. Warring- ton finished their League career a fortnight ago, so that last Saturday's engagement was a friendly match with Bolton. As a result of the heavy rains there was a slow wicket, and play never became exciting. Neither side could claim a victory, play ending in a fairly even draw. While Bolton declared aftercompiling 121 for the loss of seven wickets. Warrington were 5S for four when the time limit was reached.</p>
        <p>The Bolton eleven included Albert Ward, the well- known Landashire county player,and it was as a batsman that he did very good service during the afternoon. His innings had realised 31 when J. W. Bithy bowled him. The best batting display, however, was that of Silcock who carried out his bat with -10 to his credit. The same player also proved an effective bowler,captur- ingthree of the warrington wickets for 10 runs.</p>
        <p>There were no brilliant performances on the Warring- ton side. the highest scorer being P. B. Iliwtin who knocked up IS, while Mason contribtr ed 17. The batting averaps this week are as follows : Times Most not in</p>
        <p>Inns, out, inns, runs. average. E. A. Connor Mason P. B. [Bustin A. Thewlis W. Richardson l6 1 41 218 ... 11'53 J. NV. Bibby J. Thompson</p>
        <p>The positions of clubs in the West Lancashire League are as follows : FIRST DIVISTON.</p>
        <p>_ P. W. L. D. P. Earlestown Newton ll 6 ... 2 ... 3 ... 15 Stanley St. Helens St. Helens Roes ll ... 4 ... 5 ... 2 ... 10 Warrington Prescot</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Bowling.</head>
        <p>Bowling.</p>
        <p>The enter-club fixtures in cannection with the War- rington and District League were concluded on Saturday and the Bulls ffead team are left in the proud position of champions of the League. From an early period in the season's history their claim to that honour has never been seriously contested. Throughout, their I doings have been accompanied by a run of success which at one time threatened to eclipse the splendid record established by tha Cemetery club last year. In this however they did not succeed and the achievements of the two clubs can be bracketed equal.</p>
        <p>It was in the second match of the season that the Church-street men came out in their true colours and amblished themselves in a strong position by defeat- ing the ex-champions on the Cemetery green,a perform- ance which none of the other clubs could hope to equal, and did not equal. Seven victories in succession fell to the champions and their advent of the Sankey green on Saturday was watched with great interest. Their chances of victory were by no means slight and success meant the creating of a record which in all probability could not be improved upon by a ',mai club.</p>
        <p>The home club however rocagnized that defeat would to them be particularly disastrous a.s in the event of their not emerging triumphant they would have been called upon to vacate the second position in the League table which they have worked hard to occupy. The Sankeyites therefore faced the fixture with determination and rose to the occasion with such good effect, that they secured the verdict by a margin of 33 points.</p>
        <p>Fearnhead at one thus threatened to contest ths second position with Sankey, but the runners-up assured success by taking three points out of the Horse and Jockey team, in doing so recording the first draw in the history of the League. Fearnhead have to be content with the third place with a lead of two clear points from the ex-champions. The hidifferent display given by the Cemetery men has throughout the season aroused no little comment, but, apparently, nothing which has been said or done has aroused them to an appreciation of the true position of affairs—at least no marked improvement in play has at any period been noticeable. Their record is not one worthy of the traditions of the club and their fall from the first to the fourth place has come as a surprise.</p>
        <p>Considered broadly, the Horse and Jockey team have had a fairly sucoessful season, and but for an unfortu- nate slip in their last fixture their. position might have been a more favourable one. Prior to the concluding match they had done fairly well, having won four out of nine matches played and being then bracketed equal with the Cemetery Club. Penlceth Park's record is as unique as it is unenviable. To them the season has been a particularly disastrous one, every match having ended in favour of their opponents. Prior to Saturday's engagement ' it was hoped that they would snatch a single victory but they were not fated to succeed even to that extent and for the fifth time this season were defeated on their own green, the Cemetery players being the ones to fill their cup of bitterness to over- flowing. It was evident from the first that the Parkites were no match for the other league combinations and they are deserving of every credit for the sportsman- like way in which they have carried out their engage- ments and turned up smiling in face of the tide of ill- luck which had set in against them.</p>
        <p>The final positions of the clubs are : P. W. L. D. For. Agst. Pts. Bolls Head 10.. 8 2 . 0 .16(17 ...1221 ... 16 Sankey 10... 6 ... 3 ... 1 ...1577 _1314 ... 13 Fearnhead lO.. 6 4 . 0 .1368 ...1469 ... 12 Cemetery lO.. 5 5 . 0 .1436 ...1392 ... 10 Horse&amp;Jockey 10... 4 ... 5 1 _1132 _1441 ... 9 Penketh 10... 0 ... 10 ... 0 ...1203 ...1668 ... 0</p>
        <p>Turning to the individual averages A. Shaw of the Bull's Head team heads the list and his record is indeed an excellent one. He has assisted the champions in each of their engagements and is the only unbeaten player connected with the League—that is amongst those who have taken part in a reasonable number of matches. It is singular that the second position in the list should be occupied by a representative of the runners-up in the person of G. Rutter who has placed nine wins to his credit. Players whose records are worthy of note are : Pts. _ _ _</p>
        <p>P. W. L. For: -Agst. A. Shaw. Bill's Head .. lO ... 10 ... 0 ... 150 ... 60 G. Rutter, Sankey T. Ellison, Cemetery J W. Jolley.Bull's Head 10 ... 8 ... 2 ... 136 ... 99 W. Ellison, Sankey lO . 8 2 . 137 ... 101 Joseph Weston,Fearnbead 10 ... 8 ... 2 ... 137 ... 101 W. Andrews, Fearnhead 10 ... 8 ... 2 ... 137 ... 101 H. Jackson, Horse and J. 9 ... 8 ... 1 ... 138 ... 113 F. Bent, Bull's Head 9 7 2 130 ... 77 A. Gleave Bull's Head G. Ball, Cemetery H. Houghton, Sankey 9 . 2 . 118 ... 88 J. Goulden, Sankey lO 7 3 106 ... 73 J. Swanton.Ball's Head lO ... 7 ... 3 ... 141 ... 114 H. Clare, Bull's Head lO . 7 3. . 137 ... 111</p>
        <p>The individual records of the players connected with the clubs which on Saturday concluded their season are given below : Pts.</p>
