      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Cycling</head>
        <p>Cycling</p>
        <p>The break in the weather has greatly militated against open-air enjoyment and in consequence of the unpromis- ing outlook only six members of the Warrington Cycling Club turned out to Hunts Cross. Those who did make the journey greatly enjoyed the outing. Tuesday night's run was cancelled on account of the rain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Angling.</head>
        <p>Angling.</p>
        <p>An open competition which was fished at Agden Bridge on Saturday resulted in excellent sport. Sixty- seven fishermen turned out and carried on operations for two hours, the prize wieners being :—E. Gleave, 13oz. 15drs.; F. Jolley, 10oz. I3drs.; T.Buckley, Soz.ldr.; J. Unsworth. 6oz. 15drs.; R.Pooke, 6oz. 9drs.; H. Plews, soz. lldrs.; and H. Brogden, 50z. 4drs. The arrange- ments for the match were excellent and were made by Messrs. W. Tetlow, P. E. Tetlo_w, A. Pettit, and F. J. Emson.</p>
        <p>The members of the Latchford Angling Association fished a match at Vale Royal on baturday. The party proceeded to the scene of operations by road, the drive being greatly enjoyed. Sport was excellent and after two hour's angling the prize-winners were declared as follows:—H. Green,lb. 6oz. sdrs.;T. Wright, lb.Goz.;H. Spence,lb, 2oz. 6cl.;W.Darbyshire,lb.loz. ldr.;B.Bowden, T 5 ozs. 4drs. ; J. J. Barber, 150zs. ldr.; J. Clarke, 14ozs. 2drs. ; T, Chorley, 12ozs. 10drs. ; G. Taylor, %V. Woods, and T. Boardman, llozs. 9drs. each, and H. Craven, lOozs. 15drs. T. Chorley took the prize offered for the latgest fish caught with a roach weighing 4 ounces.</p>
        <p>An open match will be fished in the Bridgewater Canal at Lymm on September 23rd. The annual contest in aid of the District Nursing Association will be held to-day at London Bridge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>Swimming.</head>
        <p>Swimming.</p>
        <p>Warrington Water Polo team came a cropper on Tuesday evening when they met the champions of the Liverpool and District League. Such experiences are however not unusual and the result was expected. The second team fared a little better against Birkenhead in that the extent of the defeat was not so great,. In fact they gave a very poor account of themselves and at one time even looked like winning.</p>
        <p>The works squadron races for the Gilbert Greenall Challenge Shield were resumed on Tuesday when Cor- poration clerks were defeated by Rylands' representa- tives. Pearson and Knowles should have met Crosfields but were unable to raise a team. The soapmen were entitled to claim the race but displayed a truly sport. manlike spirit in agreeing to its adjournment to a future date.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>RUSSO-JAPANESE WAR.</head>
        <p>RUSSO-JAPANESE WAR.</p>
        <p>PEACE ARRANGED-WHAT RUSSIA CONCEDES.</p>
        <p>NO INDEMNITY.</p>
        <p>The greatest and most destructive war since the great Napoleonic struggle has been brought to a close. On Tuesday peace between Russia and Japan was definitely arrived at between the envoys of the two Powers sitting at Portsmouth, New Hampshire. This securing of the much-desired en-1 after so many weeks of fruitless negotiation confirms the view, that, however discourag- ing the outlook might be, the delegates would not face the condemnation of the civilised world, which would inevitably fall upon them if they separated without arriving at the basis of a treaty, and allowed the re- sumption of hostilities in Manchuria. Yet repeatedly it looked as if the points on which hopeless divergence existed between M. Witte, the Russian Plenipotentiary, and Baron Komura, the Japanese Envoy, could never be satisfactorily adjusted. It was on these occasions that President Roosevelt—through whose instrumentality negotiations between the hostile Powers were first opened—exerted himself to prevent the failure of the conferences.</p>
        <p>Though the terms have not been officially announced, the effect of thorn is as follows :</p>
        <p>Sakhalin to be divided ; Russia to buy back half at a price to be agreed upon by arbitration ; but not to pay any indemnity. Russia to pay £15,000,000 for maintenance of 80,000 prisoners in Japan. Cession of Russian leases in the Liao-tung penin- sula.</p>
        <p>Evacuation of the entire province of Manchuria. Retrocession of China of any Russian privileges in Manchuria, and Russia's recognition of the open door. Cession to Japan of the Chinese Eastern Railway below Harbin (the main line through Northern Man- churia to Vladivostok Russian). Recognition of a Japinesl protectorate over Korea. Grants of fishing rights to Japan in the waters of the Siberian littoral northwards of Vladivostok to Behring Sea. Japan abandons her claim to Russia's interned war- ships, No limitation of Russian naval strength in the Far East. The peace treaty is being drafted for signature, and an armistice is to be arranged immediately.</p>
        <p>THE WAR IN FIGURES. MONEY COST, £340,000,000; LOSSES IN BATTLE, $49,000.</p>
        <p>, It is to-day 5G7 days since war broke out in the Far East with Togo's dramatic night attack on the Russian fleet at Port Arthur.</p>
        <p>How enormous has been the waste of money and life face that time will be seen from the following table : Total dead and wounded to date: Russian</p>
        <p>Japanese</p>
        <p>Total 319,000 Russian forces in field Japanese forces in field</p>
        <p>Total, both sides Cost to Russia £200,000,000 Cost to Japan</p>
        <p>Total Russian ships lost OS Japanese ships lost Total 92 A RECORD OF VICTORY. 1904. Feb. 8-9—T"go attacks Port Arthur. April 13—Russian battleship Petropavlovsk sunk in a Sortie front Port Arthur. Admiral Makaroff</p>
        <p>drowned. May I—Battle of the Yalu. Russians defeated. Troops engaged, 71.500. Losses: Russians, 2,400; Japanese, 1,100. May 22-26—Battle of Nanshan Hill, first of the Port Arthur defences. Russians defeated. Troops engaged, 140,000. Russian losses, 1,700; Japanese losses, 3,500. May 30—Japanese occupy Dalny. June 14—Battle of Watangkao. Russians defeated. Troops engaged, 120,000. Russian losses, 4,700; Japanese losses,l,2oo. July 30-31—Battle of Haicheng. Russians abandoned the position on August 2. Aug. 11—Katninittra defeats Vladivostok squadron. Aug. 26-Sept. 4—Battle of Liaoyang. Russians defeated Total Tomes engaged, 420,000. Russian casualities, 36,000 ; Japanese casualities, 17,600. Oct. 11-12—Battle of the She River. Russians defeated. Forces engaged, 355,000. Russian losses, 60,000 ; Japanese losses, 15,000. Nov. 29-30—Battle of 203-Metre Hill, the bloodiest engagement around Port Arthur. Russians defeated With loss of 3,000 ; Japanese lost 15,000. 1905. Jun. 2—Port Arthur surrenders, having been isolated since May 14—a siege. of. 232 days. The garrison -- • .</p>
        <p>underStOessel cormirised 44,99 Men with 7-£3O</p>
        <p>guns Attacking force 88,000 men with 600 guns. From August 19th, there were thirty-one distinct attacks and three sorties from the city. Russian losses, 10,000; Japanese, 50,000. Russians surrendered 24,000 men aud vast stores of ammunition. The capture cost Japan £20,000,000, and Runia £10,000,000. Jan. 25-29—Battle of the Hun River. Russians routed. Troops engaged, 150,009„ Russian loss, 12,000 ; Japanese loss, 5,000 Feb. 24-March 12—Battle of Mukden. Greatest tight of the war. Battle front was one hundred miles long. Russians defeated. Forces engaged, 800.000 men. Russian losses. 60,000 ; -Vanes') losses. 41,000. Japanese took 10,000 prisoners, and enor- mous quantities of stores. May 27-23—Naval battle of the Sea of Japan. Rozhest- vensky's fleet annihilated by Togo. The Russians lost six battleships, five cruisers sunk ; and two battleships, two coast defence vessels, and one destroyer were captured. The Russians lost 8,550 men killed or drowned, and 3,000 were captured. Japanese lost less than 600 men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1905-09-02_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>EMMANUEL CHURCH. OPENING OF NEW SUNDAY SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>EMMANUEL CHURCH. OPENING OF NEW SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The establishment of the London and North-Western Railway district offices at Arpley Station has necessit- ated the removal of the Emmanuel Sunday school to another building and the members of the Church have succeeded in acquiring freehold property in Knutsford road which will be utilized as a Church Institute.ll The new premises were opened after re-decora- tion and alteration on Monday. In recognition of the event special services were on Sunday, held in the church in Bold-street, the preacher being the Right Rev. Bishop Eldridge, D.D.„ the Presiding Bishop of the Church. In the morning he preached on "The Brotherhood of Christ," in the afternoon o "The work of the Holy Spirit," and in the evening on "The character of the Christian minister." The ser- vices were very impressive, and there were large con- gregations. In the evening there were over a hundred communicants at the re-union celebration, Special music was rendered by the choir. OPENING MEETING.</p>
        <p>The first public meeting in the newly acquired build- ing was held on Monday evening. The proceedings commenced with a congregational tea at which a large number sat down. For the subsequent meeting the schoolroom was crowded. The Rev. F. Vaughan pre- sided and was supported amongst others by Bishop I Eldridge, presiding bishop of the Reformed Episcopal Church, and Mr. C. E. Parker, president of the Warrington Free Church Council. The Rev. John Yonge, who had been announced to address the meeting, wrote regretting his inability to be present. The Rev. F. VAUGHAN in the opening address ex- pressed his thanks to the members of the congregation for their support. Their work had been carried on in Warrington for 'twenty-six years with varying fortunes and experiences yet while there had been a history of change there had also been a history of spiritual progre3s. The work of their Church was not so much destructive as constructive.l They declared their principles whenever they felt it was their duty to do so and they carried on their work and intended to do so by the quiet, persistent declaration of those truths which were the centre-point of the great Protestant Reformation and which gave them the liberties which they enjoyed at the present time. Coupled with that declaration and expression of those principles they desired and endeavoured to use out their creed in their daily lives by endeavouring to carry out the work in power of the Holy Ghost. They fought not against men, but against systems—systems which tended so much to the evil element and which cramped the development of the spiritual life and hindered the progress of those truths which empha- sized the glory of the salvation in Jesus Christ. (Applause.) In the negotiations for the purchase of that building, they had been met very fairly by the owners, and the final price had been reduced to £1,200. To meet that sum £BOO had been raised by means of a mortgage on the property, and £5OO had been borrowed. The mem- bers of the congregation and the friends of the Church had gathered together and raised £125 in a vervlshort space of time and they bad thereby been enabled to complete the purchase of the property. They were especially pleased with their success as it was felt that if they acquired a freehold site in a densely-populated district such as that was they might be liable to advance in the great task which the Church had undertaken. After completing the purchase of the premises and making necessary alterations and improve- ments they had £7O in hand. Mr. Vaughan proceeded to thank t'e churchwarden, Mr. Allen, for his very able assistance during the negotiations. In addition to the schoolroom there was a room on the ground floor where workingmen would find a place for quiet recreation on Christian lines every day of the week. The Sunday school would be conducted in that building morning and evening and in the morning there would also be a children's service. In addition to the club for young men they are desirous, later on, of making some similar provision for the young ladies. They felt confident that so long as they were true to the great principles which called their Church into existence so long would the blessing of God rest upon them, and so long could they confidently appeal to Christian friends to assist them. (Applause). Bishop ELDRIDGE offered his very warm congratula- tions upon the work which was being done in Warring- ton. It was a singular fact that it was from the very building in which the schools had been established that the organizers of the Emmanuel Church in Warrington left in consequence of the ritualistic practices,and of the sacerdotal spirit which had for so long been making mischief in the land, and working deadly evil in the English Church. They must always keep in mind the purpose for which their Church existed—for the main- tenance of Protestant principles. They must keep those principles ever to the front, and show people that they , held fast to the ideals of the Reformation, for which their fathers died, and which were certainly worth preserving at the present time. They must by personal piety proselytising, publishing and persuading uphold I their principles against the sacerdotal teachings and practises of modern times. (Applause.) Mr. C. E. PARKER said that the Free Churches in Warrington may be divided upon many things as to forms and creeds, but they were united upon things which were vastly more important. They had their different creeds but they all had the truth that God was Love and Christ was King. (Apulause.) Let them be true to their ideals as it was necessary they should ideals with regard to creeds and beliefs as action was based upon them. In conclusion Mr. Parker congratu- lated them upon the success which had attended their endeavours. _</p>
        <p>The Rev. JOTIN VAUCII IN of Blackpool said he was thankful for the formation of a National Free Church Council. Inhis younger days a different state of things existed, especially among the Nonconformist Churches. There used to be foolish prejudices, and frequent bicker- ings but he was thankful that these were now all swept away. (Applause.) The walls which at one time stood solid between the different denominations had been thrown down and they now shook bands over the ruins. He trusted every blessing would rest upon the congre- gation of Emmanuel Churoh and that their efforts would be crowned with great success. During the evening songs were contributed by Miss Campion, of Stockport, and selections by an orchestra organized by Miss Thornton. the artistes being heartily applauded for their efforts. TUESDAY NIGHT'S PROCEEDINGS. RITUALISM CONDEMNED.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night a conversatzione was held under the auspices of the Mon's Union and Young Women's Bible Class, the Rev. F. Vaughan presiding over a large attendance. _</p>
        <p>The Rev. Dr. BOWMAN spoke upon " The Reformed Episcopal Church : why it exists." He said that when they looked over England, and saw in nearly every parish the signs and symbols of Romanism, what were they to conclude ?—that there was beneath all these symbols and ceremonialism a doctrine and a teaching which was utterly opposed to the teaching of the New Testament. Their common Protestantism was in danger, and the evils to which he had alluded were undermining their evangelical religion. The Reformed Episcopal Church existed to develop the power and spirit of that religion. They had no quarrel with Romanists ; only with their system, but they especially opposed it when it was being developed in the Church of England. (Applause.) The Rev. Dr. NATSII delivered an address upon" The Reformed Episcopal Church, its methods and needs." Their methods were, he said, evangelical. They were as soundly Protestant as it was possible for any church to be, and there never was a time when there was a greater need for sound Protestantism than to- day. (Applause.) They needed downright courage, never going about cap in hand apologizing for their existence. They needed more brotherliness,more union. and he hoped to live to see the time when there would be a union between the Reformed Episcopal Church, the Free Churches of England and the Countess of Hunting- don's Church, and if that could be accomplished they would form the strongest possible church in the whole of England. (Applause.) During th 3 proceedings fruit was distributed and pianoforte solos were given by Mrs. Landon. Musical items were also contributed by the choir. WEDNESDAY NIGHT'S MEETING. On Wednesday night the Rev. J. S. Hughes presided over a crowded gathering. He believed, he said in a brief introductory speech, that the first Christian Endeavour Society in Warrington was stated at Emmanuel Church. That society had flourished, and had rendered splendid service. Mr. Hughes commented upon the suitability of the now schools which he hoped would be of great advantage to the church. (Applause). The Rev. F. A. REWi, secretary of the Lancashire and Cheshire Federation Christian Endeavour Society to the church spoke of the great influence which Christian Endeavour Societies had on the welfare of a church, emphasising the need for the existence of such societies for furthering the work of the Lord Jesus Christ. Councillor BLAcx, Liverpool, spoke upon "The C.E. : Its objects and opportunities." The objects of the Society were worthy of their greatest support. The Endeavour was like a secondary school to the Christian Church, where the art and science of the Christian life were practised, just as in the Sunday school the elemen- tary lessons were given and the foundations of Christian truth taught. The opportunities of the Society were without limit. The Christian Endeavour movement had its widest opportunities in the mission fields and one of the largest and the most vital duties of the nvement was to keep alive the missionary spirit and to give practical and active support to mission work. (Applause) During the evening the Rev. F. Vaughan contributed a sacred solo._ _</p>
        <p>SCHOLARS ENTERTAINED. On Thursday evening the Sunday scholars were enter- tained to tea. The subsequent proceedings were pre- sided over by the Rev. .1. F. Matthews. The principal feature of the proceedings was the presentation to Mrs. Norman, of money collected by the children on behalf of the new schools, a sum of over .£5 being handed in. Addresses were given by Mrs. Norman, Mrs. A. Landon, and Mrs. Simpson. Medals struck in commemoration of the event were distributed to the children. Dorothy Atherton, a little girl who collected the highest amount (us. 3d.) was presented with a prayer book. During the evening gramaphone seleetions were given.</p>
      </div>
