        <p>MY CHURCH. (Since the Education Act., My Church is on the mountain side Besides the flowers of spring. Where joyful birds through all the vale Their sweetest matins sing. Sometimes upon the rugged cliffs So near the sounding sea, The sermon of the breaking waves May be the best for me. And in the shady peaceful lanes A sermon we may find, In summer when the golden corn Is dancing to the wind. In temples old and beautiful I cannot seek for rest. The thoughts alas would come to me That ramble in my breast. It is not now my Fathers Church Where once I loved to go, The love of sacred liberty With me has made it so. Ah ! yes the open face of day Unselfish pure and free, Since banished from my Father's Church, May be the best for me. S. KENYON. August 29th, 1905.</p>
