        <p>THE CHARGES AGAINST COUNCILLORS. Prosecution to be Undertaken At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee on Monday the report of the Sub-Committee appointed to investigate and report on the charges of corrupt practices against two members of the Council was read. It was resolved, on the racommendation of the Sub- Committee,that the Town Clerk be directed to apply to the Attorney-General for leave to institute proceedings against the two Councillors involved under the Public Bodies Corrupt Practices Act, 1889. BOROUGH AUDITORS' REPORT. The Waterworks Contract. • The Warrington Borough Auditors have just presented their annual report as follows : Warrington, August, 1905. To the Mayor, Aldermen, and burgesses of the County Borough of Warrington. BOROUGH AUDIT. Dear Sir,—We have audited the accounts of all the departments of the Corporation for the year ended 31st March, 1905, and except in the following particular certify them to be correct. In consequence of the refusal of the Town Clerk to produce certain documents in connection with the waterworks extensions, we did not feel justified in signing the accounts and they were published without out signatures. Since the publication of the accounts we have seen the documents referred to, and it appears that the contractor claims a considerable sum in addi- tion to what is already included in the accounts for the year under review. The securities for Corporation investments and deeds relating to property have been inspected and we have found them in order. Yours faithfully, JAMES FAIRCLOUGEL Mayor's Auditor EDWIN BRADSHAW, A.C.A. Elective LAURENCE BOLTON. 5 Auditors.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON EDUCATION COMMITTEE. The fickle nature of the climate of this country often gives rise to a variety of ailments and complaints, which I assumM,g at first the form of only a slight indisposition, if neglected and unheeded may become the germ of THE SENIOR ASSISTANT MISTRESS • erions diseases, which will undermine the constitution, ,nd finally result in a long and lingering illness, and FOR THE SECONDARY SCHOOL. erhaps terminate fatally. The variable temperature and the changeable weather of the last month or so, makes it incumbent on all of us to exercise wise and Appointment Again Discussed. prudent precautions to counteract the evil effects which the weather may have had upon our health and comfort. --- Already we frequently hear such complaints as "No appetite," "These frequent headaches," "This languid I The monthly meeting of the Warrington Education feeling," and dozens Of other expressions which all point Committee was held at the Town Hall on Monday even- to the urgent need of a good Tonic. Now there are ing, Alderman Bennett presidirik. There were also several tonic mixtures offered to the Public, but none present :—The Mayor, Alderman Smethurst, Councillors which have been so uniformly successful as , Burton, Barton, Stough, Ellison, Evans, Lord, Sankey, Evans' Quinine Bitters, The Vegetable Tonic. This . Fulton Smith, Wright, and Messrs. W. Bibby, J. D. Preparation is acknowledged to be The Best Remedy of• Buckton, A. J. Jolley, C. E. Parker, E. Plinston, C. H. The Age for Nervousness, Weakness. Chest Affections, Unsworth, and G. Walker, with Ma J. Moore Murray Palpitation of the Heart, Indigestion, Liver Complaint, (director). and Influenza. BOARD OF EDUCATION GRANTS. The numerous cases of marvellous cures effected, and the restoration to perfect health, of those who had HIE COST OF SCHOOL SITES. long suffered and had vainly tried other remedies, can In moving the adoption of the minutes of the Finance be accounted for by simply stat