ices, direct from the Sole SECONDARY SCHOOL. Proprietors :—Quinine Bitters atinatatcturing Corn- LIVELY DISCUSSION. pany, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. Beware of At a meeting of the School Management Committee Imitations. See that you get the genuine Preparation it was reported that the resolution of the Committee with the name," Gwilym Evans " on Label, Stamp, and at their last meeting with reference to the aprrointment Bottle, without which none is genuine, of the senior assistant mistress at the Secondary school was not approved and adopted by the Education Com- mittee at their meeting on the 17th July. After careful consideration, it was resolved that Miss Elizabeth Frost he appointed senior assistant mistress at the Secondary school, at a cornmenaing salary in accordance with the scale £125 per annum, as from the Ist August 1905. The committee also decided that authority be given for the return of the fees of two of the scholars at the Secondary school who were awarded free student- ships at the school for one year and in respect of whom satisfactory reports have been received from the Head- master, and for scholarships of £5 each,with free tuition and the necessary books to be awarded to them for a further two years. On the recommendation of the Headmaster of the Secondary school, it was resolved that free tuition (including the provision of the neces- sary books) be granted to four children who are at present attending the school. It was resolved that arrangements be made for the holding of the annual prize distribution in connection with the Secondary school and Technical Institute in the Parr Hall, and that Mr. Alfred W. W. Dale, M.A., Vice-Chancellor of the Victoria University, Liverpool, be invited to distribute the prizes. A letter was submitted from the Law Clerk to the Governors of the Grammar school, asking the com- mittee to receive a deputation from the Governors to discuss the question of the making of an interim grant by the committee to the school, pending the receipt of the new amending scheme for the government of the school, and the Director was requested to arrange the date for the meeting with the Law Clerk to the Governors. Councillor FULTON SMITH moved the confirmation of the minutes and said the deputation from the Grammar School would meet the committee in due course. With regard to the prize distribution, last year they were honoured with a visit from Dr. Hopkinson, Vice Chan- cellor of the Manchester University, and on this occasion it was thought fitting to invite Vice-Chan- cellor Dale, of the Liverpool University, to perform the ceremony.' Mr. J. D. BUCKTON selonied. Councillor WRIGHT asked if the sub-committee appointed to meet the Gymnasium Committee with reference to the question of physical instruction had held the interview and if so what was the decision arrived at. The CHAIRMAN said the meeting had not yet been held. Mr. PLLNSTON moved an amendment that the minute relating to the appointment of Mies Frost as senior assistant mistress at the Secondary School be not con- firmed. He said he much regretted that opportunity had not been given to discuss the matter at the meeting of the General Purposes Committee instead of at that public meeting. He certainly thought the mover of the minute wonld have thought the matter of sufficient importance—in view of the fact that it had previously been referred back—to specify some reason why the re- commendation again appeared in the same form. He believed that in biainess affairs as well as in town's matters it was usual when a junior was placed over the heads of two seniors to give reasons for the step, but in the present arse no reason was advanced as to why two senior teachers were to be misplaced. One of the other teachers he believed, was not only senior in years to Miss Frost but was always senior in respect of the time she had served the committee and the previous authority. With reference to the pupil teachers' centre, her qualifi- cations were of the highest. They had it on the authority of the respected Chairman of the School Management Committee, under whom Miss Warburton was trained, that she was a fully collided teacher when she left the college. They had Rae's° on the authority of Mr. Dennison that her teaching capacity was great and that during a portion of the time she served under him she had charge of the pupil teachers. They knew the results were very satisfactory to the town— he was sorry to say more satisfactory than they were under the present Education Anthorityat the Secondary School. They had it on the authority of the late prin- cipal of the school that Miss Warburton was one of the best teachers he had come into contact with. Mr. Plinston concluded by remarking that when two ladies who were thoroughly qualified and senior in years were overstepped by another teacher the committee ought to have the reasons stated. Councillor LORD seconded. Miss Warburton, he con- tended, had every right to the position of senior mistress. He believed her testimonials were of the highest and why she was being passed over he could not for the life of him understand. Councillor EVANS also pressed fot full reasons to be stated. Enough was said at the last meeting— Mr. JOLLEY : To much. Councillor EVANS : That is your opinion. Too much can be said for some men. Continuing, he seta that if certain persons were making up their minds as to who were eligibl 3 for positions and preferring juniors to I seniors,they were taking responsibilities upon themselves which belonged to the Education Committee. He I resented any such action being taken. He took excep- tion to the resolution because of the scant information I which those who were arranging these matters were dis- posed to give. He could not conceive any ground for with-holding the reasons. Mr. JOLLEY said that as he was acting as Chairman of Committee in the absence of Canon Morley Stevenson he might at that stage make a few remarks which, he hoped, would clear the air a little. The mover of the amendment had made incorrect statements and Coon- , cellor Evans, aisuming that they were correct, had ' repeated them. Councillor Evass : There is no other alternative but to make the statements, simply because you don't ' give us the information. It is very unfair for Mr. Jolley, who is in possession of the information, to say that we have made false statements. The CHAIRMAN : You are verging on a point of order, Mr. Evans. Coencillor EVANS : T think I am. (Laughter.) He is accusing me of making false statements. We made the truest statements we could with the information we had got. Mr. JOLLEY : I should have hoped Councillor Evans knew me sufficiently well not to suspect me of any desire to evade a direct issue, and I should also have hoped he knew me sufficiently well to know that I should never suggest that he made a false statement. I said and I repeat that Councillor Evans has repeated inaccurate and incorrect statements, and I said I presumed he was relying on Mr. Plinston's statements. Continuing, Mr. Jolley said it was not correct for Mr. Plinston to state that the recommendation was seat back by the School Management Committee in the same form in which it originally appeared. U he took the trouble to look at the minutes of the meeting, he would see that the statement was absolutely incorrect and unjustifiable. The Council at the meeting last month approved the principle which lay behind the minute and sanctioned the scale of salaries. In the full dis- cussion which took place on the general principle, he taok it for granted that the members of the Education Committee accepted the desirability of having an office of that kind and the Council, at any rate, accepted the principle and passed the scale of salaries. Nothing therefore remained for the School Management Com- mittee to do but to apnoint an officer to fill the position sanctioned by the Council. The committee, he con- tended, acted without prejudice and with an absolute desire to be scrupulously fair to all the persons whose names had been mentioned, and it was very regrettable that so many personalities had been intrnduced in the matter. The one desire of the committee was to secure the best person for the very important office. They did not want to go outside the existing staff if they could secure a competent person without doing so. Mr. Plinston repeated two or three times that a junior had been promoted over two seniors. but that was not correct. So far as length of service under the cora- -1 mittee was concerned, the three ladies mentioned were</p>
